; ======================================================================
; xHCI (USB 3.0) HOST CONTROLLER for the Raspberry Pi 4's VL805 -
; library, not a compiler builtin.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Polled. No interrupts and no DMA engine of our own. What it does is a
; USB host stack: find the controller behind the PCIe root complex, read
; its capability registers, halt it, reset it, stand up the DCBAA, the
; command ring and the event ring, run it, reset a root port, address a
; device, drive control transfers on endpoint zero, configure bulk
; endpoints, move bulk data, walk a USB 2.0 HUB and address the devices
; behind it.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THIS BANNER WAS STALE FOR TWO DAYS AND THAT IS WHY IT IS DATED NOW.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Until 2026-08-28 the six lines above said "no hub support, no bulk
; transfers and no mass storage", and a paragraph below said "What has
; still never happened is either file talking to hardware", and a
; paragraph below THAT said none of a list of things "has been observed
; even once". All three were written before 2026-08-26 and all three
; were false by the end of that day: bulk rings landed, usbmsc.pi4 moved
; real sectors through XhciBulkIn/XhciBulkOut, and the monitor's
; mount/load/save worked over them.
;
; A stale banner is worse than no banner. It is READ AND BELIEVED, and
; it was: the day it was corrected, a worker was sent to add "missing"
; bulk transfers that had existed for two days. The three corrections
; are made in place below rather than appended, because an appended
; correction leaves the false sentence still sitting there to be read
; first. Every claim in this header was re-checked against the code on
; 2026-08-28 and against the vault's silicon notes; the three that were
; wrong are marked WAS WRONG with the date, so that the next reader can
; see which sentences have a history.
;
; WHAT IS STILL ABSENT, as of 2026-08-28, and deliberately:
;   * INTERRUPT ENDPOINTS. Everything here is control or bulk. A HID
;     boot keyboard is polled over the control pipe by hid.pi4 instead,
;     and as of 2026-08-28 that is no longer a hope: the measurement
;     that decides it has been taken on this board and GET_REPORT
;     answered a real wireless receiver's keyboard interface in 2 ms.
;     See hid.pi4's header for the numbers.
;   * ISOCHRONOUS endpoints and streams.
;   * Hubs deeper than ONE TIER. The route string is built and honoured,
;     so the arithmetic is there, but nothing walks a hub behind a hub.
;     See XhciHubEnumerate().
;
; WAS WRONG, 2026-08-28: this list used to include "endpoint recovery
; after a stall (Reset Endpoint plus Set TR Dequeue Pointer)" as a
; deliberate omission. It was not survivable as one. A stalled endpoint
; is HALTED by the controller and every later transfer on it - on any
; interface of that device, because endpoint zero is shared - is
; accepted at the doorbell and never run, so it times out instead of
; failing. Both commands are implemented now; see xh_ClearHalt().
;
; USE - this file names no other file (house rule: a library never
; includes a library). The MAIN program lists what it needs, and the
; ORDER MATTERS because this file calls into pcie.pi4:
;
;     XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/uart.pi4"   ; only to PRINT
;     XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/timer.pi4"  ; pcie.pi4 needs delay()
;     XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/pcie.pi4"   ; MUST come before this
;     XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/xhci.pi4"
;
; That exact order builds: timer.pi4 + pcie.pi4 + xhci.pi4 through
; pmfc gives "pmfc: OK" with no duplicate symbol and no undefined
; reference. Leave timer.pi4 out and pcie.pi4's own delay() calls are
; undefined; put xhci.pi4 first and its calls into pcie.pi4 are.
;
;     If PcieInit() = 0
;       PutS(PcieErrorText())
;     ElseIf XhciInit() = 0
;       PutS(XhciErrorText())
;       PutS(" usbsts=") : PutHex8(XhciLastSts())
;       PutS(" usbcmd=") : PutHex8(XhciLastCmd())
;     Else
;       ; capability registers are now readable
;       PutS("ports=") : PutHex2(XhciMaxPorts())
;     EndIf
;
; ======================================================================
; WHAT THIS FILE OWES pcie.pi4, AND NOTHING ELSE
; ======================================================================
; FOUR entry points, and they are not duplicated here. There is no
; config-space access in this file, no ECAM index/data pair, no
; bus-to-CPU arithmetic and no DMA reachability test of its own,
; because all four already exist next door and a second copy of any of
; them would be a second thing to get wrong:
;
;   PcieCfgRead32(bus, dev, fn, off)   config space read
;   PcieCfgWrite32(bus, dev, fn, off, v)
;   PcieCpuFromBus(busAddr)            a BAR holds a PCI-BUS address
;                                      near $F8000000; this returns an
;                                      address THIS CPU can load from
;   PcieDmaOk(addr, len)               is this buffer reachable BY THE
;                                      CONTROLLER? (see THE 3 GiB WALL)
;
; PcieInit() and PcieLinkUp() are NOT called from here. Bringing the
; link up is the caller's job and it has to happen before anything in
; this file will answer; that is one dependency fewer and it keeps the
; failure in the place that can explain it. A config read attempted
; with the link down is documented in U-Boot to abort the CPU -
; v2025.01_pcie_brcmstb.c, brcm_pcie_config_address():
;
;     /* An access to our HW w/o link-up will cause a CPU Abort */
;
; so calling XhciInit() before PcieInit() succeeds is not a soft error,
; it is a hang. Do not do it.
;
; WRITTEN AGAINST THE CONTRACT FIRST. At the hour this file was started
; pcie.pi4 contained PcieCpuFromBus() and PcieDmaOk() but NOT
; PcieCfgRead32()/PcieCfgWrite32() - those were being added in parallel,
; so the two config-space calls here were written to the agreed
; signature and could not be compiled against anything. They CAN be now:
; pcie.pi4 landed PcieInit(), PcieLinkUp(), PcieCfgRead32(),
; PcieCfgWrite32() and PcieErrorText() during the same session, and the
; three-file build above was run and passed.
;
; WAS WRONG, corrected 2026-08-28: this paragraph used to end "What has
; still never happened is either file talking to hardware." Both files
; have talked to hardware since 2026-08-26 - see the silicon section
; below, which replaces the section that used to stand here.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHAT THE SILICON HAS ACTUALLY DONE. Corrected 2026-08-28.
; ======================================================================
; WAS WRONG: this section used to be headed "NOTHING HERE HAS EVER RUN
; ON A VL805" and listed six things that had "not been observed even
; once" - a config read answered, a plausible BAR, CAPLENGTH reading
; back, a device on a root port, a TRB consumed by real hardware, one
; byte of a real device descriptor. Every one of the six happened on
; 2026-08-26. The list is kept, inverted, because what a driver has
; actually seen is the most valuable paragraph in its header and the
; hardest to reconstruct later:
;
;   USBSTS $00000010 after XhciInit - no Host System Error, not halted
;   XhciNoOp succeeded - the controller read our TRB and wrote our event
;   port 1  high speed   $2109:$3431  class 09
;   port 3  super speed  $090C:$1000  class 00
;   the full 18-byte device descriptor off both, and the configuration
;   descriptor off the stick: interface 0, class $08/$06/$50, endpoint
;   $01 OUT bulk and endpoint $82 IN bulk, max packet 1024
;
; $2109 is VIA Labs and $3431 is A HUB - class 09 - and it is soldered
; to this board. THAT IS THE SINGLE MOST IMPORTANT FACT IN THIS FILE
; for anyone attaching a keyboard or a mouse; see THE TOPOLOGY below.
;
; The failure that produced those numbers is worth naming because it
; does not look like what it is: the PCIe inbound window's RC_BAR2
; register holds an OFFSET, not a base, and the firmware had left
; $4_0000_0000 in it, so every DMA address was four gigabytes short and
; USBSTS read $15 - Host System Error - which reads exactly like a
; broken controller.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE TOPOLOGY - a keyboard is NOT on a root port, and that is settled
; ======================================================================
; The vault carried this as a GUESS ("behind a hub, probably") until
; 2026-08-28. It is not a guess. The port scan above is the evidence:
; root port 1 is permanently occupied by a HIGH-SPEED HUB with VIA Labs'
; own vendor id, which is the VL805's integrated USB 2.0 hub. Nothing
; was plugged into it; it is on the die.
;
; The consequence, and it is the whole reason the route-string and TT
; machinery below exists:
;
;   * A SUPERSPEED device in a blue port trains on a ROOT port. That is
;     the boot stick on port 3, and XhciAddressDevice() handles it.
;   * A LOW- or FULL-SPEED device - which is what every USB keyboard and
;     every USB mouse is - CANNOT appear on a root port of this
;     controller. It appears on a downstream port of that internal hub,
;     and addressing it needs three things this file did not have
;     before 2026-08-28: a ROUTE STRING, a TT Hub Slot ID and TT Port
;     Number in the slot context, and the hub's OWN slot context marked
;     as a hub. XhciAddressDeviceAt() and XhciMarkSlotAsHub() are those
;     three things.
;
; A driver that only ever resets root ports will therefore enumerate a
; USB 3 flash drive perfectly and never see a keyboard at all - which is
; a failure that looks like a broken keyboard, and is not.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHAT THE MODEL HAS DONE, which is a different question
; ======================================================================
; What HAS happened against the project's A64 oracle:
;
;   * It compiles.  pmfc <harness>.pi4 -t pi4 --load-addr 0x200000 -s
;   * The emitted A64 was read back. Every MMIO access materialises as
;     "ldr w" / "str w" - THIRTY-TWO BIT - which is the whole reason
;     xh_Rd/xh_Wr exist as one-line procedures instead of inline
;     PeekN/PokeN scattered through the file. On this target .i is 64
;     bits and a bare Peek is EIGHT bytes; an xHCI operational register
;     read eight bytes wide would silently take the next register with
;     it. Specifically, in the emitted listing:
;         xh_Rd    ends "add x11, x12, x13 / ldr w12, [x11]"
;         xh_Wr    ends "and x13, x12, x11 / str w13, [x11]"
;         xh_Rd64  is TWO "ldr w" at +0 and +4, recombined with
;                  "lsl x14, x13, x11" where x11 is 32
;         xh_Wr64  is "str w13, [x11]" at +0 and then "str w12" at +4 -
;                  LOW HALF FIRST, which is the order xhci.h requires
;     and the capability offsets appear as bare 0, 4, 8, 12, 16 in
;     xh_ReadCaps, with 144 and 288 as the $0090/$0120 version bounds.
;
;   * The two cache-maintenance encodings this file's header claims
;     were found in the linked image as words, not read off a table:
;     $D50B7A29 ("dc cvac, x9") once and $D50B7E29 ("dc civac, x9")
;     once, alongside four "dsb sy" / "isb" pairs. The address
;     materialisation ahead of each is "ldr x9", not "ldr w9".
;   * The whole state machine was executed against a MODEL of an xHCI
;     controller written for the project's A64 oracle
;     (tools/a64/a64_xhci_check.py, driving tools/a64/a64_interp.py,
;     with RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/pi4XhciSelfTest.pi4 as the
;     program). The model implements the capability registers, a USBSTS
;     whose CNR bit clears only after several reads, HCRST
;     self-clearing, a command ring that consumes TRBs and posts
;     completion events, root ports that answer a reset, and a device
;     that answers GET_DESCRIPTOR. It also POLLS THE RINGS between
;     instructions, the way a real controller does, rather than only
;     when a doorbell is rung.
;
;     82 assertions made by the program, 24 more made by the model
;     from outside it, and 41 standing refusals the model raises the
;     moment the driver does something the specification forbids -
;     writing an operational register while CNR is set, ringing a
;     doorbell with bus mastering off, handing over an unaligned
;     structure, resetting a port with nothing on it. That is a real
;     test of the ORDER of operations, of the alignment of every
;     structure, and of the cycle-bit arithmetic. It is not a test of
;     the silicon.
;
;     THE MODEL FOUND A BUG IN THIS FILE, which is the only reason to
;     trust it at all. The first version of the control-transfer path
;     wrote the Setup Stage TRB with the correct cycle bit and then
;     XORed it back out one instruction later - reintroducing exactly
;     the race that xhci-ring.c's giveback_first_trb() exists to avoid.
;     The asynchronous poll caught the window. xh_QueueTrbCy() is the
;     fix: the inverted cycle is written in the same store as the rest
;     of the control word.
;
;   * EIGHT DELIBERATE BREAKAGES were injected into a copy of this file
;     and the gate watched go red on every one: skipping the CNR wait,
;     misaligning the DCBAA, dropping the Link TRB's Toggle Cycle bit,
;     never enabling PCI bus mastering, writing a 64-bit register high
;     half first, reading a register with an eight-byte PeekI, leaving
;     TRB_IOC off the Status Stage TRB, and handing the first control
;     TRB over early. A gate that passes either way is worth nothing.
;
;     A NINTH mutation was expected to fail and did NOT - deleting the
;     xh_RingRoom() call. That finding is recorded in xh_RingRoom()'s
;     own comment and in the harness's HARMLESS table rather than being
;     quietly dropped, because a comment claiming a hazard nobody has
;     ever observed is how a superstition becomes documentation.
;
; NONE of the following has been observed even once: a VL805 answering
; a config read, a BAR holding a plausible address, CAPLENGTH reading
; back as anything, a device connecting to a root port, a TRB being
; consumed by real hardware, or one byte of a real device descriptor.
; Treat every confident sentence below as a statement about what the
; SPECIFICATIONS say, not about what this board did.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE SPECIFICATION, AND EVERY NUMBER IN THIS FILE COMES FROM IT
; ======================================================================
; The xHCI register layout is not in any Broadcom document on this
; disk, and the VL805 has no public datasheet. The source used is
; U-Boot 2025.01 - the version actually running on this board - staged
; at RaspberryPi4/Reference/uboot\:
;
;   xhci.h        the register structs and every bit name. THIS IS THE
;                 SPECIFICATION for this file. Every constant below
;                 cites the symbol it came from.
;   xhci.c        xhci_reset(), xhci_start(), xhci_lowlevel_init(),
;                 the root-port feature handling, xhci_address_device()
;   xhci-mem.c    xhci_mem_init(), the ring/ERST/DCBAA layout,
;                 xhci_setup_addressable_virt_dev(), the alignment
;   xhci-ring.c   queue_trb(), inc_enq(), inc_deq(), the cycle-bit
;                 rules, xhci_ctrl_tx()
;   v2025.01_xhci-pci.c    how the controller is found on the bus and
;                          where the operational registers start
;   v2025.01_pcie_brcmstb.c   which bus number the VL805 is on
;
; A number with no citation is exactly what this project refuses, so
; every #XHCI_* constant below names its source symbol. Where a
; constant is arithmetic in the header - "(1 << 4)" - the arithmetic is
; in the comment and the initialiser is the literal, because a constant
; initialiser here takes a BARE LITERAL and "#K = 1 << 4" is refused.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHERE THE REGISTERS ARE - three layers, and all three can be wrong
; ======================================================================
; 1. THE BUS ADDRESS. The VL805 sits on PCIe bus 1. That is not a
;    guess: v2025.01_pcie_brcmstb.c, brcm_pcie_config_address(), says
;
;      /* Busses 0 (host PCIe bridge) and 1 (its immediate child)
;         are limited to a single device each */
;      /* Accesses to the RC go right to the RC registers */
;      if (pci_bus == 0) { *paddress = pcie->base + offset; ... }
;
;    so bus 0 IS the root complex's own registers and the single child
;    behind it is bus 1, device 0, function 0. XhciInit() uses 1/0/0.
;    XhciInitAt() exists for the case where that turns out to be wrong
;    on a particular board or firmware - it is the first thing to try
;    if a config read gives $FFFFFFFF.
;
; 2. THE BAR. v2025.01_xhci-pci.c, xhci_pci_init():
;
;      hccr = dm_pci_map_bar(dev, PCI_BASE_ADDRESS_0, ...)
;      hcor = hccr + HC_LENGTH(xhci_readl(&hccr->cr_capbase))
;      /* enable busmaster */  cmd |= PCI_COMMAND_MASTER
;
;    so BAR0 gives the CAPABILITY register block, the OPERATIONAL block
;    starts CAPLENGTH bytes further on, and BUS MASTER must be enabled
;    or every ring this file builds is written for a controller that is
;    not allowed to read it. xh_Locate() maps BAR0, xh_ReadCaps() reads
;    CAPLENGTH, then xh_EnableDma() sets MASTER at the same boundary as
;    U-Boot's xhci_pci_init(), before the generic halt/reset path.
;
;    BAR0 may be a 64-BIT memory BAR, in which case BAR1 is its high
;    half and is NOT a second BAR. That is architectural PCI, and
;    pcie.pi4 already carries the decoding constants; this file reads
;    both halves and refuses if the high half is non-zero, because the
;    outbound window this root complex maps is 64 MiB at bus
;    $F8000000 and a BAR above 4 GiB is not in it.
;
; 3. THE CPU ADDRESS. The value in the BAR is a PCI-BUS address. The
;    CPU does not reach it at that address. PcieCpuFromBus() performs
;    the translation and REFUSES anything outside the window rather
;    than returning a plausible number - see its comment; the plausible
;    number lands at $F8000000 on the ARM side, which is a real,
;    powered, completely unrelated device.
;
; UNVERIFIABLE FROM THIS DISK, and it is the most likely first failure:
; on a Raspberry Pi 4 the VL805's own firmware is loaded by the VideoCore
; side, and a controller whose firmware has not been loaded does not
; answer. Nothing in the staged U-Boot files performs that load and
; there is no such tag in RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4. If a config
; read of offset $00 returns $FFFFFFFF, or CAPLENGTH reads back 0 or
; $FF, that is the first thing to suspect and this file cannot fix it.
; #XHCI_ERR_NO_DEVICE and #XHCI_ERR_CAPLENGTH say so in their text.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE 3 GiB WALL - the failure that looks like working hardware
; ======================================================================
; Every ring, every table and every data buffer in this file is read or
; written BY THE CONTROLLER over PCIe. The BCM2711's PCIe wrapper
; cannot reach above $C0000000 - a documented erratum, already encoded
; in pcie.pi4 as PcieDmaOk(). From the CPU that memory is completely
; fine, so a CPU-side test cannot detect the failure at all: the ring
; reads back exactly what was written and the controller simply never
; sees it. A controller that resets cleanly and then does nothing is
; the worst thing on this list to debug, so:
;
;   THE WHOLE ARENA IS CHECKED WITH PcieDmaOk() ONCE, IN XhciInit(),
;   BEFORE ANY ADDRESS IS HANDED TO THE CONTROLLER, and a failure is
;   #XHCI_ERR_DMA_RANGE with the arena's address in XhciArenaBase().
;
; Checking the whole arena once, rather than each allocation, is
; deliberate: the arena is one contiguous run, so one test covers every
; allocation that will ever come out of it, and it cannot drift out of
; step with a new allocation added later.
;
; ALIGNMENT. xhci.h: XHCI_ALIGNMENT is 64, and xhci-mem.c's xhci_malloc()
; aligns EVERY structure to max(XHCI_ALIGNMENT, CACHELINE_SIZE). On a
; Cortex-A72 the line is 64 bytes, so 64 satisfies both. The DCBAA, the
; command ring, the event ring, the ERST, the scratchpad array, both
; device contexts and the endpoint-0 ring are all 64-byte aligned here.
; The alignment is done AT RUN TIME by rounding the arena base up,
; because the compiler's BSS is only ".align 16" and the order of
; globals inside it is not something this file may assume.
;
; An unaligned or unreachable ring produces a controller that resets
; cleanly and then does nothing. Both hazards are therefore checked
; rather than argued about: XhciArenaAligned() reports the aligned base
; and xh_Alloc() refuses a request it cannot satisfy.
;
; ======================================================================
; CACHE - which maintenance operation, and why that one
; ======================================================================
; WAS WRONG, corrected 2026-08-28: this paragraph opened "The MMU and
; caches are ON under the loader". THEY ARE OFF. pi4MmuState read
; SCTLR_EL2 back off this board on 2026-08-26 as $30C50830, which is
; byte-for-byte what armstub8.S:136-141 writes, and in it bit 0 (M),
; bit 2 (C) and bit 12 (I) are all CLEAR - MMU off, D-cache off, I-cache
; off. The maintenance below is therefore currently a NO-OP on this
; board.
;
; IT STAYS, and the reason is not sentiment. The MMU work has a staged
; plan and turns the caches on; a driver whose coherency is correct only
; while the caches happen to be off is a driver that breaks on the day
; somebody else's stage lands, in a way that looks like a USB fault and
; is not one. The cost of keeping it is a handful of instructions per
; TRB against a device that answers in milliseconds.
;
; THE OTHER HALF OF "THE MMU IS OFF" IS NOT OPTIONAL, AND IT BITES:
; with the MMU off, ARMv8-A treats every data access as Device-nGnRnE,
; and an unaligned access to Device memory is an Alignment fault
; REGARDLESS of SCTLR.A - which on this board is clear, and which is
; the reasoning that trapped a whole session elsewhere in this tree. No
; vector is installed, so the fault is not a message; the board stops
; mid-line and needs a power cycle. Every wide Poke/Peek in this file
; lands on a 64-byte-aligned structure at a multiple-of-four offset, and
; that is a requirement, not a habit. A byte-wise writer is the fix for
; anything that cannot promise it.
;
; With the caches ON - which is what the paragraph below describes, and
; what will be true again - a ring the CPU writes and the controller
; reads is not visible to the controller until the line is cleaned, and
; a ring the controller writes and the CPU reads is not visible to the
; CPU until the line is invalidated. U-Boot does
; exactly this and no more - xhci-mem.c, xhci_flush_cache() /
; xhci_inval_cache(), both rounding down to a line and up to a line.
;
; TWO OPERATIONS ARE USED HERE AND THEY ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE:
;
;   dc cvac   CLEAN to the Point of Coherency. Used before the
;             controller reads: TRBs written into a ring, the ERST, the
;             DCBAA, the device contexts, the setup packet. This is the
;             same choice RaspberryPi4/Lib/display.pi4 makes for its
;             framebuffer and for the same reason - the reader is a
;             DMA master, not the instruction fetcher.
;
;   dc civac  CLEAN AND INVALIDATE to the Point of Coherency. Used
;             before the CPU reads something the controller wrote: an
;             event TRB, an output device context, a descriptor buffer.
;
; WHY civac AND NOT ivac, which is the literal "invalidate":
;   * dc ivac is an EL1-or-above operation. This file makes no claim
;     about the exception level a loader leaves us in, and an operation
;     that traps is a fault, not a slow path.
;   * dc ivac DISCARDS a dirty line. Every buffer here was zeroed by
;     the CPU before the controller was told about it, so dirty lines
;     genuinely exist, and discarding one loses the zeroing. civac
;     writes it back first and then invalidates, which is correct in
;     both directions and costs a write-back that never happens twice.
;   * civac is already the operation RaspberryPi4/Monitor/anvil.pi4
;     uses for its autoboot record, and its header carries the encoding
;     table these come from.
;
; anvil.pi4 also uses "dc cvau" + "ic ivau". That is the OTHER
; case - clean to the point of UNIFICATION, which is what an
; instruction fetch needs after writing code. Nothing in this file is
; code, so cvau is wrong here and is not used.
;
; ENCODINGS, from the table in anvil.pi4's header:
;     dc cvac,  x9   op1=3 CRn=7 CRm=10 op2=1 Rt=9  -> $D50B7A29
;     dc civac, x9   op1=3 CRn=7 CRm=14 op2=1 Rt=9  -> $D50B7E29
;
; "ldr x9", not "ldr w9", in both maintenance procedures. xh_cacheaddr
; is a .i and a .i is EIGHT bytes on this target; ldr w9 assembles
; cleanly, uses the right register, and silently drops the top half of
; the address. That is the shape of bug the 64-bit widening creates in
; hand-written assembly, and it is why those blocks are three lines of
; address materialisation for one instruction of work - the same shape
; display.pi4's DisplayCacheLine() uses, deliberately.
;
; ======================================================================
; 64 BITS, AND THE TRAP IT SETS
; ======================================================================
; .i is 64 bits on this target. That is convenient for ring pointers,
; which really are 64-bit, and it is a trap everywhere else:
;
;   * A TRB FIELD IS 32 BITS. All four of them. The cycle bit is bit 0
;     of a 32-bit word, and every write of a TRB field goes through
;     PokeN, which is a 32-bit store - never PokeI or PokeX, which
;     would take the next field with it.
;
;   * AN OPERATIONAL REGISTER IS 32 BITS, except CRCR, DCBAAP, ERSTBA
;     and ERDP, which are 64. xhci.h is explicit about how to write the
;     wide ones - xhci_writeq():
;
;       "Registers with 64-bit address pointers should be written to
;        with dword accesses by writing the low dword first (ptr[0]),
;        then the high dword (ptr[1]) second."
;
;     xh_Wr64() does exactly that, low half then high half, two 32-bit
;     stores. It is NOT one 64-bit store, and the order is not
;     cosmetic.
;
;   * EVERY REGISTER READ IS PeekN, NOT PeekL. PeekL sign-extends -
;     ruled 2026-08-25, and observable only where .i is wider than the
;     access, which is here. USBSTS bit 11 (CNR) and PORTSC bit 31
;     (Warm Port Reset) are both real bits in the top half; read
;     through PeekL a status word with bit 31 set comes back negative
;     and every subsequent comparison is wrong.
;
;   * EVERY DESCRIPTOR BYTE IS PeekA, NOT PeekB. PeekB sign-extends
;     too. bMaxPacketSize0 of 64 is fine either way; bcdUSB's high byte
;     of $03 is fine; idVendor's high byte very often is not, and a
;     PeekB on a descriptor byte above 127 gives a negative number.
;
; ======================================================================
; NO RECURSION, AND NO DYNAMIC ALLOCATION
; ======================================================================
; Locals are statically allocated on this backend and recursion is
; refused, so there is no recursive ring walker here and no linked list
; of segments. U-Boot allocates a chain of segments and links them;
; this file allocates ONE segment per ring, which is what U-Boot
; actually ends up using anyway - xhci-mem.c says so in its own comment
; on xhci_ring_alloc():
;
;     "current code only uses one-time-allocated single-segment rings"
;
; and ERST_NUM_SEGS is 1. A single segment turns the segment walk into
; an index compare, which is what xh_QueueTrb() and xh_AckEvent() do.
;
; ======================================================================
; HOW FAR THIS GOES, STEP BY STEP - and where it stops
; ======================================================================
;  1. CAPABILITY REGISTERS - done. CAPLENGTH, HCIVERSION, HCSPARAMS1/2/3,
;     HCCPARAMS1, DBOFF, RTSOFF, all read once in xh_ReadCaps() and
;     available through XhciMaxSlots() and friends.
;  2. OPERATIONAL REGISTERS - done. USBCMD, USBSTS, PAGESIZE, CRCR,
;     DCBAAP, CONFIG, DNCTRL.
;  3. RESET AND RUN - done. Halt, HCRST, wait CNR clear, DCBAA, command
;     ring, event ring + ERST, scratchpad array, CONFIG, run, wait for
;     HCHalted to clear.
;  4. PORTS - done. XhciPortSc(), XhciPortConnected(), XhciPortSpeed(),
;     XhciPortReset().
;  5. CONTROL TRANSFERS - written, and it is the least trustworthy part
;     of this file. Enable Slot, Address Device and a three-TRB control
;     transfer are all here and all follow xhci-ring.c line for line,
;     but a control transfer is the first thing that needs a real
;     DEVICE to answer, and the model in the oracle answers the way the
;     specification says a device should. That is a test of this file's
;     state machine and not of anything else.
;
; NOT HERE, ON PURPOSE: bulk transfers, Configure Endpoint, hubs,
; interrupts, streams, isochronous transfers, mass storage, and any
; notion of a filesystem.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHAT NEEDS REAL HARDWARE BEFORE ANY OF THIS IS BELIEVABLE
; ======================================================================
;   * that PcieInit() brings the link up at all
;   * that a config read of bus 1 / device 0 / function 0 returns
;     something other than $FFFFFFFF, and what the vendor/device is
;   * that BAR0 holds a bus address inside the $F8000000 window
;   * that the CPU can actually load from the translated address - this
;     depends on the loader's page tables mapping the outbound window
;     as Device memory, which NOTHING in this repository establishes
;   * CAPLENGTH, HCIVERSION and HCSPARAMS1 as actually read
;   * whether HCCPARAMS1 says 64-byte contexts (the VL805's answer is
;     not known here and it changes every context offset)
;   * how many scratchpad buffers HCSPARAMS2 asks for
;   * that HCRST self-clears and CNR clears after it
;   * that a No-Op command produces a completion event
;   * that a port reports a connection and survives a reset
;   * everything about step 5
; ======================================================================

EnableExplicit

; ======================================================================
;  CAPABILITY REGISTERS - offsets from the BAR0 base.
;  xhci.h: struct xhci_hccr. The struct is seven uint32_t in order, so
;  the offsets are 0,4,8,...  Written out rather than computed because
;  a wrong offset here reads a real register belonging to something
;  else, which is the failure mode this project keeps paying for.
; ======================================================================
#XHCI_CAPLENGTH  = $00   ; cr_capbase:   CAPLENGTH 7:0, HCIVERSION 31:16
#XHCI_HCSPARAMS1 = $04   ; cr_hcsparams1
#XHCI_HCSPARAMS2 = $08   ; cr_hcsparams2
#XHCI_HCSPARAMS3 = $0C   ; cr_hcsparams3
#XHCI_HCCPARAMS1 = $10   ; cr_hccparams
#XHCI_DBOFF      = $14   ; cr_dboff
#XHCI_RTSOFF     = $18   ; cr_rtsoff

; xhci.h: DBOFF_MASK (~0x3) and RTSOFF_MASK (~0x1f) - the low bits are
; reserved and are NOT part of the offset. Written as positive masks
; because ~ of a literal is 64-bit here.
#XHCI_DBOFF_MASK  = $FFFFFFFC   ; ~$3
#XHCI_RTSOFF_MASK = $FFFFFFE0   ; ~$1F

; ======================================================================
;  OPERATIONAL REGISTERS - offsets from (BAR0 + CAPLENGTH).
;  xhci.h: struct xhci_hcor. Derived by walking the struct:
;    or_usbcmd 0, or_usbsts 4, or_pagesize 8, reserved_0[2] 12..19,
;    or_dnctrl 20, or_crcr 24 (8 bytes), reserved_1[4] 32..47,
;    or_dcbaap 48 (8 bytes), or_config 56, reserved_2[241] 60..1023,
;    portregs[] 1024.
; ======================================================================
#XHCI_USBCMD   = $00
#XHCI_USBSTS   = $04
#XHCI_PAGESIZE = $08
#XHCI_DNCTRL   = $14
#XHCI_CRCR     = $18   ; 64-bit
#XHCI_DCBAAP   = $30   ; 64-bit
#XHCI_CONFIG   = $38

; xhci.h: struct xhci_hcor_port_regs is four uint32_t, so 16 bytes per
; port, and portregs[] follows reserved_2[241] at byte 1024.
#XHCI_PORTBASE   = $400   ; 1024
#XHCI_PORT_STRIDE = $10   ; 16
#XHCI_PORTSC     = $00
#XHCI_PORTPMSC   = $04
#XHCI_PORTLI     = $08

; ======================================================================
;  RUNTIME REGISTERS - offsets from (BAR0 + RTSOFF).
;  xhci.h: struct xhci_run_regs { __le32 microframe_index; __le32
;  rsvd[7]; struct xhci_intr_reg ir_set[128]; } so ir_set[0] is at 32.
;  struct xhci_intr_reg: irq_pending 0, irq_control 4, erst_size 8,
;  rsvd 12, erst_base 16 (8 bytes), erst_dequeue 24 (8 bytes).
; ======================================================================
#XHCI_MFINDEX = $00
#XHCI_IR0     = $20   ; 32 - interrupter 0
#XHCI_IMAN    = $00   ; irq_pending,  relative to the interrupter
#XHCI_IMOD    = $04   ; irq_control
#XHCI_ERSTSZ  = $08   ; erst_size
#XHCI_ERSTBA  = $10   ; erst_base,    64-bit
#XHCI_ERDP    = $18   ; erst_dequeue, 64-bit

; ======================================================================
;  USBCMD bits - xhci.h
; ======================================================================
#XHCI_CMD_RUN    = $001   ; XHCI_CMD_RUN   (1 << 0)
#XHCI_CMD_RESET  = $002   ; CMD_RESET      (1 << 1)  - HCRST
#XHCI_CMD_EIE    = $004   ; XHCI_CMD_EIE   (1 << 2)
#XHCI_CMD_HSEIE  = $008   ; XHCI_CMD_HSEIE (1 << 3)
#XHCI_CMD_LRESET = $080   ; CMD_LRESET     (1 << 7)
#XHCI_CMD_EWE    = $400   ; XHCI_CMD_EWE   (1 << 10)

; ======================================================================
;  USBSTS bits - xhci.h
; ======================================================================
#XHCI_STS_HALT    = $0001   ; XHCI_STS_HALT (1 << 0)  - HCHalted
#XHCI_STS_FATAL   = $0004   ; STS_FATAL     (1 << 2)
#XHCI_STS_EINT    = $0008   ; STS_EINT      (1 << 3)
#XHCI_STS_PORT    = $0010   ; STS_PORT      (1 << 4)
#XHCI_STS_SRE     = $0400   ; STS_SRE       (1 << 10)
#XHCI_STS_CNR     = $0800   ; XHCI_STS_CNR  (1 << 11) - Controller Not
                            ;                           Ready
#XHCI_STS_HCE     = $1000   ; STS_HCE       (1 << 12)

; ======================================================================
;  CRCR bits - xhci.h. CMD_RING_RSVD_BITS is 0x3f: the low six bits of
;  CRCR are NOT part of the pointer, and bit 0 is the Ring Cycle State.
; ======================================================================
#XHCI_CRCR_RSVD_BITS = $3F   ; CMD_RING_RSVD_BITS
#XHCI_CRCR_RUNNING   = $08   ; CMD_RING_RUNNING (1 << 3)

; xhci.h: ERST_PTR_MASK (0xf) - the low four bits of ERSTBA are not
; part of the pointer; ERST_EHB (1 << 3) is Event Handler Busy in ERDP.
#XHCI_ERST_PTR_MASK = $0F
#XHCI_ERST_EHB      = $08

; xhci.h: ERST_SIZE_MASK (0xffff << 16) - the top half of ERSTSZ is
; preserved on a write.
#XHCI_ERSTSZ_MASK = $FFFF0000

; ======================================================================
;  PORTSC bits - xhci.h
; ======================================================================
#XHCI_PORT_CONNECT = $00000001   ; PORT_CONNECT (1 << 0)   CCS
#XHCI_PORT_PE      = $00000002   ; PORT_PE      (1 << 1)   enabled
#XHCI_PORT_OC      = $00000008   ; PORT_OC      (1 << 3)
#XHCI_PORT_RESET   = $00000010   ; PORT_RESET   (1 << 4)   PR
#XHCI_PORT_PLS_MASK = $000001E0  ; PORT_PLS_MASK (0xf << 5)
#XHCI_PORT_POWER   = $00000200   ; PORT_POWER   (1 << 9)   PP
#XHCI_PORT_SPD_MASK = $00003C00  ; DEV_SPEED_MASK (0xf << 10)
#XHCI_PORT_LINK_STROBE = $00010000 ; PORT_LINK_STROBE (1 << 16)
#XHCI_PORT_CSC     = $00020000   ; PORT_CSC (1 << 17)
#XHCI_PORT_PEC     = $00040000   ; PORT_PEC (1 << 18)
#XHCI_PORT_WRC     = $00080000   ; PORT_WRC (1 << 19)
#XHCI_PORT_OCC     = $00100000   ; PORT_OCC (1 << 20)
#XHCI_PORT_RC      = $00200000   ; PORT_RC  (1 << 21)  reset change
#XHCI_PORT_PLC     = $00400000   ; PORT_PLC (1 << 22)
#XHCI_PORT_CEC     = $00800000   ; PORT_CEC (1 << 23)
#XHCI_PORT_WR      = $80000000   ; PORT_WR  (1 << 31)  warm reset

; All the change bits together - every one is write-1-to-clear.
; xhci.h: XHCI_PORT_RW1CS is ((1 << 1) | (0x7f << 17)); the (1 << 1) in
; that macro is Port Enabled/Disabled, which is NOT a change bit and is
; deliberately excluded here - writing a 1 to it DISABLES the port.
#XHCI_PORT_CHANGE_BITS = $00FE0000   ; 0x7f << 17

; xhci.h: the write-back masks. XHCI_PORT_RO is
; ((1<<0)|(1<<3)|(0xf<<10)|(1<<30)) = $00003C09;
; XHCI_PORT_RWS is ((0xf<<5)|(1<<9)|(0x3<<14)|(0x7<<25)) = $0E00C3E0.
; xhci.c's xhci_port_state_to_neutral() returns their OR, which is the
; value that leaves the port in the same state if written back.
#XHCI_PORT_RO      = $00003C09
#XHCI_PORT_RWS     = $0E00C3E0
#XHCI_PORT_NEUTRAL = $0E00FFE9   ; RO | RWS

; Speeds - xhci.h, PORTSC bits 13:10, already shifted down by 10.
#XHCI_SPEED_UNDEF = 0
#XHCI_SPEED_FULL  = 1   ; XDEV_FS (0x1 << 10)
#XHCI_SPEED_LOW   = 2   ; XDEV_LS (0x2 << 10)
#XHCI_SPEED_HIGH  = 3   ; XDEV_HS (0x3 << 10)
#XHCI_SPEED_SUPER = 4   ; XDEV_SS (0x4 << 10)

; ======================================================================
;  TRB types - xhci.h, the trb_type enum. TRB_TYPE(p) is (p << 10), so
;  these are the RAW enum values and the shift happens in code.
; ======================================================================
#XHCI_TRB_NORMAL      = 1
#XHCI_TRB_SETUP       = 2
#XHCI_TRB_DATA        = 3
#XHCI_TRB_STATUS      = 4
#XHCI_TRB_LINK        = 6
; xhci.h:918, TRB_TR_NOOP - "Transfer Ring No-op (not for the command
; ring)". Used only by xh_RingRoom, to pad the tail of a segment so the
; controller can walk to the Link TRB. There is a SECOND no-op at 23 and
; they are not interchangeable: 23 is the COMMAND ring's and putting it
; on a transfer ring is a TRB Error.
#XHCI_TRB_TR_NOOP     = 8
#XHCI_TRB_ENABLE_SLOT = 9
#XHCI_TRB_DISABLE_SLOT = 10
#XHCI_TRB_ADDR_DEV    = 11
#XHCI_TRB_CONFIG_EP   = 12
#XHCI_TRB_EVAL_CTX    = 13   ; Evaluate Context, xHCI 1.1 table 6-90
; Reset Endpoint and Set TR Dequeue Pointer - rpi-6.12.y_xhci.h:1116 and
; :1120. THESE TWO ARE A PAIR and neither is any use alone; see
; xh_ClearHalt(). Stop Ring is named beside them because a reader who
; finds 14 and 16 here will wonder what 15 is, and the answer - "Stop
; Endpoint, which this file has no use for" - is worth one line.
#XHCI_TRB_RESET_EP    = 14
#XHCI_TRB_STOP_RING   = 15   ; not used; named so 14 and 16 are not a gap
#XHCI_TRB_SET_DEQ     = 16
#XHCI_TRB_CMD_NOOP    = 23
#XHCI_TRB_TRANSFER    = 32
#XHCI_TRB_COMPLETION  = 33
#XHCI_TRB_PORT_STATUS = 34

; TRB control-word bits - xhci.h
#XHCI_TRB_CYCLE   = $0001   ; TRB_CYCLE (1<<0)
#XHCI_TRB_ENT     = $0002   ; TRB_ENT   (1<<1)
#XHCI_TRB_ISP     = $0004   ; TRB_ISP   (1<<2)
#XHCI_TRB_CHAIN   = $0010   ; TRB_CHAIN (1<<4)
#XHCI_TRB_IOC     = $0020   ; TRB_IOC   (1<<5)
#XHCI_TRB_IDT     = $0040   ; TRB_IDT   (1<<6)
#XHCI_TRB_DIR_IN  = $10000  ; TRB_DIR_IN (1<<16)
#XHCI_LINK_TOGGLE = $0002   ; LINK_TOGGLE (0x1 << 1)

; xhci.h: TRB_DATA_OUT 2, TRB_DATA_IN 3, placed by TRB_TX_TYPE(p) at
; bit 16 in the Setup stage TRB of an xHCI 1.0 controller.
#XHCI_TX_TYPE_OUT = $20000   ; 2 << 16
#XHCI_TX_TYPE_IN  = $30000   ; 3 << 16

; Completion codes - xhci.h, the xhci_comp_code enum. Only the ones
; this file distinguishes are named; the rest are reported as numbers,
; because inventing a name for a code we have never seen is worse than
; printing the code.
#XHCI_COMP_SUCCESS  = 1
#XHCI_COMP_SHORT_TX = 13
#XHCI_COMP_STALL    = 6

; ======================================================================
;  RING GEOMETRY - xhci.h
;  TRBS_PER_SEGMENT 64, SEGMENT_SIZE (TRBS_PER_SEGMENT*16) = 1024.
;  The last TRB of a command or transfer ring is the Link TRB, so a
;  ring holds 63 usable entries.
; ======================================================================
#XHCI_TRBS_PER_SEG = 64
#XHCI_TRB_BYTES    = 16
#XHCI_SEGMENT_SIZE = 1024   ; 64 * 16
#XHCI_LINK_INDEX   = 63     ; TRBS_PER_SEGMENT - 1

; xhci.h: ERST_NUM_SEGS 1. One event-ring segment, so one ERST entry,
; and the ERST entry is { __le64 seg_addr; __le32 seg_size; __le32
; rsvd; } - sixteen bytes.
#XHCI_ERST_NUM_SEGS  = 1
#XHCI_ERST_ENTRY_SIZE = 16

; xhci.h: XHCI_ALIGNMENT 64, and the Cortex-A72 cache line is 64, so
; one constant serves both.
#XHCI_ALIGN = 64
#XHCI_CACHELINE = 64

; xhci.h: MAX_HC_SLOTS 256 (section 6.1). The DCBAA has one 64-bit
; entry per slot PLUS entry 0, which holds the scratchpad array - so
; 257 entries. This file only ever enables as many slots as HCSPARAMS1
; reports, but the table is sized for the architectural maximum so the
; arena arithmetic cannot depend on a register read.
#XHCI_MAX_SLOTS  = 256
#XHCI_DCBAA_BYTES = 2056   ; (256 + 1) * 8

; Context sizes - xhci.h: CTX_SIZE(_hcc) is 64 if HCC_64BYTE_CONTEXT
; else 32, and a Device Context is (MAX_EP_CTX_NUM + 1) = 32 contexts.
; An Input Context is one context longer, for the Input Control
; Context at its head. Sized for the 64-byte case because that is the
; larger one and the VL805's answer is not known here.
#XHCI_CTX_MAX      = 64
#XHCI_DEV_CTX_BYTES = 2048   ; 32 * 64
#XHCI_IN_CTX_BYTES  = 2112   ; 33 * 64

; Input Control Context flags - xhci.h: SLOT_FLAG (1 << 0),
; EP0_FLAG (1 << 1).
#XHCI_SLOT_FLAG = 1
#XHCI_EP0_FLAG  = 2

; ======================================================================
;  SLOT CONTEXT FIELDS THAT ONLY A HUB TOPOLOGY NEEDS - xhci.h:517-563.
;  Added 2026-08-28. Everything before this addressed devices on root
;  ports, where all four of these are zero and could be left implicit.
;  They cannot be left implicit for a device behind a hub, and on this
;  board every low- and full-speed device IS behind a hub.
; ======================================================================
; dev_info bits 19:0 - xhci.h:519, ROUTE_STRING_MASK (0xfffff).
#XHCI_ROUTE_MASK   = $000FFFFF
; dev_info bit 25 - xhci.h:524, DEV_MTT. "Is this LS/FS device connected
; through a HS hub?" - more precisely, through a hub running its
; per-port transaction translators.
#XHCI_DEV_MTT      = $02000000   ; 1 << 25
; dev_info bit 26 - xhci.h:526, DEV_HUB. "Set if the device is a hub".
#XHCI_DEV_HUB      = $04000000   ; 1 << 26
; tt_info bits 17:16 - xhci.h:557, TT_THINK_TIME(p) is ((p & 0x3) << 16).
#XHCI_TT_THINK_MASK = $00030000  ; 0x3 << 16

; Endpoint types - xhci.h: EP_TYPE(p) is (p << 3), CTRL_EP is 4.
#XHCI_EP_TYPE_CTRL = $20   ; 4 << 3
#XHCI_EP_TYPE_BULK_OUT = $10   ; 2 << 3   xhci.h EP_TYPE(BULK_OUT_EP)
#XHCI_EP_TYPE_BULK_IN  = $30   ; 6 << 3   xhci.h EP_TYPE(BULK_IN_EP)
; ERROR_COUNT(p) is ((p & 0x3) << 1); U-Boot uses 3.
#XHCI_EP_ERRCNT_3  = $06   ; 3 << 1

; ======================================================================
;  ENDPOINT STATE - the low three bits of word 0 of an OUTPUT endpoint
;  context. xhci.h's EP_STATE_MASK and the names beside it.
;
;  THESE ARE NAMED BECAUSE ONE OF THEM IS LOAD-BEARING. An endpoint that
;  the controller has moved to HALTED does not fail transfers - it does
;  not RUN them. The doorbell is accepted, nothing executes, no event is
;  ever posted, and the caller times out. Every transfer after it does
;  the same, on every interface of that device, because endpoint zero is
;  shared. Measured on silicon 2026-08-28: a wireless receiver's mouse
;  interface stalled SET_IDLE, endpoint 0 went to state 2, and the
;  KEYBOARD interface - which had answered GET_REPORT in 2 ms one
;  transfer earlier - then timed out at five seconds, twice, along with
;  everything else. See xh_ClearHalt().
; ======================================================================
#XHCI_EP_STATE_DISABLED = 0
#XHCI_EP_STATE_RUNNING  = 1
#XHCI_EP_STATE_HALTED   = 2
#XHCI_EP_STATE_STOPPED  = 3
#XHCI_EP_STATE_ERROR    = 4

; ======================================================================
;  SCRATCHPAD - how many this file is willing to provide.
;  xhci.h: HCS_MAX_SCRATCHPAD(p) combines two fields of HCSPARAMS2 and
;  can ask for up to 1023 buffers of PAGESIZE each. This file has a
;  fixed arena, so it provides up to #XHCI_MAX_SCRATCH pages and
;  REFUSES with #XHCI_ERR_SCRATCH if the controller wants more. That
;  refusal is the point: silently providing fewer buffers than asked
;  for is a controller writing into memory that is not its own.
; ======================================================================
; RAISED 16 -> 128 on 2026-08-25. The real VL805 refused to start at
; 16: 'the controller wants more scratchpad buffers than this driver
; provides' was the first thing this driver ever said on hardware, and
; it was the RIGHT thing to say - handing a controller fewer buffers
; than it asked for is a DMA master writing into memory that is not
; its own.
;
; 128 pages is 512 KiB of BSS. On a part with gigabytes that is free,
; and it covers anything short of the 1023 the spec permits. The
; refusal stays for the rest, because the alternative to refusing is
; still silent corruption.
#XHCI_MAX_SCRATCH  = 128
#XHCI_PAGE_BYTES   = 4096      ; the PAGESIZE=bit0 case; checked at run
                               ; time against the register
#XHCI_SCRATCH_BYTES = 524288   ; 128 * 4096
#XHCI_SCRATCH_ARRAY_BYTES = 1024 ; 128 * 8

; ======================================================================
;  Bounce buffer for control-transfer data. Sized for the largest thing
;  step 5 fetches - a configuration descriptor is capped here at 512.
;  DMA goes to THIS buffer, never to the caller's, so the caller's
;  destination has no alignment or reachability requirement at all.
; ======================================================================
#XHCI_BOUNCE_BYTES = 512

; The setup packet, in its own 64-byte-aligned slot. It is not actually
; DMA'd - a Setup Stage TRB carries the eight bytes IMMEDIATELY, in the
; TRB itself (xhci.h: TRB_IDT, "The buffer pointer contains immediate
; data") - but keeping it in the arena keeps every structure this file
; builds in one checked region.
#XHCI_SETUP_BYTES = 64

; ======================================================================
;  THE ARENA. One contiguous run, bump-allocated, 64-byte aligned, and
;  checked once with PcieDmaOk(). The extra #XHCI_ALIGN bytes are the
;  slack the run-time alignment needs, because the compiler's BSS is
;  only ".align 16".
;
;  The total is written out as a literal because a constant initialiser
;  takes a bare literal:
;      2056 DCBAA
;    + 1024 command ring
;    + 1024 event ring
;    +   64 ERST (16 bytes, rounded to alignment)
;    +  128 scratchpad array
;    +65536 scratchpad pages
;    + 2048 output device context
;    + 2112 input device context
;    + 1024 endpoint-0 ring
;    +  512 bounce buffer
;    +   64 setup packet
;    = 75592, plus 64 slack and rounding = 76032.
; ======================================================================
; Grown 2026-08-26 by #XHCI_MAX_DEVS * (one 1024-byte transfer ring +
; one 2048-byte output device context) = 4 * 3072 = 12288 bytes.
; Plus two 1024-byte bulk rings, 2026-08-26.
;
; Grown again 2026-08-28 with #XHCI_MAX_DEVS 4 -> 8: another 4 * 3072 =
; 12288. The running total, which is worth writing out because the
; literal below is otherwise unauditable:
;
;      2056  DCBAA
;   +  1024  command ring
;   + 24576  eight slots * (1024 transfer ring + 2048 output context)
;   +  2048  bulk OUT and bulk IN rings
;   +  1024  event ring
;   +    64  ERST, 16 bytes rounded to alignment
;   +  1024  scratchpad array
;   +524288  128 scratchpad pages
;   +  2112  input device context
;   +   512  bounce buffer
;   +    64  setup packet
;   =558792, plus 64 slack and rounding
;
; The arena is checked once with PcieDmaOk() and xh_Alloc() refuses
; rather than overruns, so getting this too SMALL is a named error
; (#XHCI_ERR_ARENA) and not corruption. Getting it too large costs BSS.
#XHCI_ARENA_BYTES = 558912

; ======================================================================
;  RING IDS. Two producer rings, indexed rather than pointed at,
;  because a static array is what this backend can do without
;  recursion or dynamic allocation.
; ======================================================================
; ======================================================================
;  ONE ENDPOINT-ZERO RING PER SLOT, AND ONE OUTPUT CONTEXT PER SLOT
; ======================================================================
;  CHANGED 2026-08-26, after a hub and a flash drive on the same bus
;  produced a failure that looked like everything except what it was.
;
;  There used to be a SINGLE endpoint-zero ring, #XHCI_RING_EP0, and a
;  single output device context shared by every slot. With one device
;  attached that is correct and it was tested that way for weeks.
;
;  With two, this happens: the first slot works indefinitely, the
;  second works for exactly ONE transfer, and every transfer after that
;  reports a plausible byte count into a buffer that never changes.
;  Asking for 64 bytes of a 44-byte descriptor returned "44, short
;  packet", then "64, success" - two answers that contradict each other
;  AND the buffer, which stayed zero throughout.
;
;  The cause is not subtle once stated. Address Device reset the shared
;  ring for the new slot, but the PREVIOUS slot's device context still
;  pointed into that same memory at whatever offset the controller had
;  reached. Two device contexts, one ring, two dequeue pointers, and a
;  producer cycle state that means different things to each of them.
;  The DCBAA had the same problem: every slot's entry pointed at one
;  output context, so the controller wrote all of them on top of each
;  other.
;
;  WHAT THAT COST, and it is the reason for the long comment: the
;  symptom was blamed on SuperSpeed, on short packets, and on cache
;  maintenance in turn, because the device that failed happened to be
;  the SuperSpeed one and the device that worked happened to be first.
;  The tell was that the HUB took the identical short-packet read and
;  answered perfectly - which rules out speed, short packets and the
;  invalidate path together. A reported byte count that disagrees with
;  the buffer is not a coherency problem. It is bookkeeping about a
;  transfer that did not happen.
;
;  RING INDEX IS NOW THE SLOT NUMBER. Ring 0 is the command ring; ring
;  s belongs to slot s. Slots are numbered from 1 by the controller, so
;  the mapping needs no arithmetic and cannot be got backwards.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#XHCI_RING_CMD = 0

; How many device slots this file will address at once. The controller
; on this board offers 32. A slot id beyond this is REFUSED, not
; wrapped - see #XHCI_ERR_TOO_MANY_DEVS.
;
; RAISED 4 -> 8 on 2026-08-28, and the arithmetic is why. Four was
; chosen as "two more than the hub-plus-stick this was written against".
; Adding input devices makes the realistic bench hub(1) + boot stick(2)
; + keyboard(3) + mouse(4), which is EXACTLY four - so the next thing
; anybody plugs in, or one failed enumeration retried, refuses. Slot ids
; are handed out by the controller and this file never gives one back,
; so they only climb.
;
; The cost is #XHCI_MAX_DEVS * (1024-byte transfer ring + 2048-byte
; output context) = 3072 bytes per slot, so four more slots is 12288
; bytes of BSS on a part with gigabytes. That is a much better trade
; than a bench session lost to a refusal whose cause is a constant.
#XHCI_MAX_DEVS = 8
#XHCI_RING_EP0 = 1          ; ring 1 = slot 1; kept for readability
; Rings 9 and 10 are the bulk pair. They belong to ONE device at a time -
; see xh_mscSlot - because a second mass-storage device would need its
; own pair and folding two onto one is precisely the defect the
; per-slot control rings above were introduced to end. A second claim
; is REFUSED rather than quietly served.
;
; THESE MOVED 5,6 -> 9,10 on 2026-08-28 with #XHCI_MAX_DEVS 4 -> 8, and
; they HAD to. Ring index IS the slot number for slots 1..#XHCI_MAX_DEVS,
; so with eight slots the bulk pair at 5 and 6 would have been slot 5's
; and slot 6's control rings as well. Two owners of one ring is the
; 2026-08-26 defect exactly, and it would have come back the moment a
; hub pushed the boot stick past slot 4. Nothing outside this file names
; these numbers; they are indices into xh_ringBase[].
#XHCI_RING_BULK_OUT = 9
#XHCI_RING_BULK_IN  = 10
#XHCI_RING_N   = 11         ; 1 command + 8 slot rings + 2 bulk rings.
                            ; Written out because a constant expression
                            ; is not parsed here; keep the three in step
                            ; with #XHCI_MAX_DEVS.

; ======================================================================
;  TIMEOUTS. Three of the four come straight out of xhci.h; the fourth
;  is this file's own and is labelled as such.
; ======================================================================
#XHCI_TMO_HALT_MS  = 16     ; XHCI_MAX_HALT_USEC (16*1000) us
#XHCI_TMO_RESET_MS = 250    ; XHCI_MAX_RESET_USEC (250*1000) us
#XHCI_TMO_EVENT_MS = 5000   ; XHCI_TIMEOUT 5000 - "Generic timeout for
                            ; XHCI events"
; THIS ONE IS OURS, not U-Boot's. The xHCI specification requires a
; root port reset to complete well inside this; 500 ms is a bound
; chosen to be obviously longer than any legitimate reset rather than a
; number read out of a document, and it is labelled so nobody cites it
; back at me as a specification value.
#XHCI_TMO_PORT_MS  = 500
; Port power settling. Also ours, and also not a specification value.
#XHCI_TMO_PPWR_MS  = 20

; ======================================================================
;  THE CONTROL-TRANSFER TIMEOUT IS SHORTER THAN THE COMMAND ONE, AND
;  THAT IS DELIBERATE. Added 2026-08-28.
; ======================================================================
;  Two working stacks disagree about this number and both are citable:
;
;    Linux  USB_CTRL_GET_TIMEOUT / USB_CTRL_SET_TIMEOUT are 5000 ms -
;           rpi-6.12.y_include_usb.h:1899-1900.
;    U-Boot USB_TIMEOUT_MS(pipe) is "usb_pipebulk(pipe) ? 5000 : 1000" -
;           v2025.01_include_usb.h:50. A CONTROL transfer gets one
;           second; only bulk gets five.
;
;  This board takes U-Boot's number, and the reason is not taste. The
;  BCM2711 watchdog cannot be armed past PM_WDOG_MAX_TICKS $000FFFFF,
;  about 15.999 s - safety.pi4:434 - so the total budget for a program
;  is FIXED at sixteen seconds and cannot be widened. Three failed
;  control transfers at Linux's number are the entire budget and the
;  board reboots with nothing printed; at U-Boot's they are three
;  seconds and the failure gets reported. Linux has no such ceiling: it
;  is not racing a deadman it cannot lengthen.
;
;  A device that needs longer than one second to answer a control
;  request is not a device this stack can drive anyway, because the
;  caller is a bare-metal program with a sixteen-second life.
;
;  COMMANDS KEEP 5000. An xHCI command is answered by the CONTROLLER,
;  not by a device on a wire, and Linux's XHCI_CMD_DEFAULT_TIMEOUT is
;  five seconds for exactly that reason. Bulk keeps 5000 too, which is
;  what both stacks say.
#XHCI_TMO_CTRL_MS  = 1000

; ======================================================================
;  ERROR CODES. One per failure, each with an English string, in the
;  shape emmc.pi4 uses - because "it returned 0" sends somebody to look
;  at the cable when the answer was in a status bit.
; ======================================================================
#XHCI_ERR_NONE       =  0
#XHCI_ERR_NO_CNTFRQ  =  1  ; CNTFRQ_EL0 read back 0 or absurd. Nothing
                           ; is attempted: every bounded wait below is
                           ; measured against it, and a lying clock
                           ; makes them all unbounded again.
#XHCI_ERR_NO_DEVICE  =  2  ; config read of offset $00 gave $FFFFFFFF
                           ; or 0 - nothing answering at that b/d/f
#XHCI_ERR_BAR_IO     =  3  ; BAR0 is an I/O BAR, not memory
#XHCI_ERR_BAR_HIGH   =  4  ; BAR0 is a 64-bit BAR with a non-zero high
                           ; half - outside the root complex's window
#XHCI_ERR_BAR_ZERO   =  5  ; BAR0 reads back zero - firmware never
                           ; assigned it
#XHCI_ERR_BAR_RANGE  =  6  ; PcieCpuFromBus() refused the BAR address
#XHCI_ERR_CAPLENGTH  =  7  ; CAPLENGTH is 0 or $FF - not a controller,
                           ; or its firmware was never loaded
#XHCI_ERR_HCIVERSION =  8  ; HCIVERSION below 0x0090 or above 0x0120
#XHCI_ERR_HCSPARAMS  =  9  ; HCSPARAMS1 says zero ports or zero slots
#XHCI_ERR_DMA_RANGE  = 10  ; the arena is not reachable by the
                           ; controller - see THE 3 GiB WALL
#XHCI_ERR_ARENA      = 11  ; the arena ran out. A build-time mistake in
                           ; #XHCI_ARENA_BYTES, not a hardware fault.
#XHCI_ERR_HALT       = 12  ; HCHalted never set after clearing Run/Stop
#XHCI_ERR_RESET      = 13  ; HCRST never self-cleared
#XHCI_ERR_CNR        = 14  ; Controller Not Ready never cleared
#XHCI_ERR_PAGESIZE   = 15  ; PAGESIZE names a page this file cannot
                           ; provide
#XHCI_ERR_SCRATCH    = 16  ; the controller wants more scratchpad
                           ; buffers than #XHCI_MAX_SCRATCH
#XHCI_ERR_RUN        = 17  ; HCHalted never cleared after setting Run
#XHCI_ERR_NOT_READY  = 18  ; a call that needs XhciInit() to have
                           ; succeeded, made before it did
#XHCI_ERR_PORTNUM    = 19  ; port number outside 1..MaxPorts
#XHCI_ERR_NO_CONNECT = 20  ; no device connected to that port
#XHCI_ERR_PORT_POWER = 21  ; port power never came up
#XHCI_ERR_PORT_RESET = 22  ; the port never reported reset complete
#XHCI_ERR_PORT_ENABLE = 23 ; reset finished but the port is not enabled
#XHCI_ERR_EVT_TIMEOUT = 24 ; no event of the expected type inside the
                           ; timeout
#XHCI_ERR_CMD_FAILED = 25  ; a command completed with a completion code
                           ; other than Success - XhciLastComp() has it
#XHCI_ERR_NO_SLOT    = 26  ; Enable Slot returned slot 0
#XHCI_ERR_BAD_SLOT   = 27  ; slot id outside 1..MaxSlots
#XHCI_ERR_XFER       = 28  ; the transfer event carried a failing
                           ; completion code
#XHCI_ERR_STALL      = 29  ; the device stalled the control endpoint
#XHCI_ERR_ARG        = 30  ; a caller argument is refused - null
                           ; pointer, negative length, length above
                           ; #XHCI_BOUNCE_BYTES
#XHCI_ERR_SPEED      = 31  ; the port reports a speed this file has no
                           ; max-packet size for
#XHCI_ERR_SHORT      = 32  ; the device returned fewer bytes than the
                           ; descriptor's own bLength claims

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; FOUR CODES WERE DUPLICATES AND THE ERROR TEXT LIED. Fixed 2026-08-28.
;
; The endpoint and slot-count codes were added in a separate block above
; the port codes and were numbered from 18, which the port block had
; already used:
;
;     #XHCI_ERR_TOO_MANY_DEVS = 18   collided with #XHCI_ERR_NOT_READY
;     #XHCI_ERR_EP_BUSY       = 19   collided with #XHCI_ERR_PORTNUM
;     #XHCI_ERR_EP_ARG        = 20   collided with #XHCI_ERR_NO_CONNECT
;     #XHCI_ERR_NO_EPS        = 21   collided with #XHCI_ERR_PORT_POWER
;
; A duplicate constant is not itself fatal - the code is only ever
; compared against itself - but XhciErrorText() is a Select over those
; values, the FIRST matching Case wins, and the port cases come first.
; So XhciConfigureBulk() refusing a second claim on the bulk ring pair
; printed "xhci: port number outside 1..MaxPorts", and a bulk transfer
; attempted before Configure Endpoint printed "xhci: port power never
; came up". Both of those send somebody to look at a cable.
;
; This is the exact failure the error-code block's own header says it
; exists to prevent - "it returned 0 sends somebody to look at the cable
; when the answer was in a status bit" - reintroduced by appending a new
; block instead of extending the last number. THE RULE, from here on:
; ONE ascending run, new codes at the END, and the end is right here.
;
; Renumbering is safe because nothing outside this file names the
; numbers: callers compare XhciLastError() against the #XHCI_ERR_*
; names, and the gate's expectation table is regenerated from them.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#XHCI_ERR_TOO_MANY_DEVS = 33  ; the controller handed out a slot id
                              ; past #XHCI_MAX_DEVS. Refused rather
                              ; than wrapped onto another slot ring
#XHCI_ERR_EP_BUSY    = 34  ; the bulk ring pair already belongs to a
                           ; different slot
#XHCI_ERR_EP_ARG     = 35  ; an endpoint address that is not one bulk
                           ; IN and one bulk OUT
#XHCI_ERR_NO_EPS     = 36  ; a bulk transfer before the endpoints were
                           ; configured
#XHCI_ERR_NOT_HUB    = 37  ; a hub call against a device whose device
                           ; descriptor does not say class 09
#XHCI_ERR_HUB_PORT   = 38  ; hub port number outside 1..bNbrPorts
#XHCI_ERR_HUB_DESC   = 39  ; the hub descriptor is too short, or names
                           ; more ports than this file will walk
#XHCI_ERR_ROUTE      = 40  ; a route string this file will not build -
                           ; a hub port above 15, or a depth past one
                           ; tier
#XHCI_ERR_TT         = 41  ; a low- or full-speed device was addressed
                           ; behind something that is not a high-speed
                           ; hub, so there is no transaction translator
                           ; to name
#XHCI_ERR_EP_HALTED  = 42  ; a transfer was attempted on an endpoint the
                           ; controller has HALTED, or one timed out and
                           ; the endpoint was found halted afterwards.
                           ; This is the error a bare timeout USED to be
                           ; reported as, and the two are worth telling
                           ; apart: a timeout says "the device did not
                           ; answer", this says "nothing was ever sent".
#XHCI_ERR_UNHALT     = 43  ; Reset Endpoint and Set TR Dequeue Pointer
                           ; both ran and the endpoint is STILL halted.
                           ; The pipe cannot be recovered and every
                           ; transfer after this one would time out; the
                           ; device needs re-addressing.

; ======================================================================
;  STATE
; ======================================================================
Global xh_err.i      = #XHCI_ERR_NONE
Global xh_ready.i    = 0     ; XhciInit() completed
Global xh_hz.i       = 0     ; CNTFRQ_EL0, cached

Global xh_bus.i      = 1     ; where the controller was found
Global xh_dev.i      = 0
Global xh_fn.i       = 0
Global xh_id.i       = 0     ; (device << 16) | vendor, AS READ
Global xh_barBus.i   = 0     ; BAR0, PCI-bus address, low half
Global xh_barHigh.i  = 0     ; BAR0's high half if it is a 64-bit BAR
Global xh_cap.i      = 0     ; CPU address of the capability block
Global xh_op.i       = 0     ; CPU address of the operational block
Global xh_db.i       = 0     ; CPU address of the doorbell array
Global xh_rt.i       = 0     ; CPU address of the runtime block
Global xh_ir.i       = 0     ; CPU address of interrupter 0

Global xh_caplen.i   = 0
Global xh_version.i  = 0     ; HCIVERSION, e.g. $0100 for xHCI 1.0
Global xh_hcs1.i     = 0
Global xh_hcs2.i     = 0
Global xh_hcs3.i     = 0
Global xh_hcc1.i     = 0
Global xh_maxSlots.i = 0
Global xh_maxIntrs.i = 0
Global xh_maxPorts.i = 0
Global xh_erstMax.i  = 0
Global xh_scratch.i  = 0     ; scratchpad buffers the controller wants
Global xh_ctxSize.i  = 32    ; 32 or 64, from HCCPARAMS1 bit 2
Global xh_ac64.i     = 0     ; HCCPARAMS1 bit 0 - 64-bit addressing
Global xh_pageSize.i = 0     ; decoded from PAGESIZE, in bytes

Global xh_lastSts.i  = 0     ; USBSTS as it was at the last failure
Global xh_lastCmd.i  = 0     ; USBCMD ditto
Global xh_lastComp.i = 0     ; completion code of the last command or
                             ; transfer event
Global xh_lastSlot.i = 0     ; slot id from the last completion event
Global xh_lastResid.i = 0    ; residual byte count from the last
                             ; transfer event
; The completion code of the LAST Reset Endpoint or Set TR Dequeue
; Pointer issued by xh_ClearHalt. Kept apart from xh_lastComp because
; the recovery runs INSIDE a failing transfer and must not overwrite
; what that transfer's caller is about to read. 0 means no recovery has
; been attempted since the last one that was reported.
Global xh_haltComp.i = 0
Global xh_stale.i    = 0     ; right event type, wrong transfer
Global xh_skipped.i  = 0     ; events discarded while waiting for the
                             ; expected type - a diagnostic, not an
                             ; error

; The arena, and the bump pointer over it.
Global Dim xh_arena.a[#XHCI_ARENA_BYTES]
Global xh_base.i = 0     ; the aligned base
Global xh_brk.i  = 0     ; next free byte
Global xh_end.i  = 0     ; one past the last usable byte

; The allocations.
Global xh_dcbaa.i   = 0
Global xh_erst.i    = 0
Global xh_spArray.i = 0
Global xh_spPages.i = 0
Global xh_outCtx.i  = 0      ; slot 0 is never used; kept so the name
                             ; still resolves where it means "current"
; Four per-slot tables, indexed 1..#XHCI_MAX_DEVS. The literal 9 is
; #XHCI_MAX_DEVS + 1 written out, because a Dim bound here takes a bare
; literal for the same reason a constant initialiser does. KEEP THE TWO
; IN STEP - the sizes below and #XHCI_MAX_DEVS above.
Global Dim xh_devCtx.i[9]    ; output context per slot
Global Dim xh_devMps.i[9]    ; endpoint-0 max packet, 0 = never addressed
; Recorded at Address Device time so that a caller switching between
; slots with XhciUseSlot() can ask what it is talking to without keeping
; a parallel table of its own. hid.pi4 needs the speed of the device it
; is polling and the route it was reached by, and re-deriving either
; from the output context means knowing the context size and the field
; layout at a second call site.
Global Dim xh_devSpeed.i[9]
Global Dim xh_devRoute.i[9]
Global xh_mscSlot.i = 0      ; which slot owns the bulk ring pair
Global xh_epOut.i   = 0      ; endpoint address, e.g. $01
Global xh_epIn.i    = 0      ; endpoint address, e.g. $82
Global xh_dciOut.i  = 0      ; doorbell/context index for each
Global xh_dciIn.i   = 0
Global xh_inCtx.i   = 0
Global xh_bounce.i  = 0
Global xh_setup.i   = 0

; Producer rings - command and endpoint 0.
Global Dim xh_ringBase.i[#XHCI_RING_N]
Global Dim xh_ringEnq.i[#XHCI_RING_N]    ; index of the next free TRB
Global Dim xh_ringCycle.i[#XHCI_RING_N]  ; the Producer Cycle State

; The event ring - consumer side, no link TRB.
Global xh_evBase.i  = 0
Global xh_evDeq.i   = 0
Global xh_evCycle.i = 1

; The last event's four fields, captured before it is acknowledged.
Global xh_evF0.i = 0
Global xh_evF1.i = 0
Global xh_evF2.i = 0
Global xh_evF3.i = 0

; Address materialisation for the cache maintenance blocks. A global,
; not a parameter, for the reason display.pi4 gives: the inline block
; has to name a symbol it can adrp, and a parameter's home is a
; compiler-chosen slot this file may not name.
Global xh_cacheaddr.i = 0

; ======================================================================
;  WHICH PART OF THE BRING-UP IS RUNNING - added 2026-09-11.
;
;  WHY A DRIVER CARRIES THIS AT ALL. Every wait in this file is bounded,
;  so every FAILURE here is a refusal with a code and a sentence. A board
;  that stops inside XhciInit() with no sentence is therefore not a
;  failure this file can describe - it is the machine wedging on an
;  access, and the only thing that can name where is a marker set BEFORE
;  the access rather than a code returned after it.
;
;  That is what it is for. The boot log's one line for this step -
;  "USB host (xHCI) ....." - covers seven distinct pieces of work, three
;  of which touch a bus that can stall: the config-space walk, the first
;  read of the controller's own registers through the outbound window,
;  and the reset. Without a marker, all seven look identical from the
;  panel: a label with no result.
;
;  THE HOOK TAKES NO ARGUMENT, and that is deliberate. It is the shape
;  genet.pi4's progress hook already has and the one this tree has run on
;  silicon; the phase is read back through XhciPhase() by whoever
;  installed it. A hook is only installed by a caller that wants the
;  progress printed, so nothing is printed on the lazy path after boot.
;
;  THE MARKER IS FILE-SCOPE AND NOT A LOCAL. Automatic storage lives in
;  the invocation's frame as of 2026-09-11 and is gone when the call
;  returns; a marker whose whole job is to survive the call that set it -
;  and to be readable by a hook running underneath it - has to live on a
;  layer that outlives the frame. Same rule, same reason, as the cache
;  walk's bounds in dma.pi4.
; ======================================================================
#XHCI_PH_IDLE    = 0
#XHCI_PH_CLOCK   = 1     ; CNTFRQ_EL0, before anything that can wait
#XHCI_PH_ARENA   = 2     ; the arena's base, and the 3 GiB reachability test
#XHCI_PH_LOCATE  = 3     ; PCI config: the id, bus master, BAR0
#XHCI_PH_CAPS    = 4     ; the FIRST read through the outbound window
#XHCI_PH_RESET   = 5     ; halt, HCRST, Controller Not Ready
#XHCI_PH_CONFIG  = 6     ; the enabled device slot count
#XHCI_PH_MEMORY  = 7     ; rings, contexts, ERST, scratchpad
#XHCI_PH_RUN     = 8     ; CMD_RUN and the wait for HCHalted to clear
#XHCI_PH_DONE    = 9
#XHCI_PH_ADOPT_CAPS = 10
#XHCI_PH_ADOPT_READY = 11
#XHCI_PH_ADOPT_HALT = 12
#XHCI_PH_ADOPT_DMA = 13

Global xh_phaseAt.i = #XHCI_PH_IDLE
Global *xh_phaseHook = 0

Procedure.i XhciPhase() : ProcedureReturn xh_phaseAt : EndProcedure

; The word for a phase. Present tense, because it is printed as the work
; starts and it is what the operator is looking at when it stops.
Procedure.i XhciPhaseText(p.i)
  Select p
    Case #XHCI_PH_CLOCK  : ProcedureReturn "clock"
    Case #XHCI_PH_ARENA  : ProcedureReturn "arena"
    Case #XHCI_PH_LOCATE : ProcedureReturn "config"
    Case #XHCI_PH_CAPS   : ProcedureReturn "registers"
    Case #XHCI_PH_RESET  : ProcedureReturn "reset"
    Case #XHCI_PH_CONFIG : ProcedureReturn "slots"
    Case #XHCI_PH_MEMORY : ProcedureReturn "rings"
    Case #XHCI_PH_RUN    : ProcedureReturn "run"
    Case #XHCI_PH_DONE   : ProcedureReturn "ok"
    Case #XHCI_PH_ADOPT_CAPS : ProcedureReturn "firmware registers"
    Case #XHCI_PH_ADOPT_READY : ProcedureReturn "firmware readiness"
    Case #XHCI_PH_ADOPT_HALT : ProcedureReturn "firmware halt"
    Case #XHCI_PH_ADOPT_DMA : ProcedureReturn "firmware DMA ownership"
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn "idle"
EndProcedure

; Install a hook, get the old one back. Zero removes it.
Procedure.i XhciSetPhaseHook(*fn)
  Define old.i
  old = *xh_phaseHook
  *xh_phaseHook = *fn
  ProcedureReturn old
EndProcedure

Procedure xh_Phase(p.i)
  xh_phaseAt = p
  If *xh_phaseHook <> 0
    xh_phaseHook()
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; Optional retained diagnostics, deliberately separate from the printing hook.
; Callback must not print, allocate, poll hardware, or service the display.
Global xh_traceAt.i
Global *xh_traceHook
Procedure.i XhciTrace() : ProcedureReturn xh_traceAt : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciSetTraceHook(*fn)
  Define old.i
  old = *xh_traceHook
  *xh_traceHook = *fn
  ProcedureReturn old
EndProcedure
Procedure xh_Trace(p.i)
  xh_traceAt = p
  If *xh_traceHook <> 0
    xh_traceHook()
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE CLOCK. Two lines of assembly and no dependency on any other
;  file, exactly as emmc.pi4 does it and for the same reason:
;  CNTPCT_EL0 and CNTFRQ_EL0 are readable with no trap and no setup at
;  the exception level a loader leaves us in, so a driver that has to
;  bound every wait can do it without owing anything to another file.
;  A spin count would have had to be calibrated against a CPU frequency
;  the firmware is free to change underneath us.
; ======================================================================

Procedure.i xh_Ticks()
  ASM
    mrs x0, cntpct_el0
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure.i xh_TickHz()
  ASM
    mrs x0, cntfrq_el0
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; A deadline, in ticks, `ms` milliseconds from now. Returns 0 if the
; clock is unusable, which every caller treats as "do not wait".
Procedure.i xh_Deadline(ms.i)
  If xh_hz <= 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn xh_Ticks() + ((xh_hz / 1000) * ms)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i xh_Expired(deadline.i)
  If deadline = 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  If xh_Ticks() >= deadline
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; Spin for approximately n milliseconds. Used only where a settling
; delay is genuinely required - port power - and never as a substitute
; for polling a status bit.
Procedure xh_DelayMs(ms.i)
  Define d.i
  d = xh_Deadline(ms)
  If d = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  While xh_Expired(d) = 0
  Wend
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  CACHE MAINTENANCE. See CACHE in the header for why these two
;  operations and not the other two.
; ======================================================================

; Clean one line to the Point of Coherency - the CPU wrote it, the
; controller is about to read it.
Procedure xh_CleanLine()
  ASM
    adrp x9, global_xh_cacheaddr
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_xh_cacheaddr
    ldr  x9, [x9]
    dc cvac, x9
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; Clean AND invalidate one line to the Point of Coherency - the
; controller wrote it, the CPU is about to read it.
Procedure xh_InvalLine()
  ASM
    adrp x9, global_xh_cacheaddr
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_xh_cacheaddr
    ldr  x9, [x9]
    dc civac, x9
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; Round the start down to a line and the end up to a line, exactly as
; xhci-mem.c's xhci_flush_cache() does. Shift pair rather than a mask,
; because a mask literal is 64-bit here and the shift pair cannot be
; got wrong by a missing top half.
Procedure xh_Clean(addr.i, len.i)
  Define a.i
  Define last.i
  If len <= 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  last = addr + len - 1
  a = (addr >> 6) << 6
  While a <= last
    xh_cacheaddr = a
    xh_CleanLine()
    a = a + #XHCI_CACHELINE
  Wend
  a64_barrier()
EndProcedure

Procedure xh_Inval(addr.i, len.i)
  Define a.i
  Define last.i
  If len <= 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  last = addr + len - 1
  a = (addr >> 6) << 6
  While a <= last
    xh_cacheaddr = a
    xh_InvalLine()
    a = a + #XHCI_CACHELINE
  Wend
  a64_barrier()
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  REGISTER ACCESS. Every read and every write in this file goes
;  through one of these four, so the width question is settled in one
;  place instead of at every call site.
;
;  PeekN and PokeN - THIRTY-TWO BITS, UNSIGNED. Not PeekL, which
;  sign-extends (ruled 2026-08-25) and would make USBSTS bit 11 and
;  PORTSC bit 31 negative; not PeekI or PeekX, which are eight bytes
;  and would take the neighbouring register with them.
; ======================================================================

Procedure.i xh_Rd(base.i, off.i)
  Define v.i
  v = PeekN(base + off)
  ; xhci_readl() is an ordered readl(), not a raw load.
  a64_barrier()
  ProcedureReturn v
EndProcedure

Procedure xh_Wr(base.i, off.i, v.i)
  ; xhci_writel() is an ordered writel(). Keep DMA descriptors and prior
  ; controller state ordered before the command/doorbell write, and finish
  ; the device write before returning to a dependent operation.
  a64_barrier()
  PokeN(base + off, v & $FFFFFFFF)
  a64_barrier()
EndProcedure

; A 64-bit register, read as two 32-bit halves. xhci.h, xhci_readq().
Procedure.i xh_Rd64(base.i, off.i)
  Define lo.i
  Define hi.i
  lo = PeekN(base + off)
  a64_barrier()
  hi = PeekN(base + off + 4)
  a64_barrier()
  ProcedureReturn lo | (hi << 32)
EndProcedure

; A 64-bit register, written LOW HALF FIRST. xhci.h, xhci_writeq():
;   "Registers with 64-bit address pointers should be written to with
;    dword accesses by writing the low dword first (ptr[0]), then the
;    high dword (ptr[1]) second."
; The order is a hardware requirement, not a style choice, and this is
; the only place in the file that writes a wide register.
Procedure xh_Wr64(base.i, off.i, v.i)
  a64_barrier()
  PokeN(base + off,     v & $FFFFFFFF)
  a64_barrier()
  PokeN(base + off + 4, (v >> 32) & $FFFFFFFF)
  a64_barrier()
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  FAILURE BOOKKEEPING. One place, so every refusal captures the same
;  two words. A report naming only the error code sends somebody to
;  look at the cable when the answer was in USBSTS.
;
;  The register capture is guarded: before xh_op is known there is no
;  operational block to read, and reading one from address 0 is not a
;  diagnostic, it is a second fault.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i xh_Fail(code.i)
  xh_err = code
  If xh_op <> 0
    xh_lastSts = xh_Rd(xh_op, #XHCI_USBSTS)
    xh_lastCmd = xh_Rd(xh_op, #XHCI_USBCMD)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE ARENA. Bump allocation, aligned, zeroed, and it REFUSES rather
;  than wrapping. There is no free.
;
;  Zeroing matters and is not belt-and-braces: xhci.h quotes the
;  specification's section 4.11.1 in xhci_segment_alloc()'s comment -
;  "All components of all Command and Transfer TRBs shall be
;  initialized to '0'". The compiler's BSS zero loop already does it
;  once at boot, and this does it again so that a second XhciInit()
;  after a failure starts from the same state as the first.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i xh_Alloc(bytes.i, align.i)
  Define p.i
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  If bytes <= 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  p = (xh_brk + (align - 1)) / align
  p = p * align
  If (p + bytes) > xh_end
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  xh_brk = p + bytes
  ; Zero it, eight bytes at a time. bytes is always a multiple of 8
  ; here by construction - every caller passes a size that is - and the
  ; tail loop below picks up anything that is not.
  n = bytes / 8
  i = 0
  While i < n
    PokeX(p + (i * 8), 0)
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  i = n * 8
  While i < bytes
    PokeA(p + i, 0)
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn p
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  STEP 1 - FIND THE CONTROLLER AND MAP IT
; ======================================================================
; BAR discovery and register decoding happen here. Their ordering comes from
; v2025.01_xhci-pci.c's xhci_pci_init():
;
;   hccr = dm_pci_map_bar(dev, PCI_BASE_ADDRESS_0, ...)
;   hcor = (uintptr_t)hccr + HC_LENGTH(xhci_readl(&hccr->cr_capbase))
;   dm_pci_read_config32(dev, PCI_COMMAND, &cmd)
;   cmd |= PCI_COMMAND_MASTER
;   dm_pci_write_config32(dev, PCI_COMMAND, cmd)
;
; BUS MASTER IS NOT OPTIONAL. Without it the controller is not
; permitted to initiate reads, so every ring this file builds is
; invisible to it - and, again, the CPU-side view is perfect. It is
; deliberately left clear while ownership and BAR routing change.
; XhciInitAt() enables it immediately after xh_ReadCaps(), matching the
; boundary in U-Boot: BAR mapped and CAPLENGTH read, then MASTER set,
; then the generic xHCI halt/reset path begins.
;
; MEMORY SPACE is enabled alongside it. U-Boot's PCI core has usually
; done that already by the time xhci_pci_init() runs, which is why the
; function above only sets MASTER; nothing in this project's PCIe
; bring-up promises the same, so both bits are set here and the
; register is read back.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i xh_Locate()
  Define id.i
  Define bar.i
  Define cmd.i
  Define cpu.i

  id = PcieCfgRead32(xh_bus, xh_dev, xh_fn, $00)
  xh_id = id
  ; $FFFFFFFF is PCI's own "nobody home". Zero is not a legal vendor id
  ; either, and a zero here usually means the config window itself is
  ; not working rather than that the device is absent - both are worth
  ; the same refusal and the caller has XhciDeviceId() to tell them
  ; apart.
  If id = $FFFFFFFF Or id = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_NO_DEVICE)
  EndIf

  ; Register decode only; PcieEnumerate left bus mastering disabled.
  ; Mask the status half so RW1C events are not accidentally cleared.
  cmd = PcieCfgRead32(xh_bus, xh_dev, xh_fn, $04)
  cmd = (cmd & $FFFF) | $0002    ; DMA stays off until new rings are installed
  PcieCfgWrite32(xh_bus, xh_dev, xh_fn, $04, cmd)

  bar = PcieCfgRead32(xh_bus, xh_dev, xh_fn, $10)   ; BAR0
  xh_barBus = bar
  ; Architectural PCI BAR decoding. Bit 0 set is an I/O BAR; bits 2:1 =
  ; 10b is a 64-bit memory BAR whose high half lives in BAR1.
  If (bar & $0001) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_BAR_IO)
  EndIf
  If (bar & $0006) = $0004
    xh_barHigh = PcieCfgRead32(xh_bus, xh_dev, xh_fn, $14)
    If xh_barHigh <> 0
      ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_BAR_HIGH)
    EndIf
  EndIf
  bar = bar & $FFFFFFF0
  If bar = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_BAR_ZERO)
  EndIf

  ; The BAR holds a PCI-BUS address. PcieCpuFromBus() refuses anything
  ; outside the outbound window rather than returning a plausible
  ; number, and a negative return is one of its #PCIE_ERR_* codes.
  cpu = PcieCpuFromBus(bar)
  If cpu <= 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_BAR_RANGE)
  EndIf
  xh_cap = cpu
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  STEP 1 (continued) - THE CAPABILITY REGISTERS
; ======================================================================
; xhci.h gives every field:
;   HC_LENGTH(p)      XHCI_HC_LENGTH(p)  = (p >> 0) & 0xff
;   HC_VERSION(p)     ((p) >> 16) & 0xffff
;   HCS_MAX_SLOTS(p)  ((p) >> 0) & 0xff
;   HCS_MAX_INTRS(p)  ((p) >> 8) & 0x7ff
;   HCS_MAX_PORTS(p)  ((p) >> 24) & 0xff
;   HCS_ERST_MAX(p)   ((p) >> 4) & 0xf
;   HCS_MAX_SCRATCHPAD(p)  ((((p) >> 16) & 0x3e0) | (((p) >> 27) & 0x1f))
;   HCC_64BIT_ADDR(p)      ((p) & (1 << 0))
;   HCC_64BYTE_CONTEXT(p)  ((p) & (1 << 2))
;   CTX_SIZE(_hcc)         (HCC_64BYTE_CONTEXT(_hcc) ? 64 : 32)
;   DBOFF_MASK   (~0x3)
;   RTSOFF_MASK  (~0x1f)
;
; HCS_MAX_SCRATCHPAD is the one worth reading twice: it is TWO fields,
; bits 25:21 and bits 31:27, and the macro reassembles them. Getting
; that wrong gives a scratchpad count that is plausibly small, which is
; the kind of wrong that boots and then corrupts memory.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i xh_ReadCaps()
  Define v.i

  v = xh_Rd(xh_cap, #XHCI_CAPLENGTH)
  xh_caplen  = v & $FF
  xh_version = (v >> 16) & $FFFF
  ; A CAPLENGTH of 0 means the operational registers would overlap the
  ; capability registers; $FF means the read did not reach a device.
  ; The specification's own minimum is $20 (the size of the capability
  ; block itself) and no real controller is anywhere near $FF.
  If xh_caplen < $20 Or xh_caplen >= $FF
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_CAPLENGTH)
  EndIf
  ; xHCI 0.96 through 1.2. Outside that this is not an xHCI controller,
  ; or the read is not reaching one.
  If xh_version < $0090 Or xh_version > $0120
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_HCIVERSION)
  EndIf

  xh_op = xh_cap + xh_caplen

  xh_hcs1 = xh_Rd(xh_cap, #XHCI_HCSPARAMS1)
  xh_hcs2 = xh_Rd(xh_cap, #XHCI_HCSPARAMS2)
  xh_hcs3 = xh_Rd(xh_cap, #XHCI_HCSPARAMS3)
  xh_hcc1 = xh_Rd(xh_cap, #XHCI_HCCPARAMS1)

  xh_maxSlots = xh_hcs1 & $FF
  xh_maxIntrs = (xh_hcs1 >> 8) & $7FF
  xh_maxPorts = (xh_hcs1 >> 24) & $FF
  If xh_maxSlots = 0 Or xh_maxPorts = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_HCSPARAMS)
  EndIf
  ; A controller cannot have more slots than the architecture defines.
  If xh_maxSlots > #XHCI_MAX_SLOTS
    xh_maxSlots = #XHCI_MAX_SLOTS
  EndIf

  xh_erstMax  = (xh_hcs2 >> 4) & $0F
  xh_scratch  = ((xh_hcs2 >> 16) & $3E0) | ((xh_hcs2 >> 27) & $1F)

  xh_ac64 = xh_hcc1 & $01
  If (xh_hcc1 & $04) <> 0
    xh_ctxSize = 64
  Else
    xh_ctxSize = 32
  EndIf

  xh_db = xh_cap + (xh_Rd(xh_cap, #XHCI_DBOFF)  & #XHCI_DBOFF_MASK)
  xh_rt = xh_cap + (xh_Rd(xh_cap, #XHCI_RTSOFF) & #XHCI_RTSOFF_MASK)
  xh_ir = xh_rt + #XHCI_IR0

  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  STEP 3 - HALT, THEN RESET
; ======================================================================
; xhci.c, xhci_reset(), in this exact order:
;
;   state = xhci_readl(&hcor->or_usbsts) & STS_HALT;
;   if (!state) { cmd = readl(usbcmd); cmd &= ~CMD_RUN; writel(cmd); }
;   handshake(usbsts, STS_HALT, STS_HALT, XHCI_MAX_HALT_USEC)
;   cmd = readl(usbcmd); cmd |= CMD_RESET; writel(cmd);
;   handshake(usbcmd, CMD_RESET, 0, XHCI_MAX_RESET_USEC)
;   return handshake(usbsts, STS_CNR, 0, XHCI_MAX_RESET_USEC)
;
; THE CNR WAIT IS THE ONE THAT GETS SKIPPED, and xhci.c says exactly
; why it must not be:
;
;   "xHCI cannot write to any doorbells or operational registers other
;    than status until the 'Controller Not Ready' flag is cleared."
;
; Skip it and the DCBAAP, CRCR and CONFIG writes that follow land in a
; controller that is not accepting them. Every one of those writes
; succeeds from the CPU's point of view. The result is a controller
; that resets cleanly and then does nothing - which is why the gate for
; this file deliberately removes the CNR wait and watches the test go
; red.
;
; The halt is skipped if the controller is ALREADY halted, which it
; will be after a power-on reset. The read-modify-write of USBCMD
; preserves the reserved bits 4:6 and 12:31, as xhci.h requires.
; ======================================================================
; xHCI 1.2 section 4.2: after hardware reset, incoming CNR must clear
; before any operational/runtime write. The post-HCRST wait alone is not
; sufficient when taking over a controller still completing its cold reset.
; https://cdrdv2-public.intel.com/625472/625472_xHCI_Rev1_2b.pdf
Procedure.i xh_WaitReady()
  Define d.i
  d = xh_Deadline(#XHCI_TMO_RESET_MS)
  While (xh_Rd(xh_op, #XHCI_USBSTS) & #XHCI_STS_CNR) <> 0
    If xh_Expired(d) <> 0
      ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_CNR)
    EndIf
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i xh_Halt()
  Define cmd.i
  Define d.i

  If xh_WaitReady() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  If (xh_Rd(xh_op, #XHCI_USBSTS) & #XHCI_STS_HALT) = 0
    cmd = xh_Rd(xh_op, #XHCI_USBCMD)
    cmd = cmd & (~#XHCI_CMD_RUN)
    xh_Wr(xh_op, #XHCI_USBCMD, cmd)
  EndIf

  d = xh_Deadline(#XHCI_TMO_HALT_MS)
  While (xh_Rd(xh_op, #XHCI_USBSTS) & #XHCI_STS_HALT) = 0
    If xh_Expired(d) <> 0
      ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_HALT)
    EndIf
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; Take over only the already validated firmware BAR. Do not alter live
; xh_* register pointers/rings: this phase is separate from XhciInit.
#XHCI_ERR_TAKEOVER = 45
Procedure.i XhciQuiesceAdopted()
  Define cpu.i
  Define cap.i
  Define op.i
  Define hz.i
  Define started.i
  Define cmd.i
  If PcieNeedsTakeover() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  cpu = PcieAdoptCpu()
  If cpu = 0
    xh_err = #XHCI_ERR_TAKEOVER
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  hz = xh_TickHz()
  If hz < 1000000 Or hz > 1000000000
    xh_err = #XHCI_ERR_NO_CNTFRQ
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  xh_Phase(#XHCI_PH_ADOPT_CAPS)
  cap = xh_Rd(cpu, 0)
  If (cap & $FF) < 32 Or (cap & 3) <> 0 Or ((cap >> 16) & $FFFF) < $0090 Or ((cap >> 16) & $FFFF) > $0120
    xh_err = #XHCI_ERR_BAR_RANGE
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  op = cpu + (cap & $FF)
  xh_Phase(#XHCI_PH_ADOPT_READY)
  started = xh_Ticks()
  While (xh_Rd(op, #XHCI_USBSTS) & #XHCI_STS_CNR) <> 0
    If xh_Ticks() - started >= (hz / 1000) * #XHCI_TMO_RESET_MS
      xh_err = #XHCI_ERR_CNR
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
  Wend
  xh_Phase(#XHCI_PH_ADOPT_HALT)
  If (xh_Rd(op, #XHCI_USBSTS) & #XHCI_STS_HALT) = 0
    cmd = xh_Rd(op, #XHCI_USBCMD)
    xh_Wr(op, #XHCI_USBCMD, cmd & (~#XHCI_CMD_RUN))
  EndIf
  started = xh_Ticks()
  While (xh_Rd(op, #XHCI_USBSTS) & #XHCI_STS_HALT) = 0
    If xh_Ticks() - started >= (hz / 1000) * #XHCI_TMO_HALT_MS
      xh_err = #XHCI_ERR_HALT
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
  Wend
  xh_Phase(#XHCI_PH_ADOPT_DMA)
  If PcieAdoptHalted() = 0
    xh_err = #XHCI_ERR_TAKEOVER
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i xh_Reset()
  Define cmd.i
  Define d.i

  xh_Trace(1)
  If xh_Halt() = 0
    xh_Trace(2)
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  xh_Trace(3)

  xh_Trace(4)
  cmd = xh_Rd(xh_op, #XHCI_USBCMD)
  xh_Trace(5)
  cmd = cmd | #XHCI_CMD_RESET
  xh_Trace(6)
  xh_Wr(xh_op, #XHCI_USBCMD, cmd)
  xh_Trace(7)

  ; HCRST is self-clearing. xhci.c waits XHCI_MAX_RESET_USEC for it.
  d = xh_Deadline(#XHCI_TMO_RESET_MS)
  xh_Trace(8)
  cmd = xh_Rd(xh_op, #XHCI_USBCMD)
  xh_Trace(9)
  While (cmd & #XHCI_CMD_RESET) <> 0
    If xh_Expired(d) <> 0
      xh_Trace(10)
      ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_RESET)
    EndIf
    cmd = xh_Rd(xh_op, #XHCI_USBCMD)
  Wend

  ; And THEN Controller Not Ready. See the comment above; this is the
  ; wait whose absence produces a silent, plausible-looking failure.
  d = xh_Deadline(#XHCI_TMO_RESET_MS)
  xh_Trace(11)
  cmd = xh_Rd(xh_op, #XHCI_USBSTS)
  xh_Trace(12)
  While (cmd & #XHCI_STS_CNR) <> 0
    If xh_Expired(d) <> 0
      xh_Trace(13)
      ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_CNR)
    EndIf
    cmd = xh_Rd(xh_op, #XHCI_USBSTS)
  Wend

  xh_Trace(14)
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  RINGS
; ======================================================================
; A producer ring is one 1024-byte segment of 64 TRBs whose LAST TRB is
; a Link TRB pointing back at the segment's own start, with the Toggle
; Cycle bit set. xhci-mem.c, xhci_link_segments() and xhci_ring_alloc():
;
;   prev->trbs[TRBS_PER_SEGMENT-1].link.segment_ptr = next->dma
;   val &= ~TRB_TYPE_BITMASK; val |= TRB_TYPE(TRB_LINK)
;   ... and for a single-segment ring, next IS first_seg:
;   prev->trbs[TRBS_PER_SEGMENT-1].link.control |= LINK_TOGGLE
;
; and xhci_initialize_ring_info():
;
;   "The ring is initialized to 0. The producer must write 1 to the
;    cycle bit to handover ownership of the TRB to the host controller,
;    so PCS = 1."
;
; THE RING'S DMA ADDRESS IS ITS CPU ADDRESS on this board. Inbound DMA
; is identity-mapped below 3 GiB - pcie.pi4's PcieDmaOk() comment cites
; bcm2711.dtsi's dma-ranges for exactly that - so no translation is
; applied here and none should be. PcieBusFromCpu() is the OUTBOUND
; window's translation and applying it to a ring would be wrong.
; ======================================================================

Procedure.i xh_TrbAddr(r.i, idx.i)
  ProcedureReturn xh_ringBase[r] + (idx * #XHCI_TRB_BYTES)
EndProcedure

; Write the Link TRB at the end of a producer ring and set the ring's
; state to empty. The Link TRB's own cycle bit starts CLEAR, which is
; correct: with PCS = 1 the controller must not own it yet.
Procedure xh_RingReset(r.i)
  Define lnk.i
  Define base.i

  base = xh_ringBase[r]
  lnk  = base + (#XHCI_LINK_INDEX * #XHCI_TRB_BYTES)

  ; segment_ptr - 64 bits, low half then high half, same as every other
  ; wide field. The segment points at itself: one segment.
  PokeN(lnk + 0,  base & $FFFFFFFF)
  PokeN(lnk + 4,  (base >> 32) & $FFFFFFFF)
  PokeN(lnk + 8,  0)                     ; intr_target
  ; control: TRB_TYPE(TRB_LINK) | LINK_TOGGLE, cycle clear.
  PokeN(lnk + 12, ((#XHCI_TRB_LINK << 10) | #XHCI_LINK_TOGGLE) & $FFFFFFFF)

  xh_ringEnq[r]   = 0
  xh_ringCycle[r] = 1
  xh_Clean(base, #XHCI_SEGMENT_SIZE)
EndProcedure

; Queue one TRB. `f3` must NOT carry the cycle bit - this supplies it -
; and it must NOT carry the TRB type shifted; callers pass the shifted
; type in f3 because several TRBs need other bits in the same word.
;
; Returns the CPU address the TRB was written at, so a caller that has
; to match a completion event against the command it issued can.
;
; The four fields are written with PokeN. THIRTY-TWO BITS EACH. A .i is
; 64 bits here and one careless PokeI would write field 0 and field 1
; together, putting a 64-bit value where the controller expects two
; independent words - and on a Setup Stage TRB, field 1 is wIndex and
; wLength.
;
; `cyc` IS AN EXPLICIT CYCLE BIT AND NOT ALWAYS THE RING'S. The first
; TRB of a control transfer is written with the ring's cycle INVERTED,
; so that the controller cannot own it until the rest of the transfer
; descriptor exists - see XhciControlIn(). Writing the right value the
; first time is the point: an earlier version of this procedure wrote
; the correct cycle and then XORed it back out one instruction later,
; and the gate's asynchronous-controller model caught the window in
; between. That window is exactly the race xhci-ring.c's
; giveback_first_trb() exists to avoid, reintroduced by the fix for it.
Procedure.i xh_QueueTrbCy(r.i, f0.i, f1.i, f2.i, f3.i, cyc.i)
  Define at.i
  Define lnk.i
  Define ctl.i

  at = xh_TrbAddr(r, xh_ringEnq[r])
  PokeN(at + 0,  f0 & $FFFFFFFF)
  PokeN(at + 4,  f1 & $FFFFFFFF)
  PokeN(at + 8,  f2 & $FFFFFFFF)
  PokeN(at + 12, (f3 | (cyc & 1)) & $FFFFFFFF)
  xh_Clean(at, #XHCI_TRB_BYTES)

  xh_ringEnq[r] = xh_ringEnq[r] + 1

  ; Reached the Link TRB. xhci-ring.c, inc_enq(): give the Link TRB to
  ; the controller by setting its cycle bit to the CURRENT producer
  ; cycle, then - because LINK_TOGGLE is set on it - toggle the
  ; producer cycle and wrap to the top of the segment.
  If xh_ringEnq[r] >= #XHCI_LINK_INDEX
    lnk = xh_TrbAddr(r, #XHCI_LINK_INDEX)
    ctl = PeekN(lnk + 12)
    ctl = (ctl & (~#XHCI_TRB_CYCLE)) | xh_ringCycle[r]
    PokeN(lnk + 12, ctl & $FFFFFFFF)
    xh_Clean(lnk, #XHCI_TRB_BYTES)
    xh_ringCycle[r] = xh_ringCycle[r] ! 1
    xh_ringEnq[r] = 0
  EndIf

  ProcedureReturn at
EndProcedure

; The ordinary case: hand the TRB to the controller immediately.
Procedure.i xh_QueueTrb(r.i, f0.i, f1.i, f2.i, f3.i)
  ProcedureReturn xh_QueueTrbCy(r, f0, f1, f2, f3, xh_ringCycle[r])
EndProcedure

; Make sure `n` TRBs will fit before the Link TRB, so that a transfer
; descriptor is CONTIGUOUS and the first TRB of it is still the first
; TRB when the last one has been written.
;
; xhci-ring.c, prepare_ring(), and inc_enq()'s comment says why the
; Link TRB is handed over here rather than when it was reached:
;
;   "If the caller doesn't plan on enqueueing more TDs before ringing
;    the doorbell, then we don't want to give the link TRB to the
;    hardware just yet. We'll give the link TRB back in prepare_ring()
;    just before we enqueue the TD at the top of the ring."
;
; ======================================================================
;  THIS PROCEDURE DEADLOCKED THE RING. Fixed 2026-08-28. Read this.
; ======================================================================
;  WHAT IT USED TO DO: when a transfer descriptor would not fit before
;  the Link TRB, it handed the Link TRB to the controller, toggled the
;  producer cycle and set the enqueue index straight to 0 - SKIPPING the
;  TRBs between the old enqueue index and the Link TRB without writing
;  them.
;
;  THAT IS A DEADLOCK, and the arithmetic is short enough to check here.
;  Say the enqueue index is 62 and a three-TRB control transfer arrives.
;  The consumer's dequeue pointer is also at 62, because every transfer
;  in this file is synchronous and the ring is idle. The old code:
;
;    * handed over the Link TRB at index 63 with the producer cycle;
;    * toggled the producer cycle;
;    * wrote the transfer at indices 0, 1, 2 with the NEW cycle.
;
;  The controller then looks at index 62, WHICH NOBODY WROTE THIS LAP.
;  It holds the previous lap's TRB, whose cycle bit is the opposite of
;  what the controller now expects, or zeros on the first lap. So the
;  controller does not own it, stops there, and never reaches the Link
;  TRB one slot further on. The transfer at index 0 is never seen. Every
;  transfer after that times out.
;
;  WHY IT HAD NEVER FIRED, and this is the part worth remembering: the
;  ring holds 63 usable TRBs, and every transfer this file made until
;  now used a count that divides 63. A control transfer WITH DATA is
;  three TRBs and 63 is 21 threes; a bulk transfer is one. The enqueue
;  index therefore landed exactly on 63 every lap, xh_QueueTrbCy's own
;  wrap handled it, and the branch below was never taken.
;
;  IT FIRES THE MOMENT ENDPOINT ZERO CARRIES A ZERO-LENGTH TRANSFER.
;  SET_CONFIGURATION, SET_PROTOCOL, SET_IDLE and every hub port feature
;  request have no Data Stage, so they queue TWO TRBs. Mix twos and
;  threes and the enqueue index stops landing on 63. hid.pi4 issues
;  dozens of them, and the gate for it - tools/a64/a64_hid_check.py -
;  hit the deadlock on its first run, at a ClearPortFeature(C_RESET)
;  after the fourth hub port was reset. The model reported "an EP0
;  doorbell produced no complete control transfer", which is precisely
;  what the silicon would have done, silently, as a hang.
;
;  THE COMMENT THAT USED TO BE HERE CLAIMED THIS WAS SAFE, and said so
;  on the strength of a measurement: a64_xhci_check.py's HARMLESS table
;  records that DELETING the call left thirty control transfers working
;  over two laps of the ring. That measurement was real and it proved
;  nothing, because with only three-TRB transfers the branch never ran
;  in either version. A mutation that removes code which never executes
;  is measured harmless for the wrong reason. That is the transferable
;  lesson and it is why this paragraph is longer than the fix.
;
;  THE FIX: PAD THE TAIL WITH TRANSFER-RING NO-OP TRBs so the consumer
;  can WALK to the Link TRB instead of being asked to jump. xhci.h:918,
;  TRB_TR_NOOP, value 8, commented "Transfer Ring No-op (not for the
;  command ring)". A No-Op TRB with no IOC bit is consumed and produces
;  no event, so nothing downstream has to know they happened.
;
;  THE REJECTED ALTERNATIVE was to delete the call and let a transfer
;  descriptor STRADDLE the Link TRB. It is legal - U-Boot does it, and
;  xhci-ring.c's inc_enq() hands the Link TRB over mid-descriptor
;  whenever more_trbs_coming is set, which for a control transfer it is.
;  It was rejected because the deferred-cycle trick in XhciControlIn
;  interacts with it: the Setup TRB is written with the wrong cycle and
;  corrected at the end, and reasoning about that across a segment
;  boundary and a producer-cycle toggle is exactly the kind of thing
;  that is right until it is not. Padding costs at most 62 TRB writes
;  per lap of the ring, once every twenty-odd transfers, against a
;  device that answers in milliseconds.
;
;  The straddle is not merely asserted to be worse - it is a MUTATION in
;  a64_hid_check.py ("straddle-the-link"), so whether it also works is
;  measured rather than argued, this time on a workload that actually
;  reaches the branch.
;
;  Padding is only safe because every transfer in this file is
;  synchronous: the ring is idle when this is called, so the No-Ops are
;  written ahead of a dequeue pointer that is not moving.
; ======================================================================
Procedure xh_RingRoom(r.i, n.i)
  Define pad.i

  If (xh_ringEnq[r] + n) <= #XHCI_LINK_INDEX
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  ; How many slots are left before the Link TRB. The count is taken
  ; BEFORE the loop, not tested inside it, because xh_QueueTrb resets
  ; the enqueue index to 0 when it reaches the Link TRB - so a
  ; `While xh_ringEnq[r] < #XHCI_LINK_INDEX` loop would see 0 and never
  ; end. That is not a hypothetical; it was the first version.
  pad = #XHCI_LINK_INDEX - xh_ringEnq[r]
  While pad > 0
    ; No interrupt-on-completion, no chain, no data. Just a TRB the
    ; controller owns and steps over.
    xh_QueueTrb(r, 0, 0, 0, (#XHCI_TRB_TR_NOOP << 10))
    pad = pad - 1
  Wend
EndProcedure

; Ring a doorbell. xhci.h:
;   DB_VALUE(ep, stream)  ((((ep) + 1) & 0xff) | ((stream) << 16))
;   DB_VALUE_HOST         0x00000000
; The command ring is doorbell 0 with value 0; a device slot's endpoint
; 0 is doorbell <slot> with value 1.
;
; The barrier is not decoration. The doorbell write must not be
; observed before the TRB writes that it announces, and the TRB writes
; only reached memory because xh_Clean() cleaned them - which ends in a
; dsb of its own. This second one orders the doorbell after that.
Procedure xh_Doorbell(slot.i, value.i)
  a64_barrier()
  xh_Wr(xh_db, slot * 4, value)
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  STEP 3 (continued) - THE DATA STRUCTURES
; ======================================================================
; xhci-mem.c, xhci_mem_init(), in this order:
;   DCBAA -> or_dcbaap
;   command ring -> or_crcr, preserving CMD_RING_RSVD_BITS and ORing
;                   in the ring's cycle state
;   dba  = hccr + (cr_dboff  & DBOFF_MASK)
;   run  = hccr + (cr_rtsoff & RTSOFF_MASK)
;   event ring, ERST entries, flush
;   ir_set->erst_dequeue = deq & ~ERST_PTR_MASK
;   ir_set->erst_size    = (old & ERST_SIZE_MASK) | ERST_NUM_SEGS
;   ir_set->erst_base    = (old & ERST_PTR_MASK) | (erst & ~ERST_PTR_MASK)
;   scratchpad
;   or_dnctrl = 0
;
; Two of those read-modify-writes look pointless and are not. ERSTSZ's
; top half is reserved-preserve; ERSTBA's low four bits are reserved.
; Writing either as a flat value is the kind of thing that works on one
; controller and not on the next.
;
; ERDP IS WRITTEN BEFORE ERSTBA, deliberately, because that is the
; order xhci_mem_init() uses. The xHCI specification's initialisation
; sequence sets the dequeue pointer before the segment table base so
; that the controller cannot start consuming from a stale dequeue the
; instant the table becomes valid.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i xh_MemInit()
  Define v.i
  Define i.i
  Define page.i
  Define sp.i

  ; ---- the DCBAA. xhci.h: 64-bit entries, one per slot, entry 0 is
  ; the scratchpad array pointer. 64-byte aligned.
  xh_dcbaa = xh_Alloc(#XHCI_DCBAA_BYTES, #XHCI_ALIGN)
  If xh_dcbaa = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_ARENA)
  EndIf

  ; ---- the command ring.
  xh_ringBase[#XHCI_RING_CMD] = xh_Alloc(#XHCI_SEGMENT_SIZE, #XHCI_ALIGN)
  If xh_ringBase[#XHCI_RING_CMD] = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_ARENA)
  EndIf
  xh_RingReset(#XHCI_RING_CMD)

  ; ---- an endpoint-0 transfer ring AND an output device context PER
  ; SLOT. Allocated now rather than at Address Device time, because this
  ; file has no allocator that can give memory back and a re-addressed
  ; slot would leak its predecessor.
  ;
  ; Ring index IS the slot number, so this loop starts at 1. Slot 0 is
  ; not a slot - the DCBAA's entry 0 belongs to the scratchpad array.
  Define d.i
  d = 1
  While d <= #XHCI_MAX_DEVS
    xh_ringBase[d] = xh_Alloc(#XHCI_SEGMENT_SIZE, #XHCI_ALIGN)
    If xh_ringBase[d] = 0
      ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_ARENA)
    EndIf
    xh_RingReset(d)
    xh_devCtx[d] = xh_Alloc(#XHCI_DEV_CTX_BYTES, #XHCI_ALIGN)
    If xh_devCtx[d] = 0
      ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_ARENA)
    EndIf
    xh_devMps[d] = 0
    d = d + 1
  Wend

  ; The bulk pair, unowned until XhciConfigureBulk claims it.
  xh_ringBase[#XHCI_RING_BULK_OUT] = xh_Alloc(#XHCI_SEGMENT_SIZE, #XHCI_ALIGN)
  xh_ringBase[#XHCI_RING_BULK_IN]  = xh_Alloc(#XHCI_SEGMENT_SIZE, #XHCI_ALIGN)
  If xh_ringBase[#XHCI_RING_BULK_OUT] = 0 Or xh_ringBase[#XHCI_RING_BULK_IN] = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_ARENA)
  EndIf
  xh_RingReset(#XHCI_RING_BULK_OUT)
  xh_RingReset(#XHCI_RING_BULK_IN)
  xh_mscSlot = 0

  ; ---- the event ring. NO LINK TRB: xhci-ring.c's last_trb() treats
  ; the event ring as a plain array and wraps on the segment boundary,
  ; and xhci_mem_init() passes link_trbs = false for it.
  xh_evBase = xh_Alloc(#XHCI_SEGMENT_SIZE, #XHCI_ALIGN)
  If xh_evBase = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_ARENA)
  EndIf
  xh_evDeq   = 0
  xh_evCycle = 1
  xh_Clean(xh_evBase, #XHCI_SEGMENT_SIZE)

  ; ---- the ERST. One entry: { seg_addr:64, seg_size:32, rsvd:32 }.
  xh_erst = xh_Alloc(#XHCI_ERST_ENTRY_SIZE, #XHCI_ALIGN)
  If xh_erst = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_ARENA)
  EndIf
  PokeN(xh_erst + 0, xh_evBase & $FFFFFFFF)
  PokeN(xh_erst + 4, (xh_evBase >> 32) & $FFFFFFFF)
  PokeN(xh_erst + 8, #XHCI_TRBS_PER_SEG)
  PokeN(xh_erst + 12, 0)
  xh_Clean(xh_erst, #XHCI_ERST_ENTRY_SIZE)

  ; ---- the device contexts. One slot's worth; this file addresses one
  ; device.
  ; The single shared output context is gone - see the note at
  ; #XHCI_MAX_DEVS. xh_outCtx now simply tracks the CURRENT slot's
  ; context so the accessors below keep reading the same name.
  xh_outCtx = 0
  xh_inCtx  = xh_Alloc(#XHCI_IN_CTX_BYTES,  #XHCI_ALIGN)
  xh_bounce = xh_Alloc(#XHCI_BOUNCE_BYTES,  #XHCI_ALIGN)
  xh_setup  = xh_Alloc(#XHCI_SETUP_BYTES,   #XHCI_ALIGN)
  If xh_inCtx = 0 Or xh_bounce = 0 Or xh_setup = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_ARENA)
  EndIf

  ; ---- DCBAAP. Written before the command ring, as xhci_mem_init()
  ; does. This is a plain 64-bit pointer with no flag bits.
  xh_Wr64(xh_op, #XHCI_DCBAAP, xh_dcbaa)

  ; ---- CRCR. Read-modify-write preserving CMD_RING_RSVD_BITS, then OR
  ; in the ring's cycle state, exactly as xhci_mem_init() does:
  ;   val_64 = (val_64 & CMD_RING_RSVD_BITS)
  ;          | (trb_64 & ~CMD_RING_RSVD_BITS)
  ;          | ctrl->cmd_ring->cycle_state;
  v = xh_Rd64(xh_op, #XHCI_CRCR)
  v = (v & #XHCI_CRCR_RSVD_BITS) | (xh_ringBase[#XHCI_RING_CMD] & (~#XHCI_CRCR_RSVD_BITS)) | xh_ringCycle[#XHCI_RING_CMD]
  xh_Wr64(xh_op, #XHCI_CRCR, v)

  ; ---- the interrupter. ERDP first, then ERSTSZ, then ERSTBA.
  xh_Wr64(xh_ir, #XHCI_ERDP, xh_evBase & (~#XHCI_ERST_PTR_MASK))

  v = xh_Rd(xh_ir, #XHCI_ERSTSZ)
  v = (v & #XHCI_ERSTSZ_MASK) | #XHCI_ERST_NUM_SEGS
  xh_Wr(xh_ir, #XHCI_ERSTSZ, v)

  v = xh_Rd64(xh_ir, #XHCI_ERSTBA)
  v = (v & #XHCI_ERST_PTR_MASK) | (xh_erst & (~#XHCI_ERST_PTR_MASK))
  xh_Wr64(xh_ir, #XHCI_ERSTBA, v)

  ; ---- PAGESIZE. Decoded ALWAYS, not only when a scratchpad is
  ; needed, because it is one of the operational registers a caller is
  ; entitled to ask about and XhciPageSize() would otherwise lie by
  ; returning zero on a controller that wants no scratchpad.
  ;
  ; Bit n set means the controller supports 2^(n+12) byte pages.
  ; xhci-mem.c, xhci_scratchpad_alloc(), takes the LOWEST set bit:
  ;   for (i = 0; i < 16; i++) { if (0x1 & page_size) break;
  ;                              page_size >>= 1; }
  ;   ctrl->page_size = 1 << (i + 12);
  page = xh_Rd(xh_op, #XHCI_PAGESIZE) & $FFFF
  i = 0
  While i < 16
    If (page & 1) <> 0
      Break
    EndIf
    page = page >> 1
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  If i >= 16
    ; No bit set at all. U-Boot BUG_ON()s here; a register with no
    ; supported page size is not a working controller.
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_PAGESIZE)
  EndIf
  xh_pageSize = 1 << (i + 12)

  ; ---- the scratchpad. xhci-mem.c, xhci_scratchpad_alloc(): if
  ; HCS_MAX_SCRATCHPAD is zero there is nothing to do at all, and the
  ; DCBAA's entry 0 stays zero.
  If xh_scratch > 0
    If xh_scratch > #XHCI_MAX_SCRATCH
      ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_SCRATCH)
    EndIf
    ; This file provides 4 KiB pages and nothing else. A controller
    ; that insists on larger pages gets a refusal rather than pages
    ; that are the wrong size, which it would write past the end of.
    ; The refusal is HERE and not above, because a page size this file
    ; cannot provide only matters if the controller actually wants
    ; scratchpad buffers.
    If xh_pageSize <> #XHCI_PAGE_BYTES
      ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_PAGESIZE)
    EndIf

    xh_spArray = xh_Alloc(#XHCI_SCRATCH_ARRAY_BYTES, #XHCI_ALIGN)
    xh_spPages = xh_Alloc(xh_scratch * #XHCI_PAGE_BYTES, #XHCI_PAGE_BYTES)
    If xh_spArray = 0 Or xh_spPages = 0
      ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_ARENA)
    EndIf
    i = 0
    While i < xh_scratch
      sp = xh_spPages + (i * #XHCI_PAGE_BYTES)
      PokeX(xh_spArray + (i * 8), sp)
      i = i + 1
    Wend
    xh_Clean(xh_spArray, #XHCI_SCRATCH_ARRAY_BYTES)
    xh_Clean(xh_spPages, xh_scratch * #XHCI_PAGE_BYTES)
    ; DCBAA entry 0 IS the scratchpad array pointer.
    PokeX(xh_dcbaa + 0, xh_spArray)
  EndIf
  xh_Clean(xh_dcbaa, #XHCI_DCBAA_BYTES)

  ; ---- DNCTRL. xhci_mem_init()'s own comment: "Just Zero'ing this
  ; register completely, or some spurious Device Notification Events
  ; might screw things here."
  xh_Wr(xh_op, #XHCI_DNCTRL, 0)

  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  STEP 3 (continued) - CONFIG AND RUN
; ======================================================================
; xhci.c, xhci_lowlevel_init():
;   val  = hcsparams1 & HCS_SLOTS_MASK
;   val2 = or_config
;   val |= (val2 & ~HCS_SLOTS_MASK)
;   writel(or_config, val)
;   ... xhci_mem_init() ...
;   xhci_start(hcor)
;   writel(ir_set->irq_control, 0)
;   writel(ir_set->irq_pending, 0)
;
; and xhci_start():
;   temp = readl(usbcmd); temp |= CMD_RUN; writel(usbcmd, temp)
;   handshake(usbsts, STS_HALT, 0, XHCI_MAX_HALT_USEC)
;
; CONFIG is written BEFORE the memory init in U-Boot and that ordering
; is kept here. The interrupter registers are zeroed AFTER the run,
; also as U-Boot does; this file never enables an interrupt, so those
; two writes are the whole of its interrupt policy.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i xh_Start()
  Define cmd.i
  Define d.i

  cmd = xh_Rd(xh_op, #XHCI_USBCMD)
  cmd = cmd | #XHCI_CMD_RUN
  xh_Wr(xh_op, #XHCI_USBCMD, cmd)

  d = xh_Deadline(#XHCI_TMO_HALT_MS)
  While (xh_Rd(xh_op, #XHCI_USBSTS) & #XHCI_STS_HALT) <> 0
    If xh_Expired(d) <> 0
      ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_RUN)
    EndIf
  Wend

  ; Interrupt moderation and pending, both zero. No interrupt is ever
  ; enabled by this file - it polls - so IMAN's Interrupt Enable bit is
  ; left clear on purpose.
  xh_Wr(xh_ir, #XHCI_IMOD, 0)
  xh_Wr(xh_ir, #XHCI_IMAN, 0)

  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE EVENT RING - consumer side
; ======================================================================
; xhci-ring.c, event_ready():
;   xhci_inval_cache(dequeue, sizeof(union xhci_trb))
;   if ((le32_to_cpu(event->event_cmd.flags) & TRB_CYCLE) !=
;        ctrl->event_ring->cycle_state) return 0;
;
; THE INVALIDATE IS THE WHOLE MECHANISM. The controller writes the
; event TRB by DMA; without invalidating the line the CPU reads a stale
; cache line forever and every wait times out. It is the mirror of the
; clean on the producer side and it is just as easy to leave out.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i xh_EventReady()
  Define at.i
  Define ctl.i
  at = xh_evBase + (xh_evDeq * #XHCI_TRB_BYTES)
  xh_Inval(at, #XHCI_TRB_BYTES)
  ctl = PeekN(at + 12)
  If (ctl & #XHCI_TRB_CYCLE) <> xh_evCycle
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; Advance past the event at the dequeue pointer and tell the
; controller. xhci-ring.c, inc_deq() plus xhci_acknowledge_event():
;   ring->dequeue++  (wrapping and toggling the cycle at the end of the
;                     segment, because the event ring has no link TRB)
;   xhci_writeq(&ir_set->erst_dequeue, deq | ERST_EHB)
;
; ERST_EHB is Event Handler Busy and writing a 1 CLEARS it. It is
; write-1-to-clear, which is why it is ORed into every dequeue update
; rather than being carefully preserved.
Procedure xh_AckEvent()
  Define at.i
  xh_evDeq = xh_evDeq + 1
  If xh_evDeq >= #XHCI_TRBS_PER_SEG
    xh_evDeq = 0
    xh_evCycle = xh_evCycle ! 1
  EndIf
  at = xh_evBase + (xh_evDeq * #XHCI_TRB_BYTES)
  xh_Wr64(xh_ir, #XHCI_ERDP, at | #XHCI_ERST_EHB)
EndProcedure

; Wait for an event of a given TRB type, discarding any other. The four
; fields of the matching event are left in xh_evF0..xh_evF3 and the
; event IS acknowledged before this returns, so the caller must read
; them from the globals and not from the ring.
;
; xhci-ring.c's xhci_wait_for_event() BUG_ON()s on an unexpected Port
; Status Change event. This does not: it counts them in xh_skipped and
; carries on, because a port status change arriving while a command is
; in flight is normal on a live bus and killing the machine over it is
; not a defensible response in a library.
Procedure.i xh_WaitEventFor(expect.i, wantTrb.i, ms.i)
  Define at.i
  Define d.i
  Define ty.i

  xh_skipped = 0
  xh_stale = 0
  d = xh_Deadline(ms)
  While xh_Expired(d) = 0
    If xh_EventReady() <> 0
      at = xh_evBase + (xh_evDeq * #XHCI_TRB_BYTES)
      xh_evF0 = PeekN(at + 0)
      xh_evF1 = PeekN(at + 4)
      xh_evF2 = PeekN(at + 8)
      xh_evF3 = PeekN(at + 12)
      ; xhci.h: TRB_FIELD_TO_TYPE(p) is ((p & 0xfc00) >> 10)
      ty = (xh_evF3 & $FC00) >> 10
      ; xhci.h: GET_COMP_CODE(p) is ((p & (0xff << 24)) >> 24), and the
      ; completion code lives in field 2 of both a command completion
      ; and a transfer event.
      xh_lastComp  = (xh_evF2 >> 24) & $FF
      ; xhci.h: TRB_TO_SLOT_ID(p) is ((p & (0xff << 24)) >> 24) on the
      ; FLAGS word.
      xh_lastSlot  = (xh_evF3 >> 24) & $FF
      ; xhci.h: EVENT_TRB_LEN(p) is (p & 0xffffff) - the bytes NOT
      ; transferred, for a transfer event.
      xh_lastResid = xh_evF2 & $FFFFFF
      xh_AckEvent()
      If ty = expect
        ; MATCH THE EVENT TO THE TRB THAT CAUSED IT, when the caller
        ; knows which one that is.
        ;
        ; A transfer event's first two fields are the ADDRESS of the TRB
        ; that generated it. Accepting any event of the right TYPE looks
        ; correct and is not: the event ring is shared by every endpoint
        ; on every slot, so a leftover event from an earlier transfer is
        ; indistinguishable from this one's.
        ;
        ; That is not hypothetical. Bulk-Only Transport does three
        ; exchanges per command, and one stale event left over from the
        ; control transfers put the whole conversation ONE REPLY APART:
        ; the command wrapper consumed the stale event, the status
        ; wrapper consumed the command wrapper's, and the status bytes
        ; were read before the device had written them. Every transfer
        ; reported success and the buffer was always zero. It only
        ; worked at all when something happened to issue one throwaway
        ; command first, which is a horrible thing to depend on.
        ;
        ; wantTrb 0 means "any", for callers that genuinely cannot say.
        If wantTrb = 0
          ProcedureReturn 1
        EndIf
        If ((xh_evF1 << 32) | (xh_evF0 & $FFFFFFFF)) = wantTrb
          ProcedureReturn 1
        EndIf
        ; The right kind of event about the wrong transfer. Drop it and
        ; keep waiting - it belongs to something already finished.
        xh_stale = xh_stale + 1
      Else
        xh_skipped = xh_skipped + 1
      EndIf
    EndIf
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_EVT_TIMEOUT)
EndProcedure

; The original shape, for callers that do not track their TRB address.
; Accepts any event of the right type - see the warning above about what
; that costs when the event ring has traffic from more than one source.
Procedure.i xh_WaitEvent(expect.i, ms.i)
  ProcedureReturn xh_WaitEventFor(expect, 0, ms)
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  COMMANDS
; ======================================================================
; xhci-ring.c, xhci_queue_command():
;   fields[0] = lower_32_bits(addr)
;   fields[1] = upper_32_bits(addr)
;   fields[2] = 0
;   fields[3] = TRB_TYPE(cmd) | SLOT_ID_FOR_TRB(slot_id) | cycle_state
;   queue_trb(...)
;   xhci_writel(&ctrl->dba->doorbell[0], DB_VALUE_HOST)
;
; SLOT_ID_FOR_TRB(p) is ((p & 0xff) << 24).
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i xh_Command(cmd.i, addr.i, slot.i, ms.i)
  Define f3.i
  f3 = (cmd << 10) | ((slot & $FF) << 24)
  xh_QueueTrb(#XHCI_RING_CMD, addr & $FFFFFFFF, (addr >> 32) & $FFFFFFFF, 0, f3)
  xh_Doorbell(0, 0)                      ; DB_VALUE_HOST
  If xh_WaitEvent(#XHCI_TRB_COMPLETION, ms) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If xh_lastComp <> #XHCI_COMP_SUCCESS
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_CMD_FAILED)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; xh_CommandEp - a command TRB that names an ENDPOINT as well as a slot.
;
; Reset Endpoint and Set TR Dequeue Pointer both need one, and xh_Command
; cannot carry it because it writes zero into that field. Two procedures
; rather than one with a mostly-unused argument: every existing caller
; of xh_Command is a slot-only command and would have had to grow a 0.
;
; rpi-6.12.y_xhci.h:823 - EP_INDEX_FOR_TRB(p) is (((p) + 1) & 0x1f) << 16,
; and Linux's ep_index is the DCI minus one, so the field written here is
; simply the DCI. The off-by-one is real and it is worth stating in both
; directions: endpoint 0 is DCI 1 is ep_index 0, and the TRB carries 1.
;
; f0/f1 are the command's 64-bit parameter, already split, because the
; only caller that has one has to OR a cycle bit into its low half.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i xh_CommandEp(cmd.i, f0.i, f1.i, slot.i, dci.i, ms.i)
  Define f3.i
  f3 = (cmd << 10) | ((dci & $1F) << 16) | ((slot & $FF) << 24)
  xh_QueueTrb(#XHCI_RING_CMD, f0 & $FFFFFFFF, f1 & $FFFFFFFF, 0, f3)
  xh_Doorbell(0, 0)                      ; DB_VALUE_HOST
  If xh_WaitEvent(#XHCI_TRB_COMPLETION, ms) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If xh_lastComp <> #XHCI_COMP_SUCCESS
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_CMD_FAILED)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; xh_EpStateAt - the controller's own view of one endpoint on one slot.
;
; The low three bits of word 0 of the OUTPUT endpoint context. This is
; what the CONTROLLER believes, not what was asked for, which is the
; only reason it is worth reading: a Configure Endpoint that reported
; success and left the endpoint DISABLED is a command that was accepted
; and did not take.
;
; XhciEpState() is the public version and answers only for the CURRENT
; slot. This one takes the slot explicitly because xh_ClearHalt is
; called from the bulk path, where the current slot and the slot that
; owns the endpoints are the same today and need not always be.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i xh_EpStateAt(slot.i, dci.i)
  Define ep.i
  If slot < 1 Or slot > #XHCI_MAX_DEVS Or dci < 1 Or dci > 31
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If xh_devCtx[slot] = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  xh_Inval(xh_devCtx[slot], #XHCI_DEV_CTX_BYTES)
  ep = xh_devCtx[slot] + (xh_ctxSize * dci)
  ProcedureReturn PeekN(ep + 0) & $07
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  xh_ClearHalt - PUT A HALTED ENDPOINT BACK TO WORK.
; ======================================================================
;  THIS IS THE MISSING HALF OF STALL HANDLING, and leaving it out cost
;  an entire investigation. Added 2026-08-28.
;
;  WHAT WAS WRONG. xh_Control saw a Stall completion, reported
;  #XHCI_ERR_STALL, and returned. That is a correct report of one
;  transfer and a silent corruption of every transfer after it. xHCI
;  1.1 section 4.8.3 moves a stalled endpoint to the HALTED state, and
;  a halted endpoint does not fail the next transfer - it does not RUN
;  it. The doorbell is accepted, the ring is never walked, no event is
;  ever posted, and the caller times out. rpi-6.12.y_xhci_ring.c:2345
;  says it in one sentence:
;
;    "xhci internal endpoint state will go to a 'halt' state for any
;     stall, including default control pipe protocol stall. To clear the
;     host side halt we need to issue a reset endpoint command, followed
;     by a set dequeue command to move past the TD."
;
;  and rpi-6.12.y_xhci.c:3199-3201 states the ordering from the other
;  end: "The host side of the halt should already be cleared by a reset
;  endpoint command issued when the STALL event was received."
;
;  MEASURED ON SILICON, 2026-08-28, and this is the whole reason the file
;  changed. A wireless keyboard-and-mouse receiver on hub port 1 of the
;  VL805's internal hub:
;
;    interface 0, keyboard   GET_REPORT returned 8 bytes in 2 ms
;    interface 1, mouse      SET_IDLE stalled - which is LEGAL, HID 1.11
;                            Appendix G makes Set_Idle optional for
;                            non-keyboards - and endpoint 0 went to
;                            state 2, HALTED
;    interface 1, mouse      GET_REPORT: no event, 5000 ms
;    interface 0, keyboard   GET_REPORT AGAIN: no event, 5000 ms
;
;  The keyboard had answered two milliseconds earlier on that same pipe.
;  Endpoint zero belongs to the DEVICE, not to an interface, so one
;  optional request that one interface is allowed to refuse took down
;  every interface on the device - and it did it as three five-second
;  timeouts, which is the whole of a BCM2711 watchdog budget
;  (safety.pi4:434, ~15.999 s). The board rebooted mid-measurement with
;  nothing printed, and that was read as "enumeration is too slow".
;  Enumeration was 445 ms.
;
;  ONLY THE HOST SIDE IS CLEARED HERE, and that is not an omission. USB
;  2.0 section 8.5.3.4: a control pipe's protocol stall "is cleared by
;  the next Setup transaction" - the DEVICE side of a control-pipe
;  stall unhalts itself. A FUNCTIONAL stall on a bulk or interrupt
;  endpoint does need CLEAR_FEATURE(ENDPOINT_HALT) as well, which is
;  what Linux means by "class drivers clear the device side halt from a
;  functional stall later" in the same comment. This file's only halted
;  endpoints today are control ones.
;
;  WHERE THE DEQUEUE POINTER IS PUT, AND WHY NOT THE TOP OF THE RING.
;  The obvious move - reset the ring and point the controller at its
;  base - IS WRONG, and it is wrong in a way that would have looked
;  fine. A transfer ring is not empty because its index is zero; it is
;  empty because the dequeue pointer equals the enqueue pointer, and
;  ownership is carried by the cycle bit. The TRBs already written
;  BELOW the enqueue point still carry the producer's cycle from when
;  they were queued, so a controller aimed at the base with that same
;  cycle would find them owned and RE-EXECUTE the whole dead transfer -
;  a Setup Stage TRB from a request that already failed, replayed onto
;  a device that has moved on.
;
;  So the pointer goes to the CURRENT ENQUEUE POSITION with the CURRENT
;  producer cycle. The TRB sitting there has the previous, inverted,
;  cycle bit, which is exactly the "not yours yet" the controller needs
;  to see to stop. That is also what Linux means by "move past the TD"
;  in the comment above; it points just past the failed descriptor
;  because it can have several outstanding, while this file issues one
;  transfer at a time and waits, so past-the-TD and the enqueue point
;  are the same place.
;
;  The Set TR Dequeue Pointer command has to be issued either way -
;  after a Reset Endpoint the controller's dequeue pointer is undefined,
;  and skipping it leaves the endpoint running against a pointer into
;  the middle of a dead TD.
;
;  Returns 1 if the endpoint is usable again, 0 with the error set if it
;  is not. A failure here is worth reporting loudly: it means the pipe
;  is gone and the device needs re-addressing.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i xh_ClearHalt(slot.i, ring.i, dci.i)
  Define deq.i
  Define st.i
  Define savedComp.i
  Define savedResid.i
  Define savedSlot.i
  Define ok.i

  If slot < 1 Or slot > #XHCI_MAX_DEVS
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_BAD_SLOT)
  EndIf
  If ring < 0 Or ring >= #XHCI_RING_N Or dci < 1 Or dci > 31
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_ARG)
  EndIf

  ; THE CALLER'S COMPLETION CODE IS NOT OURS TO OVERWRITE. Two command
  ; completions are about to land in xh_lastComp, xh_lastResid and
  ; xh_lastSlot, and the caller is in the middle of reporting a
  ; TRANSFER. XhciLastComp() answering 1 - the Reset Endpoint's Success
  ; - to somebody asking why their transfer failed is the exact shape of
  ; wrong answer this project refuses. The recovery's own completion
  ; codes are kept in xh_haltComp, which XhciLastUnhaltComp() reports,
  ; so a recovery that fails still says what the controller said.
  savedComp  = xh_lastComp
  savedResid = xh_lastResid
  savedSlot  = xh_lastSlot
  xh_haltComp = 0

  ok = 1

  ; 1. Reset Endpoint. No parameter - rpi-6.12.y_xhci_ring.c:4590-4602
  ;    queues it with all three data words zero.
  If xh_CommandEp(#XHCI_TRB_RESET_EP, 0, 0, slot, dci, #XHCI_TMO_EVENT_MS) = 0
    xh_haltComp = xh_lastComp
    xh_lastComp  = savedComp
    xh_lastResid = savedResid
    xh_lastSlot  = savedSlot
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; 2. Set TR Dequeue Pointer, at the enqueue position - see above for
  ;    why not at the base. rpi-6.12.y_xhci_ring.c:758-763: the low half
  ;    of the address carries the CYCLE BIT (`new_cycle`), the high half
  ;    is the rest of the address, and the third word holds the stream
  ;    id - zero here, because no endpoint in this file uses streams.
  deq = xh_TrbAddr(ring, xh_ringEnq[ring]) | xh_ringCycle[ring]
  If xh_CommandEp(#XHCI_TRB_SET_DEQ, deq & $FFFFFFFF, (deq >> 32) & $FFFFFFFF, slot, dci, #XHCI_TMO_EVENT_MS) = 0
    ok = 0
  EndIf

  ; 3. ASK THE CONTROLLER, do not assume. Both commands can report
  ;    success and leave the endpoint halted if the arguments named the
  ;    wrong endpoint - and that failure is invisible until the next
  ;    transfer times out, which is exactly the shape being fixed.
  If ok <> 0
    st = xh_EpStateAt(slot, dci)
    If st = #XHCI_EP_STATE_HALTED Or st < 0
      xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_UNHALT)
      ok = 0
    EndIf
  EndIf

  xh_haltComp  = xh_lastComp
  xh_lastComp  = savedComp
  xh_lastResid = savedResid
  xh_lastSlot  = savedSlot
  ProcedureReturn ok
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  PUBLIC - INITIALISATION
; ======================================================================

; XhciInitAt - bring the controller at a named bus/device/function up.
; Use this if XhciInit()'s 1/0/0 turns out to be wrong on a board.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  xh_FailStop - a refusal must leave the hardware STOPPED.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ADDED 2026-08-25 after the board reset itself, twice, SECONDS AFTER a
;  program had finished and returned cleanly to the monitor.
;
;  XhciInit had six failure returns and not one of them halted the
;  controller. By the time the scratchpad check refuses, the controller
;  has already been reset and is being handed pointers into our arena.
;  Returning 0 there reports the failure honestly to the caller and
;  leaves a DMA master pointed at memory the caller is about to reuse.
;
;  The symptom is the worst kind: nothing goes wrong while the program
;  runs. It prints its refusal, returns, the monitor prints its prompt -
;  and a second or two later the machine resets, with the evidence
;  belonging to a program that has already exited. It took two
;  occurrences and an idle-board control run (40 seconds, silent, no
;  reset) to rule out a watchdog and attribute it here.
;
;  This is recurring bug shape #80 - a teardown that cannot complete,
;  abandoned halfway - seen from the other end: an INITIALISATION
;  abandoned halfway, with no teardown at all.
;
;  Deliberately tolerant: it is called on paths where xh_op may be zero
;  because the controller was never mapped, and XhciStop already returns
;  immediately in that case.
Procedure xh_FailStop()
  XhciStop()
EndProcedure

#XHCI_ERR_DMA_ENABLE = 44

Procedure.i xh_EnableDma()
  If PcieEnableDma() = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_DMA_ENABLE)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i XhciInitAt(bus.i, dev.i, fn.i)
  Define maxs.i
  Define v.i

  ; This Pi4 ownership path manages the soldered VL805 only. Refuse
  ; unsupported BDF before changing driver state or touching hardware.
  If bus <> 1 Or dev <> 0 Or fn <> 0
    xh_err = #XHCI_ERR_TAKEOVER
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  xh_err   = #XHCI_ERR_NONE
  xh_ready = 0
  xh_bus = bus
  xh_dev = dev
  xh_fn  = fn
  ; Clear the mapped addresses first, so that a failure part-way
  ; through cannot leave a stale operational base that xh_Fail() would
  ; then read from.
  xh_cap = 0
  xh_op  = 0
  xh_db  = 0
  xh_rt  = 0
  xh_ir  = 0

  ; The clock, before anything that can wait. A lying clock makes every
  ; bounded wait in this file unbounded again, so nothing is attempted
  ; without it. 1 MHz is the floor: the Pi 4's generic timer runs at
  ; 54 MHz and a plausible ARM system timer is never below 1 MHz, so a
  ; value under that is a register that is not answering.
  xh_Phase(#XHCI_PH_CLOCK)
  xh_hz = xh_TickHz()
  If xh_hz < 1000000 Or xh_hz > 1000000000
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_NO_CNTFRQ)
  EndIf

  ; The arena. Aligned at run time, because the compiler's BSS is only
  ; ".align 16" and the order of globals within it is not this file's
  ; to assume.
  xh_Phase(#XHCI_PH_ARENA)
  xh_base = (@xh_arena[0] + (#XHCI_ALIGN - 1)) / #XHCI_ALIGN
  xh_base = xh_base * #XHCI_ALIGN
  xh_end  = @xh_arena[0] + #XHCI_ARENA_BYTES
  xh_brk  = xh_base

  ; THE 3 GiB WALL. One test, over the whole arena, before a single
  ; address is handed to the controller. See the header.
  If PcieDmaOk(xh_base, xh_end - xh_base) = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_DMA_RANGE)
  EndIf

  xh_Phase(#XHCI_PH_LOCATE)
  If xh_Locate() = 0
    xh_FailStop()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ; THE FIRST ACCESS THROUGH THE OUTBOUND WINDOW is the next line, and it
  ; is the one access in this whole file that can wedge the machine rather
  ; than answer: a read with no completion does not abort here, it stalls.
  ; That is why the marker is set BEFORE it.
  xh_Phase(#XHCI_PH_CAPS)
  If xh_ReadCaps() = 0
    xh_FailStop()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  xh_Phase(#XHCI_PH_RESET)
  ; Match U-Boot's xhci_pci_init() ordering exactly: after mapping BAR0
  ; and reading CAPLENGTH, PCI_COMMAND_MASTER is enabled before
  ; xhci_register() enters the generic halt/reset path.  The previous
  ; ordering enabled BME only after xh_Halt(), so its claim of matching
  ; Linux/U-Boot was false.  Run/Stop remains clear; enabling BME here
  ; does not start command-ring processing.
  If xh_EnableDma() = 0
    xh_FailStop()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If xh_Halt() = 0
    xh_FailStop()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If xh_Reset() = 0
    xh_FailStop()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; CONFIG - the number of device slots enabled. xhci_lowlevel_init()
  ; programs the maximum the controller reports, preserving the
  ; reserved bits 8:31.
  xh_Phase(#XHCI_PH_CONFIG)
  maxs = xh_maxSlots & $FF
  v = xh_Rd(xh_op, #XHCI_CONFIG)
  v = maxs | (v & (~$FF))
  xh_Wr(xh_op, #XHCI_CONFIG, v)

  xh_Phase(#XHCI_PH_MEMORY)
  If xh_MemInit() = 0
    xh_FailStop()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  xh_Phase(#XHCI_PH_RUN)
  If xh_Start() = 0
    xh_FailStop()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  xh_ready = 1
  xh_Phase(#XHCI_PH_DONE)
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; XhciInit - the VL805 on a Raspberry Pi 4: bus 1, device 0, function 0.
; See WHERE THE REGISTERS ARE in the header for the citation.
Procedure.i XhciInit()
  ; IDEMPOTENT. The controller is brought up ONCE and shared - the mouse,
  ; the keyboard and the boot stick all hang off it. A second caller
  ; (storage after the cursor, or the reverse) must NOT halt and reset a
  ; controller that is already running, because that voids every slot the
  ; first caller addressed. Whoever gets here first does the work; every
  ; caller after reuses it.
  If xh_ready <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn XhciInitAt(1, 0, 0)
EndProcedure

; XhciStop - halt the controller and disable its event interrupt.
; xhci.c, xhci_lowlevel_stop(): reset, clear STS_EINT, disable the
; interrupter's pending bit. ER_IRQ_DISABLE(p) is
; ((p & 0xfffffffe) & ~0x2).
Procedure XhciStop()
  Define t.i
  xh_ready = 0
  If xh_op = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  ; A failed reset may leave CNR set. Cleanup must not issue runtime writes
  ; after the readiness gate refused them in the initialization path.
  If xh_Reset() = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  t = xh_Rd(xh_op, #XHCI_USBSTS)
  xh_Wr(xh_op, #XHCI_USBSTS, t & (~#XHCI_STS_EINT))
  If xh_ir <> 0
    t = xh_Rd(xh_ir, #XHCI_IMAN)
    xh_Wr(xh_ir, #XHCI_IMAN, (t & $FFFFFFFE) & (~$00000002))
  EndIf
  xh_ready = 0
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  PUBLIC - WHAT THE CONTROLLER SAID ABOUT ITSELF
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i XhciReady()        : ProcedureReturn xh_ready   : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciLastError()    : ProcedureReturn xh_err     : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciDeviceId()     : ProcedureReturn xh_id      : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciBar()          : ProcedureReturn xh_barBus  : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciCapBase()      : ProcedureReturn xh_cap     : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciOpBase()       : ProcedureReturn xh_op      : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciDbBase()       : ProcedureReturn xh_db      : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciRtBase()       : ProcedureReturn xh_rt      : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciCapLength()    : ProcedureReturn xh_caplen  : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciVersion()      : ProcedureReturn xh_version : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciHcsParams1()   : ProcedureReturn xh_hcs1    : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciHcsParams2()   : ProcedureReturn xh_hcs2    : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciHcsParams3()   : ProcedureReturn xh_hcs3    : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciHccParams1()   : ProcedureReturn xh_hcc1    : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciMaxSlots()     : ProcedureReturn xh_maxSlots : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciMaxIntrs()     : ProcedureReturn xh_maxIntrs : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciMaxPorts()     : ProcedureReturn xh_maxPorts : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciErstMax()      : ProcedureReturn xh_erstMax  : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciScratchpads()  : ProcedureReturn xh_scratch  : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciContextSize()  : ProcedureReturn xh_ctxSize  : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciAc64()         : ProcedureReturn xh_ac64     : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciPageSize()     : ProcedureReturn xh_pageSize : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciArenaBase()    : ProcedureReturn xh_base     : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciArenaUsed()    : ProcedureReturn xh_brk - xh_base : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciDcbaa()        : ProcedureReturn xh_dcbaa    : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciCmdRing()      : ProcedureReturn xh_ringBase[#XHCI_RING_CMD] : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciEventRing()    : ProcedureReturn xh_evBase   : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciErst()         : ProcedureReturn xh_erst     : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciLastComp()     : ProcedureReturn xh_lastComp : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciLastSlot()     : ProcedureReturn xh_lastSlot : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciLastResidual() : ProcedureReturn xh_lastResid : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciSkippedEvents(): ProcedureReturn xh_skipped  : EndProcedure
; The completion code of the last stall-recovery command. See xh_haltComp.
Procedure.i XhciLastUnhaltComp() : ProcedureReturn xh_haltComp : EndProcedure

; Live register reads, for a caller that wants to look rather than be
; told. These are the only public MMIO reads in the file and they are
; read-only on purpose.
Procedure.i XhciUsbSts()
  If xh_op = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn xh_Rd(xh_op, #XHCI_USBSTS)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i XhciUsbCmd()
  If xh_op = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn xh_Rd(xh_op, #XHCI_USBCMD)
EndProcedure

; USBSTS and USBCMD as they were AT THE LAST FAILURE, which is usually
; the pair that explains it - by the time a caller asks, the live
; registers may have moved on.
Procedure.i XhciLastSts() : ProcedureReturn xh_lastSts : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciLastCmd() : ProcedureReturn xh_lastCmd : EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  STEP 4 - PORTS
; ======================================================================
; PORTSC for port n (1-based) is at
;   op + 0x400 + (n - 1) * 0x10
; from xhci.h's struct xhci_hcor. Ports are numbered from 1 in every
; xHCI document and in every register description, and this file keeps
; that numbering rather than translating it, because a port number that
; means one thing in the code and another in the manual is a bug
; waiting for its first reader.
; ======================================================================

Procedure.i xh_PortOff(port.i)
  ProcedureReturn #XHCI_PORTBASE + ((port - 1) * #XHCI_PORT_STRIDE)
EndProcedure

; XhciPortSc - the raw PORTSC word, or 0 with an error code set.
;
; ZERO IS AMBIGUOUS AND THAT IS SAID OUT LOUD: an unpowered port with
; nothing in it reads back as exactly 0, and so does a refused call.
; Use XhciLastError() to tell them apart - #XHCI_ERR_NOT_READY or
; #XHCI_ERR_PORTNUM means the call was refused, #XHCI_ERR_NONE means
; the port really is that empty. The alternative was a negative
; sentinel, and PORTSC bit 31 is a real bit, so a negative return would
; have collided with a legitimate value.
Procedure.i XhciPortSc(port.i)
  If xh_ready = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_NOT_READY)
  EndIf
  If port < 1 Or port > xh_maxPorts
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_PORTNUM)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn xh_Rd(xh_op, xh_PortOff(port) + #XHCI_PORTSC)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i XhciPortConnected(port.i)
  Define v.i
  v = XhciPortSc(port)
  If v <= 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (v & #XHCI_PORT_CONNECT) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i XhciPortEnabled(port.i)
  Define v.i
  v = XhciPortSc(port)
  If v <= 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (v & #XHCI_PORT_PE) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; The speed field, PORTSC bits 13:10, shifted down. xhci.h's
; DEV_SPEED_MASK / XDEV_FS / XDEV_LS / XDEV_HS / XDEV_SS.
; Zero means "undefined - the port has not been reset yet", which is
; information, not an error.
Procedure.i XhciPortSpeed(port.i)
  Define v.i
  v = XhciPortSc(port)
  If v <= 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn (v & #XHCI_PORT_SPD_MASK) >> 10
EndProcedure

Procedure.i XhciSpeedText(speed.i)
  Select speed
    Case #XHCI_SPEED_FULL
      ProcedureReturn "full speed (12 Mb/s)"
    Case #XHCI_SPEED_LOW
      ProcedureReturn "low speed (1.5 Mb/s)"
    Case #XHCI_SPEED_HIGH
      ProcedureReturn "high speed (480 Mb/s)"
    Case #XHCI_SPEED_SUPER
      ProcedureReturn "super speed (5 Gb/s)"
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn "undefined - the port has not been reset"
EndProcedure

; Write a PORTSC value without disturbing anything the write would
; otherwise clear. xhci.c, xhci_port_state_to_neutral():
;   "Save Read Only (RO) bits and save read/write bits where writing a
;    0 clears the bit and writing a 1 sets the bit (RWS). For all other
;    types (RW1S, RW1CS, RW, and RZ), writing a '0' has no effect."
; So the neutral value is (state & (RO | RWS)) and anything the caller
; wants to DO is ORed on top of that.
;
; This is the single most dangerous write in the file. PORTSC's change
; bits are write-1-to-clear and its Port Enabled bit is write-1-to-
; DISABLE, so a read-modify-write that just writes the value back
; clears every change bit and disables the port. The neutral mask is
; what stops that.
Procedure xh_PortWrite(port.i, bits.i)
  Define v.i
  Define off.i
  off = xh_PortOff(port) + #XHCI_PORTSC
  v = xh_Rd(xh_op, off)
  v = (v & #XHCI_PORT_NEUTRAL) | bits
  xh_Wr(xh_op, off, v)
EndProcedure

; Clear a set of change bits - write 1 to each, on top of neutral.
Procedure xh_PortClearChange(port.i, bits.i)
  xh_PortWrite(port, bits & #XHCI_PORT_CHANGE_BITS)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XhciPortReset - power the port if the controller has port power
; switches, reset it, and wait for the reset to finish.
;
; xhci.c's root-hub emulation is the model:
;   SetPortFeature(POWER): reg = neutral | PORT_POWER; write
;   SetPortFeature(RESET): reg = neutral | PORT_RESET; write
;   ClearPortFeature(C_RESET): write (port_status | PORT_RC)
;
; and HCC_PPC(p) is ((p) & (1 << 3)) - whether the controller HAS port
; power switches at all. If it does not, PORT_POWER is hard-wired to 1
; and writing it is meaningless.
;
; A SUPERSPEED PORT MAY ALREADY BE ENABLED. USB 3 link training happens
; without software help, so a port that reports Connected AND Enabled
; before any reset is normal and this returns success without touching
; it. A USB 2 port is never enabled until reset, which is why the reset
; is conditional rather than unconditional.
;
; Returns 1 on an enabled port, 0 with an error code otherwise.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i XhciPortReset(port.i)
  Define v.i
  Define d.i

  If xh_ready = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_NOT_READY)
  EndIf
  If port < 1 Or port > xh_maxPorts
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_PORTNUM)
  EndIf

  ; Port power first, if this controller switches it.
  If (xh_hcc1 & $08) <> 0
    v = xh_Rd(xh_op, xh_PortOff(port) + #XHCI_PORTSC)
    If (v & #XHCI_PORT_POWER) = 0
      xh_PortWrite(port, #XHCI_PORT_POWER)
      xh_DelayMs(#XHCI_TMO_PPWR_MS)
      v = xh_Rd(xh_op, xh_PortOff(port) + #XHCI_PORTSC)
      If (v & #XHCI_PORT_POWER) = 0
        ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_PORT_POWER)
      EndIf
    EndIf
  EndIf

  v = xh_Rd(xh_op, xh_PortOff(port) + #XHCI_PORTSC)
  If (v & #XHCI_PORT_CONNECT) = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_NO_CONNECT)
  EndIf

  ; Clear a stale connect-status-change before doing anything, so that
  ; what is seen afterwards belongs to this reset.
  xh_PortClearChange(port, #XHCI_PORT_CSC)

  If (v & #XHCI_PORT_PE) <> 0
    ; Already enabled - a SuperSpeed port that trained itself.
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  xh_PortWrite(port, #XHCI_PORT_RESET)

  ; PORT_RC is the 1-to-0 transition of PORT_RESET. Waiting on PORT_RC
  ; rather than on PORT_RESET going clear is what xhci.h's comment on
  ; PORT_RC describes and it is the one that cannot miss a fast reset.
  d = xh_Deadline(#XHCI_TMO_PORT_MS)
  v = 0
  While xh_Expired(d) = 0
    v = xh_Rd(xh_op, xh_PortOff(port) + #XHCI_PORTSC)
    If (v & #XHCI_PORT_RC) <> 0
      Break
    EndIf
  Wend
  If (v & #XHCI_PORT_RC) = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_PORT_RESET)
  EndIf

  ; Acknowledge the reset change and the port-enable change together.
  xh_PortClearChange(port, #XHCI_PORT_RC | #XHCI_PORT_PEC)

  v = xh_Rd(xh_op, xh_PortOff(port) + #XHCI_PORTSC)
  If (v & #XHCI_PORT_PE) = 0
    ; The reset completed and the port did not come up enabled. On a
    ; USB 2 port that usually means the device failed to respond; the
    ; caller has XhciPortSc() for the raw word.
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_PORT_ENABLE)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; XhciFirstConnectedPort - the lowest-numbered port reporting a
; connection, or 0. A convenience for a bench program, not part of any
; specification.
Procedure.i XhciFirstConnectedPort()
  Define p.i
  If xh_ready = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  p = 1
  While p <= xh_maxPorts
    If (xh_Rd(xh_op, xh_PortOff(p) + #XHCI_PORTSC) & #XHCI_PORT_CONNECT) <> 0
      ProcedureReturn p
    EndIf
    p = p + 1
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  A NO-OP COMMAND - the cheapest proof the rings work
; ======================================================================
; xhci.h: TRB_CMD_NOOP is 23, "No-op Command - not for transfer rings".
; It touches no device and changes no state, so it is the safest thing
; to send first: if it comes back with a Success completion event then
; the command ring, the doorbell, the event ring, the ERST, the cycle
; bits and both cache-maintenance directions are all working. If it
; does not, none of the rest is worth attempting.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i XhciNoOp()
  If xh_ready = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_NOT_READY)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn xh_Command(#XHCI_TRB_CMD_NOOP, 0, 0, #XHCI_TMO_EVENT_MS)
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  STEP 5 - A DEVICE SLOT, AND ONE CONTROL TRANSFER
; ======================================================================
; THIS IS THE LEAST TRUSTWORTHY PART OF THIS FILE. Everything above it
; can be checked against a model that only has to behave like a
; register block. From here on the controller has to talk to a real
; device, and no part of that has been observed.
;
; The sequence, from xhci.c:
;   _xhci_alloc_device():   TRB_ENABLE_SLOT, slot id comes back in the
;                           completion event's flags word
;   xhci_address_device():  build the input context, set add_flags to
;                           SLOT_FLAG | EP0_FLAG, TRB_ADDR_DEV with the
;                           input context's address
;   xhci_ctrl_tx():         Setup / Data / Status TRBs on the endpoint
;                           0 ring, doorbell the slot
; ======================================================================

Global xh_slot.i = 0     ; the slot this file has addressed, 0 if none
Global xh_slotPort.i = 0
Global xh_slotSpeed.i = 0
; The one-tier route string of the current slot - 0 on a root port,
; otherwise the hub port number. Kept beside the speed because the two
; are always wanted together: "low speed on hub port 2" is one fact.
Global xh_slotRoute.i = 0
Global xh_maxPacket.i = 0

Procedure.i XhciSlot()      : ProcedureReturn xh_slot      : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciMaxPacket() : ProcedureReturn xh_maxPacket : EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; The default control endpoint's max packet size, by speed.
; xhci-mem.c, xhci_setup_addressable_virt_dev():
;   USB_SPEED_SUPER -> MAX_PACKET(512)
;   USB_SPEED_HIGH  -> MAX_PACKET(64)
;   USB_SPEED_FULL  -> MAX_PACKET(64)   ("USB core guesses at a 64-byte
;                                         max packet first for FS")
;   USB_SPEED_LOW   -> MAX_PACKET(8)
;
; The full-speed 64 IS A GUESS and U-Boot says so in that comment. A
; full-speed device's real bMaxPacketSize0 is in byte 7 of its device
; descriptor and can be 8, 16, 32 or 64; correcting it needs an
; Evaluate Context command (xhci_check_maxpacket()).
;
; WAS WRONG until 2026-08-28: this said the Evaluate Context command
; "this file does NOT implement". It does - XhciSetEp0MaxPacket() - and
; the receiver this stack drives has a real bMaxPacketSize0 of 8, so
; nothing longer than one packet worked until it landed. What is still
; true is that the correction is the CALLER's to make: read eight
; bytes, hand byte 7 to XhciSetEp0MaxPacket(), then read the rest.
; Until that call is made, the first eight bytes of the descriptor are
; trustworthy and a longer read may not be.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i xh_MaxPacketFor(speed.i)
  Select speed
    Case #XHCI_SPEED_SUPER
      ProcedureReturn 512
    Case #XHCI_SPEED_HIGH
      ProcedureReturn 64
    Case #XHCI_SPEED_FULL
      ProcedureReturn 64
    Case #XHCI_SPEED_LOW
      ProcedureReturn 8
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XhciEnableSlot - ask the controller for a device slot.
; Returns the slot id (1..MaxSlots) or 0.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i XhciEnableSlot()
  If xh_ready = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_NOT_READY)
  EndIf
  If xh_Command(#XHCI_TRB_ENABLE_SLOT, 0, 0, #XHCI_TMO_EVENT_MS) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ; xhci.h: TRB_TO_SLOT_ID(p) on the completion event's flags word.
  ; xh_WaitEvent() has already extracted it.
  If xh_lastSlot < 1 Or xh_lastSlot > xh_maxSlots
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_NO_SLOT)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn xh_lastSlot
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XhciAddressDeviceAt - build the input context and issue Address Device
; for a device that may be BEHIND A HUB.
;
; XhciAddressDevice(), below, is the root-port case and is now a wrapper
; over this. The generalisation was made on 2026-08-28 for one reason:
; on this board a keyboard cannot be on a root port. See THE TOPOLOGY in
; this file's header - root port 1 is permanently occupied by the
; VL805's own high-speed hub, so every low- and full-speed device on the
; machine is one tier down.
;
; THREE FIELDS DISTINGUISH A DEVICE BEHIND A HUB, and leaving any of
; them zero gives a controller that accepts the command and then cannot
; talk to the device:
;
;   route     Route String, dev_info bits 19:0. xhci.h:519,
;             ROUTE_STRING_MASK (0xfffff). Five nibbles, one per tier;
;             nibble 0 is the downstream port number of the TIER-ONE
;             hub. xhci-mem.c:785 builds it as
;                 route |= port_num << (hub->hub_depth * 4)
;             and :780-784 says a port number above 15 is CLAMPED to 15
;             because the field is four bits. This file refuses instead
;             of clamping - see #XHCI_ERR_ROUTE and the note below.
;
;   ttSlot    TT Hub Slot ID, tt_info bits 7:0. xhci.h:551, TT_SLOT(p).
;   ttPort    TT Port Number, tt_info bits 15:8. xhci.h:556, TT_PORT(p).
;             Together they name the transaction translator that carries
;             this device's low- or full-speed traffic across the
;             high-speed bus. xhci-mem.c:826-831 sets both, and ONLY for
;             a low- or full-speed device whose nearest high-speed
;             ancestor is not the root hub - which is exactly our case.
;
;   mtt       DEV_MTT, dev_info bit 25. xhci.h:524. See below; this file
;             always passes 0 and the reason is not laziness.
;
; WHY mtt IS ALWAYS ZERO HERE, and it is a real decision.
; A hi-speed hub with bDeviceProtocol 2 has a transaction translator PER
; PORT, but only once the host has selected alternate setting 1 on its
; interface. usb_hub.c:717-725 shows the whole rule: it calls
; usb_set_interface(dev, 0, 1) and sets hub->tt.multi = true ONLY IF
; THAT SUCCEEDS, and falls back to single TT otherwise. This file does
; not issue SET_INTERFACE, so the hub stays in its default single-TT
; setting and DEV_MTT must be 0. Setting the bit anyway would tell the
; controller to address a per-port translator that the hub has not
; switched on - and the failure would be intermittent, appearing only
; when two low-speed devices are busy at once. The parameter exists so
; that the day SET_INTERFACE lands, the caller has somewhere to say so.
;
; WHY A PORT ABOVE 15 IS REFUSED RATHER THAN CLAMPED. U-Boot clamps, and
; its own comment (xhci-mem.c:778-784) explains that the route string
; then stops being unique. A driver that quietly aliases port 16 onto
; port 15 sends every transfer for one device to another device, and
; nothing in the answer says so. Refusing costs a hub nobody here owns -
; no hub on this board has more than four downstream ports - and it
; cannot be got wrong silently.
;
; The input context, from xhci-mem.c:
;
;   Input Control Context, at offset 0, one context long:
;     drop_flags = 0
;     add_flags  = SLOT_FLAG | EP0_FLAG    (bits 0 and 1)
;
;   Slot Context, at offset 1*ctxsize:
;     dev_info  = route string | (speed << 20) | LAST_CTX(1)
;                 route is 0 for a device on a root port
;                 LAST_CTX(p) is (p << 27) - "Only the control endpoint
;                 is valid - one endpoint context"
;     dev_info2 = ROOT_HUB_PORT(port), which is ((port & 0xff) << 16)
;     tt_info   = 0     (no low/full-speed device behind a hub here)
;     dev_state = 0
;
;   Endpoint 0 Context, at offset 2*ctxsize:
;     ep_info  = 0
;     ep_info2 = EP_TYPE(CTRL_EP) | MAX_PACKET(mps) | MAX_BURST(0)
;                                 | ERROR_COUNT(3)
;                EP_TYPE(p) is (p << 3), CTRL_EP is 4
;                MAX_PACKET(p) is ((p & 0xffff) << 16)
;                ERROR_COUNT(p) is ((p & 0x3) << 1)
;                "EP 0 can handle burst sizes of 1, so Max Burst Size
;                 field is 0"
;     deq      = ep0 ring address | cycle_state
;     tx_info  = EP_AVG_TRB_LENGTH(8)
;                "xHCI spec 6.2.3: software shall set 'Average TRB
;                 Length' to 8 for control endpoints"
;
; and the OUTPUT device context's address goes in the DCBAA at the slot
; index - xhci_alloc_virt_device():
;     ctrl->dcbaa->dev_context_ptrs[slot_id] = virt_dev->out_ctx->dma
;
; THE CONTEXT SIZE IS 32 OR 64 AND IT COMES FROM HCCPARAMS1. Every
; offset above is a multiple of it. Hard-coding 32 is the mistake that
; produces a slot context the controller reads at the wrong address,
; and the VL805's answer is not known here - which is why xh_ctxSize is
; read rather than assumed.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i XhciAddressDeviceAt(slot.i, port.i, speed.i, route.i, ttSlot.i, ttPort.i, mtt.i)
  Define ictl.i
  Define sctx.i
  Define ectx.i
  Define mps.i
  Define deq.i
  Define info.i
  Define tt.i

  If xh_ready = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_NOT_READY)
  EndIf
  If slot < 1 Or slot > xh_maxSlots
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_BAD_SLOT)
  EndIf
  ; The controller may hand out a slot id larger than this file has
  ; rings for. Refuse it. Folding it onto an existing ring is exactly
  ; the bug this per-slot arrangement was built to end.
  If slot > #XHCI_MAX_DEVS
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_TOO_MANY_DEVS)
  EndIf
  If port < 1 Or port > xh_maxPorts
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_PORTNUM)
  EndIf
  mps = xh_MaxPacketFor(speed)
  If mps = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_SPEED)
  EndIf

  ; ---- the route string. One tier only, so one nibble.
  ; A route of 0 means "on the root port"; anything else is the
  ; downstream port number of the tier-one hub. Refuse a value that will
  ; not fit in nibble 0, because the only alternatives are clamping it
  ; (which aliases two devices onto one route - see the header above)
  ; and truncating it (which does the same thing more quietly).
  If route < 0 Or route > 15
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_ROUTE)
  EndIf

  ; ---- the transaction translator. A LOW- or FULL-speed device that is
  ; not on a root port MUST name one; without it the controller has no
  ; way to build the split transaction and every transfer times out with
  ; nothing to say why. The reverse is also refused: naming a TT for a
  ; high-speed or SuperSpeed device is a caller that has confused itself
  ; about the topology, and the controller would ignore the fields
  ; rather than complain.
  If speed = #XHCI_SPEED_LOW Or speed = #XHCI_SPEED_FULL
    If route <> 0
      If ttSlot < 1 Or ttPort < 1
        ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_TT)
      EndIf
    EndIf
  Else
    If ttSlot <> 0 Or ttPort <> 0
      ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_TT)
    EndIf
  EndIf

  ; This device's OWN endpoint-0 ring starts fresh. Ring index is the
  ; slot number, so no other slot's ring is touched.
  xh_RingReset(slot)

  ictl = xh_inCtx
  sctx = xh_inCtx + xh_ctxSize
  ectx = xh_inCtx + (xh_ctxSize * 2)

  ; Input Control Context.
  PokeN(ictl + 0, 0)                                     ; drop_flags
  PokeN(ictl + 4, #XHCI_SLOT_FLAG | #XHCI_EP0_FLAG)      ; add_flags

  ; Slot Context. dev_info is built in a named variable rather than as
  ; one long expression, because five fields ORed together on one line
  ; is not something anybody can check against xhci.h by eye.
  info = (route & #XHCI_ROUTE_MASK)
  info = info | ((speed & $0F) << 20)
  If mtt <> 0
    info = info | #XHCI_DEV_MTT
  EndIf
  info = info | (1 << 27)                                ; LAST_CTX(1)

  ; tt_info. Zero unless this is a low/full-speed device behind a hub;
  ; the checks above have already refused the inconsistent cases.
  tt = (ttSlot & $FF) | ((ttPort & $FF) << 8)

  PokeN(sctx + 0,  info)                                 ; dev_info
  PokeN(sctx + 4,  (port & $FF) << 16)                   ; dev_info2
  PokeN(sctx + 8,  tt)                                   ; tt_info
  PokeN(sctx + 12, 0)                                    ; dev_state

  ; Endpoint 0 Context.
  PokeN(ectx + 0, 0)                                     ; ep_info
  PokeN(ectx + 4, #XHCI_EP_TYPE_CTRL | #XHCI_EP_ERRCNT_3 | ((mps & $FFFF) << 16))
  deq = xh_ringBase[slot] | xh_ringCycle[slot]
  PokeN(ectx + 8,  deq & $FFFFFFFF)                      ; deq low
  PokeN(ectx + 12, (deq >> 32) & $FFFFFFFF)              ; deq high
  PokeN(ectx + 16, 8)                                    ; tx_info, avg 8

  xh_Clean(xh_inCtx, #XHCI_IN_CTX_BYTES)

  ; The output context goes in the DCBAA at this slot's index. It is
  ; written by the CONTROLLER, so it is only ever cleaned before the
  ; controller is told about it and invalidated before it is read.
  PokeX(xh_dcbaa + (slot * 8), xh_devCtx[slot])
  xh_Clean(xh_dcbaa + (slot * 8), 8)
  xh_Clean(xh_devCtx[slot], #XHCI_DEV_CTX_BYTES)

  If xh_Command(#XHCI_TRB_ADDR_DEV, xh_inCtx, slot, #XHCI_TMO_EVENT_MS) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  xh_Inval(xh_devCtx[slot], #XHCI_DEV_CTX_BYTES)
  xh_devMps[slot] = mps
  xh_devSpeed[slot] = speed
  xh_devRoute[slot] = route
  xh_outCtx    = xh_devCtx[slot]
  xh_slot      = slot
  xh_slotPort  = port
  xh_slotSpeed = speed
  xh_slotRoute = route
  xh_maxPacket = mps
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XhciSetEp0MaxPacket - correct endpoint 0's Max Packet Size after the
; device descriptor has told us the real one.
;
; WHY THIS EXISTS. A full-speed device is addressed before anybody knows
; its bMaxPacketSize0, so xh_MaxPacketFor() GUESSES 64 - the same guess
; U-Boot and Linux make. The real value is byte 7 of the device
; descriptor and may be 8, 16, 32 or 64. If it is not 64, the endpoint
; context the controller is using is WRONG, and every control transfer
; longer than one real packet returns short.
;
; rpi-6.12.y_xhci.c:1446-1451, above xhci_check_ep0_maxpacket():
;   "Full speed devices may have a max packet size greater than 8 bytes,
;    but the USB core doesn't know that until it reads the first 8 bytes
;    of the descriptor.  If the usb_device's max packet size changes
;    after that point, we need to issue an evaluate context command and
;    wait on it."
;
; MEASURED ON SILICON 2026-08-28. A wireless keyboard-and-mouse receiver
; on hub port 1 of the VL805's internal hub: the nine-byte
; configuration-descriptor read succeeded, the full-length read that
; followed did not, and HidAttach reported #HID_ERR_DESC. The whole
; stack said "no keyboard and no mouse found" with the device addressed
; and answering.
;
; WHAT AN EVALUATE CONTEXT COMMAND IS, AND HOW IT DIFFERS FROM ADDRESS
; DEVICE. It hands the controller an input context in which only the
; fields being CHANGED are marked, and changes them on a slot that is
; already addressed - the device is not re-enumerated and its address
; does not move. Only A1 (endpoint 0) is set in add_flags, drop_flags is
; zero, and the driver above copies the endpoint context rather than
; inventing one, so nothing but the packet size moves.
;
; The three fields Linux clears or sets, and why each matters here:
;   * ep_info's EP_STATE must be CLEARED - xhci.c:1497 marks it "must
;     clear". The output context's copy carries a running state the
;     controller will reject on input.
;   * ep_info2's MAX_PACKET field is replaced, not ORed.
;   * add_flags is EP0_FLAG alone; SLOT_FLAG must NOT be set, or the
;     controller evaluates slot fields nobody filled in.
;
; The dequeue pointer is written from this file's own ring bookkeeping
; rather than copied out of the output context, because the controller
; keeps its own DCS there and copying it back has bitten other drivers.
; The ring is NOT reset: the device is mid-enumeration and its ring has
; live state.
;
; Returns 1 on success, 0 with xh_err set. A device already using the
; guessed size is not an error and costs no command.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i XhciSetEp0MaxPacket(mps.i)
  Define ictl.i
  Define ectx.i
  Define deq.i

  If xh_ready = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_NOT_READY)
  EndIf
  If xh_slot < 1 Or xh_slot > xh_maxSlots
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_BAD_SLOT)
  EndIf
  If xh_devMps[xh_slot] = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_BAD_SLOT)
  EndIf

  ; The legal set, xhci.c:1468 - "case 8: case 16: case 32: case 64:
  ; case 9". Nine is a SuperSpeed bMaxPacketSize0 expressed as a power
  ; of two and never reaches this path, so it is refused here rather
  ; than silently accepted.
  If mps <> 8 And mps <> 16 And mps <> 32 And mps <> 64
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_ARG)
  EndIf

  ; Already right. xhci.c:1465 returns 0 here too.
  If mps = xh_devMps[xh_slot]
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ictl = xh_inCtx
  ectx = xh_inCtx + (xh_ctxSize * 2)

  PokeN(ictl + 0, 0)                    ; drop_flags - nothing is dropped
  PokeN(ictl + 4, #XHCI_EP0_FLAG)       ; add_flags - EP0 ONLY, no SLOT_FLAG

  PokeN(ectx + 0, 0)                    ; ep_info, EP_STATE cleared
  PokeN(ectx + 4, #XHCI_EP_TYPE_CTRL | #XHCI_EP_ERRCNT_3 | ((mps & $FFFF) << 16))
  deq = xh_ringBase[xh_slot] | xh_ringCycle[xh_slot]
  PokeN(ectx + 8,  deq & $FFFFFFFF)
  PokeN(ectx + 12, (deq >> 32) & $FFFFFFFF)
  PokeN(ectx + 16, 8)

  xh_Clean(xh_inCtx, #XHCI_IN_CTX_BYTES)

  If xh_Command(#XHCI_TRB_EVAL_CTX, xh_inCtx, xh_slot, #XHCI_TMO_EVENT_MS) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  xh_Inval(xh_devCtx[xh_slot], #XHCI_DEV_CTX_BYTES)
  xh_devMps[xh_slot] = mps
  xh_maxPacket = mps
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XhciAddressDevice - the ROOT PORT case, unchanged in behaviour.
;
; Kept as its own name rather than making every caller pass four zeroes,
; because "this device is on a root port" is a statement worth reading
; at the call site, and because pi4UsbEnum.pi4, pi4UsbConfig.pi4,
; pi4UsbDisk.pi4 and the existing gate all call it with three arguments
; and none of them should have to change to say something they already
; said.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i XhciAddressDevice(slot.i, port.i, speed.i)
  ProcedureReturn XhciAddressDeviceAt(slot, port, speed, 0, 0, 0, 0)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XhciMarkSlotAsHub - tell the controller that an addressed device is a
; HUB, how many downstream ports it has, and how long its transaction
; translator thinks for.
;
; THIS IS NOT OPTIONAL AND IT IS NOT OBVIOUS. A hub that has been
; addressed like any other device will answer its own control transfers
; perfectly - GET_DESCRIPTOR, the hub descriptor, port status, port
; reset, all of it - and the devices behind it will still never work.
; The controller has to be told, because it schedules split transactions
; on behalf of the low-speed device using the hub's slot as the
; translator, and until DEV_HUB is set the hub's slot is just a device.
; A driver that skips this reads plausible port status out of a hub and
; then times out on every transfer to the thing plugged into it.
;
; xhci.c:1351-1414, xhci_update_hub_device(), and this follows it:
;
;   ctrl_ctx->add_flags = SLOT_FLAG          (nothing else)
;   ctrl_ctx->drop_flags = 0
;   xhci_slot_copy(in, out)                  copy the slot context back
;   slot_ctx->dev_info  |= DEV_HUB           xhci.h:526, bit 26
;   slot_ctx->dev_info2 |= XHCI_MAX_PORTS(n) xhci.h:543, bits 31:24
;   slot_ctx->tt_info   |= TT_THINK_TIME(t)  xhci.h:557, bits 17:16
;   slot_ctx->dev_state  = 0
;   xhci_configure_endpoints(udev, false)    a Configure Endpoint command
;
; A CONFIGURE ENDPOINT COMMAND WITH NO ENDPOINTS looks like a mistake
; and is not. The Add Context flags select which contexts the command
; updates; with only A0 set it updates the slot context and leaves every
; endpoint alone. That is precisely what is wanted here - the hub's
; endpoint zero is already running and must not be disturbed - and it is
; what U-Boot does at :1414.
;
; THE SLOT CONTEXT IS COPIED FROM THE OUTPUT CONTEXT FIRST. The
; controller owns the output context and has written things into it that
; this file never set - the device address, the slot state. Building a
; fresh input slot context and setting only the hub fields would hand
; those back as zeros. xhci_slot_copy() exists for exactly this and the
; bulk path already does the same thing (see XhciConfigureBulk).
;
; TT THINK TIME NEEDS NO ARITHMETIC, and the round trip in the vendor
; sources hides that. usb_hub.c:736-758 turns the two-bit
; wHubCharacteristics field into nanoseconds - 666, 1332, 1998, 2664 -
; and xhci.c:1407-1410 turns it straight back with (t / 666) - 1. The
; two cancel: TTT is the raw two-bit field, bits 6:5 of
; wHubCharacteristics (usb_defs.h:323, HUB_CHAR_TTTT $0060). It is
; passed in already extracted, and XhciHubAttach() extracts it.
;
; `multi` is accepted and is expected to be 0 - see the DEV_MTT
; discussion above XhciAddressDeviceAt(). It is a parameter and not a
; hard zero so that the bit has one place to be set when SET_INTERFACE
; arrives, rather than two.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i XhciMarkSlotAsHub(slot.i, nports.i, ttt.i, multi.i)
  Define ictl.i
  Define sctx.i
  Define out.i
  Define info.i
  Define info2.i
  Define tt.i

  If xh_ready = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_NOT_READY)
  EndIf
  If slot < 1 Or slot > #XHCI_MAX_DEVS
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_BAD_SLOT)
  EndIf
  If xh_devMps[slot] = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_BAD_SLOT)
  EndIf
  ; The field is eight bits wide (xhci.h:543) and a hub with no ports is
  ; not a hub. Both ends are refused rather than masked, because a
  ; masked port count is a hub whose last ports silently do not exist.
  If nports < 1 Or nports > 255
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_HUB_DESC)
  EndIf
  If ttt < 0 Or ttt > 3
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_HUB_DESC)
  EndIf

  out  = xh_devCtx[slot]
  ictl = xh_inCtx
  sctx = xh_inCtx + xh_ctxSize

  PokeN(ictl + 0, 0)                       ; drop_flags
  PokeN(ictl + 4, #XHCI_SLOT_FLAG)         ; add_flags - the slot, only

  xh_Inval(out, #XHCI_DEV_CTX_BYTES)
  info  = PeekN(out + 0)
  info2 = PeekN(out + 4)
  tt    = PeekN(out + 8)

  info = info | #XHCI_DEV_HUB
  ; xhci.c:1389-1396 clears MTT rather than leaving whatever was there,
  ; and says why in its own comment: section 6.2.2 requires MTT to be 0
  ; for a full-speed hub, and the bit may already be set from the
  ; Address Device that created this slot.
  If multi <> 0
    info = info | #XHCI_DEV_MTT
  Else
    info = info & (~#XHCI_DEV_MTT)
  EndIf
  ; Number of Ports replaces whatever is in 31:24 rather than being ORed
  ; in. Address Device left that field zero, so an OR would work today
  ; and would silently accumulate the day this is called twice on one
  ; slot - which is what a re-enumerated hub does.
  info2 = (info2 & $00FFFFFF) | ((nports & $FF) << 24)
  tt = (tt & (~#XHCI_TT_THINK_MASK)) | ((ttt & 3) << 16)

  PokeN(sctx + 0,  info)
  PokeN(sctx + 4,  info2)
  PokeN(sctx + 8,  tt)
  PokeN(sctx + 12, 0)                      ; dev_state - xhci.c:1412

  xh_Clean(xh_inCtx, #XHCI_IN_CTX_BYTES)

  If xh_Command(#XHCI_TRB_CONFIG_EP, xh_inCtx, slot, #XHCI_TMO_EVENT_MS) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  xh_Inval(out, #XHCI_DEV_CTX_BYTES)
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; The USB device address the controller assigned, from the OUTPUT slot
; context. xhci.h: DEV_ADDR_MASK (0xff) on dev_state, which is word 3
; of the slot context. Zero until Address Device has succeeded.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XhciUseSlot - talk to a slot that was addressed earlier.
;
; Every device that has been through Address Device keeps its own ring,
; its own output context and its own endpoint-0 max packet, so switching
; between them is just a matter of saying which. Before this existed the
; only "current" device was the last one addressed, and going back to an
; earlier one meant addressing it again - which reset its ring underneath
; a controller that was still using it.
;
; Returns 1, or 0 with the error set if the slot was never addressed.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i XhciUseSlot(slot.i)
  If slot < 1 Or slot > #XHCI_MAX_DEVS
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_BAD_SLOT)
  EndIf
  ; A slot with no recorded max packet has never been addressed. Zero is
  ; not a legal endpoint-0 packet size, so it doubles as the "unused"
  ; marker without needing a separate array.
  If xh_devMps[slot] = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_BAD_SLOT)
  EndIf
  xh_slot      = slot
  xh_outCtx    = xh_devCtx[slot]
  xh_maxPacket = xh_devMps[slot]
  ; RESTORE THE SPEED AND THE ROUTE TOO, added 2026-08-28. Before this,
  ; xh_slotSpeed and xh_slotPort were left holding whatever the LAST
  ; DEVICE ADDRESSED had, so switching back to an earlier slot reported
  ; another device's speed. Nothing read them at the time, which is the
  ; only reason it was not a bug; it became one the moment a keyboard
  ; and a mouse were both attached and something wanted to know which
  ; was which. The per-slot tables are the record and this is the one
  ; place that copies out of them.
  xh_slotSpeed = xh_devSpeed[slot]
  xh_slotRoute = xh_devRoute[slot]
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; The speed and the one-tier route string of the CURRENT slot, as
; recorded when it was addressed. Exported because "which of these two
; devices am I talking to" has no other cheap answer once two are
; attached, and because a diagnostic that prints the topology should not
; have to keep a parallel table.
;
; A route of 0 means the device is on a root port; anything else is the
; downstream port number of the hub named by XhciHubSlot().
Procedure.i XhciSlotSpeed() : ProcedureReturn xh_slotSpeed : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciSlotRoute() : ProcedureReturn xh_slotRoute : EndProcedure

Procedure.i XhciDeviceAddress()
  If xh_slot = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  xh_Inval(xh_outCtx, xh_ctxSize)
  ProcedureReturn PeekN(xh_outCtx + 12) & $FF
EndProcedure

; The output slot context's Slot State. xhci.h: GET_SLOT_STATE(p) is
; ((p & (0x1f << 27)) >> 27); 0 disabled/enabled, 1 default,
; 2 addressed, 3 configured.
Procedure.i XhciSlotState()
  If xh_slot = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  xh_Inval(xh_outCtx, xh_ctxSize)
  ProcedureReturn (PeekN(xh_outCtx + 12) >> 27) & $1F
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XhciControlIn - one IN control transfer into the internal bounce
; buffer. Returns the number of bytes actually transferred, or -1.
;
; xhci-ring.c, xhci_ctrl_tx(), and this follows it closely enough that
; the differences are worth naming:
;
;   * ONE Data Stage TRB, never a chain. U-Boot's own comment says
;     "data in control transfers will never get bigger than 16MB" and
;     this file caps at #XHCI_BOUNCE_BYTES, so the 64 KiB TRB buffer
;     limit cannot be reached.
;   * NO AUTOMATIC xhci_check_maxpacket() for full-speed devices. The
;     Evaluate Context command it needs IS implemented - see
;     XhciSetEp0MaxPacket() - but this file does not call it behind the
;     caller's back, because the caller is the one holding the device
;     descriptor that says what the real bMaxPacketSize0 is.
;     WAS WRONG until 2026-08-28: this said "which this file does not
;     implement", and it had not been true since Evaluate Context
;     landed the same day.
;   * WAS WRONG until 2026-08-28: this list said "NO reset_ep() on a
;     stall... half of that is worse than none". Both halves are here
;     now, in xh_ClearHalt(), and NEITHER half is worse than none - none
;     is worst of all. A stall that is reported and not cleared leaves
;     the endpoint HALTED, and the controller then refuses to run
;     anything else on it: the next transfer, on any interface of that
;     device, is doorbelled and silently never executed, so it times out
;     rather than failing. Measured, on this board, at three consecutive
;     five-second timeouts out of a sixteen-second watchdog budget.
;
; THE FIRST TRB IS DELIBERATELY WRITTEN WITH THE WRONG CYCLE BIT and
; corrected at the end, which is xhci-ring.c's giveback_first_trb():
;
;     "Don't give the first TRB to the hardware (by toggling the cycle
;      bit) until we've finished creating all the other TRBs."
;
; Without that, the controller can start executing the Setup stage
; while the Data and Status stages are still half-written. It is a real
; race and it is invisible until it is not.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; xh_Control - the body of both directions.
;
; SPLIT OUT OF XhciControlIn 2026-08-28, when SET_REPORT arrived and
; needed an OUT transfer that carries data. The ONLY difference between
; the two directions in this file is what happens to the bounce buffer
; before the transfer: an IN transfer wants it zeroed, so that a short
; read leaves zeros rather than the previous transfer's bytes; an OUT
; transfer wants it left exactly as the caller filled it.
;
; `preloaded` says which. It is one parameter instead of a second copy
; of a hundred lines of TRB construction, and the alternative was
; considered and rejected: two near-identical control paths is how one
; of them ends up with a fix the other never gets, and the fix in
; question here would be the deferred-cycle race, which took a gate to
; find once already.
;
; The direction bit itself is NOT this parameter's business - it is bit
; 7 of bmRequestType, which the Data Stage and Status Stage TRBs below
; already read. A caller that passes an OUT request type with
; preloaded = 0 gets a transfer that sends zeros, which is exactly what
; SET_CONFIGURATION and SET_PROTOCOL want, since they carry no data at
; all.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i xh_Control(bmRequestType.i, bRequest.i, wValue.i, wIndex.i, wLength.i, preloaded.i)
  Define startTrb.i
  Define startCycle.i
  Define f3.i
  Define sf0.i
  Define sf1.i
  Define i.i
  Define ctl.i
  Define moved.i

  If xh_ready = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If xh_slot = 0
    xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_BAD_SLOT)
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If wLength < 0 Or wLength > #XHCI_BOUNCE_BYTES
    xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_ARG)
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf

  ; The bounce buffer is what the controller writes into. Zero it, then
  ; clean it, so that a short transfer leaves zeros behind rather than
  ; the previous transfer's bytes - a stale byte that happens to look
  ; plausible is exactly the kind of answer this project refuses.
  ;
  ; NOT ZEROED when the caller has already filled it: that is an OUT
  ; transfer and the bytes in there are the payload. It is still
  ; CLEANED, because the caller wrote it with the CPU and the controller
  ; is about to read it by DMA.
  If preloaded = 0
    i = 0
    While i < #XHCI_BOUNCE_BYTES
      PokeX(xh_bounce + i, 0)
      i = i + 8
    Wend
  EndIf
  xh_Clean(xh_bounce, #XHCI_BOUNCE_BYTES)

  ; Three TRBs at most - Setup, Data, Status - and they have to be
  ; contiguous. See xh_RingRoom().
  xh_RingRoom(xh_slot, 3)
  startCycle = xh_ringCycle[xh_slot]
  startTrb   = xh_TrbAddr(xh_slot, xh_ringEnq[xh_slot])

  ; ---- Setup Stage TRB. xhci-ring.c:
  ;   trb_fields[0] = requesttype | request << 8 | value << 16
  ;   trb_fields[1] = index | length << 16
  ;   trb_fields[2] = TRB_LEN(8) | TRB_INTR_TARGET(0)
  ;   field = TRB_IDT | TRB_TYPE(TRB_SETUP)
  ;   if (start_cycle == 0) field |= 0x1
  ;   plus, on xHCI >= 1.0 with data, TRB_TX_TYPE(TRB_DATA_IN/OUT)
  ;
  ; TRB_LEN(8) is the eight bytes of the setup packet and it is always
  ; 8; the packet itself rides in fields 0 and 1 because TRB_IDT says
  ; the pointer IS the data.
  f3 = #XHCI_TRB_IDT | (#XHCI_TRB_SETUP << 10)
  If xh_version >= $0100 And wLength > 0
    If (bmRequestType & $80) <> 0
      f3 = f3 | #XHCI_TX_TYPE_IN
    Else
      f3 = f3 | #XHCI_TX_TYPE_OUT
    EndIf
  EndIf
  ; THE CYCLE BIT IS WRITTEN INVERTED, IN ONE STORE. Not written
  ; correctly and then corrected: see xh_QueueTrbCy(). The gate's model
  ; polls the ring asynchronously, the way a real controller does, and
  ; it caught the write-then-correct version owning this TRB for the few
  ; instructions in between.
  ;
  ; The two data words of the setup packet are built separately because
  ; a call's arguments have to fit on one line here, and a 96-character
  ; expression inside a call is not something anybody can check.
  sf0 = (bmRequestType & $FF) | ((bRequest & $FF) << 8) | ((wValue & $FFFF) << 16)
  sf1 = (wIndex & $FFFF) | ((wLength & $FFFF) << 16)
  xh_QueueTrbCy(xh_slot, sf0, sf1, 8, f3, startCycle ! 1)

  ; ---- Data Stage TRB, only if there is data.
  ;   field = TRB_ISP | TRB_TYPE(TRB_DATA)   (ISP on IN endpoints only)
  ;   field |= TRB_DIR_IN for an IN transfer
  ;   trb_fields[2] = TRB_LEN(length) | TRB_TD_SIZE(remainder)
  ;                                   | TRB_INTR_TARGET(0)
  ; TD Size is 0 here because this is the only Data TRB in the transfer
  ; and xhci.h says "The last TRB in a TD must have the TD size set to
  ; zero".
  If wLength > 0
    f3 = (#XHCI_TRB_DATA << 10)
    If (bmRequestType & $80) <> 0
      f3 = f3 | #XHCI_TRB_ISP | #XHCI_TRB_DIR_IN
    EndIf
    sf0 = xh_bounce & $FFFFFFFF
    sf1 = (xh_bounce >> 32) & $FFFFFFFF
    xh_QueueTrb(xh_slot, sf0, sf1, wLength & $1FFFF, f3)
  EndIf

  ; ---- Status Stage TRB. xhci-ring.c:
  ;   if (length > 0 && requesttype & USB_DIR_IN) field = 0
  ;   else field = TRB_DIR_IN
  ;   trb_fields[3] = field | TRB_IOC | TRB_TYPE(TRB_STATUS) | cycle
  ; TRB_IOC is what makes the controller post the transfer event this
  ; function then waits for. Without it nothing ever completes.
  f3 = #XHCI_TRB_IOC | (#XHCI_TRB_STATUS << 10)
  If wLength > 0 And (bmRequestType & $80) <> 0
    ; status stage is OUT - no direction bit
  Else
    f3 = f3 | #XHCI_TRB_DIR_IN
  EndIf
  xh_QueueTrb(xh_slot, 0, 0, 0, f3)

  ; ---- hand the first TRB over, then ring the endpoint 0 doorbell.
  ; xhci-ring.c, giveback_first_trb().
  ctl = PeekN(startTrb + 12)
  If startCycle <> 0
    ctl = ctl | #XHCI_TRB_CYCLE
  Else
    ctl = ctl & (~#XHCI_TRB_CYCLE)
  EndIf
  PokeN(startTrb + 12, ctl & $FFFFFFFF)
  xh_Clean(startTrb, #XHCI_TRB_BYTES)

  ; xhci.h: DB_VALUE(ep_index, stream) with ep_index 0 gives 1.
  xh_Doorbell(xh_slot, 1)

  ; ANY transfer event, deliberately. A transfer event names the TRB
  ; that CARRIED IOC, and for a control transfer that is the STATUS
  ; stage - the last of the three - not the Setup TRB whose address
  ; this procedure happens to be holding. Matching against startTrb was
  ; tried and every control transfer timed out, which is exactly what a
  ; check comparing the wrong two things should do.
  ;
  ; The bulk path CAN match, because it queues exactly one TRB and that
  ; one carries IOC. See xh_Bulk.
  If xh_WaitEvent(#XHCI_TRB_TRANSFER, #XHCI_TMO_CTRL_MS) = 0
    ; A TIMEOUT ON A HALTED ENDPOINT IS NOT A TIMEOUT, and reporting it
    ; as one sends the next reader to look at the device. Nothing was
    ; ever sent: the controller does not run the ring of an endpoint it
    ; has halted, so the doorbell was accepted and ignored. Name that,
    ; and clear the halt so the caller's next attempt is not another
    ; wasted second - the pipe is recoverable and leaving it halted is
    ; what turned one refused SET_IDLE into an unusable device.
    If xh_EpStateAt(xh_slot, 1) = #XHCI_EP_STATE_HALTED
      xh_ClearHalt(xh_slot, xh_slot, 1)
      xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_EP_HALTED)
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf

  ; A Stall is a device saying no, and it is not the same failure as a
  ; transaction error. xhci.h: COMP_STALL is 6.
  ;
  ; THE HALT IS CLEARED BEFORE THE ERROR IS REPORTED, and the order
  ; matters both ways round. Clearing it first means a caller that
  ; treats a stall as survivable - HidAttach's SET_IDLE does, correctly,
  ; because HID 1.11 Appendix G makes Set_Idle optional for non-keyboard
  ; devices - gets a working pipe back. Reporting #XHCI_ERR_STALL after
  ; means the caller still learns the device said no. Before
  ; 2026-08-28 the report happened and the clear did not, and every
  ; transfer after a stall timed out on every interface of that device.
  ; See xh_ClearHalt() for the measurement.
  If xh_lastComp = #XHCI_COMP_STALL
    If xh_ClearHalt(xh_slot, xh_slot, 1) = 0
      ProcedureReturn -1      ; xh_err already names the recovery failure
    EndIf
    xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_STALL)
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  ; Short Packet is a SUCCESS with fewer bytes - xhci.h: COMP_SHORT_TX
  ; is 13 - and it is the normal answer to asking for more descriptor
  ; than exists.
  If xh_lastComp <> #XHCI_COMP_SUCCESS And xh_lastComp <> #XHCI_COMP_SHORT_TX
    ; A Babble or a Transaction Error can halt the endpoint too -
    ; rpi-6.12.y_xhci_ring.c:2336-2343 resets it for those exactly as
    ; for a stall. The state is READ rather than assumed from the
    ; completion code, because the list of codes that halt an endpoint
    ; is longer than the ones this file names and a wrong list here is
    ; a pipe that dies silently.
    If xh_EpStateAt(xh_slot, 1) = #XHCI_EP_STATE_HALTED
      xh_ClearHalt(xh_slot, xh_slot, 1)
    EndIf
    xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_XFER)
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf

  ; xhci.h: EVENT_TRB_LEN is the RESIDUAL - the bytes NOT transferred.
  moved = wLength - xh_lastResid
  If moved < 0
    moved = 0
  EndIf
  If moved > wLength
    moved = wLength
  EndIf

  ; The controller wrote the bounce buffer by DMA. Invalidate before
  ; reading it or the CPU sees the zeros it wrote itself.
  If moved > 0
    xh_Inval(xh_bounce, moved)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn moved
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XhciControlIn - one control transfer whose data, if any, comes FROM
; the device. The name and signature are unchanged from before the split
; above; every existing caller compiles untouched.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i XhciControlIn(bmRequestType.i, bRequest.i, wValue.i, wIndex.i, wLength.i)
  ProcedureReturn xh_Control(bmRequestType, bRequest, wValue, wIndex, wLength, 0)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XhciControlOut - one control transfer whose data goes TO the device.
;
; Added 2026-08-28 for SET_REPORT, which is how a host drives a
; keyboard's Caps Lock, Num Lock and Scroll Lock lamps: HID 1.11
; Appendix C says "Synchronization between LED states and CAPS LOCK,
; NUM LOCK, SCROLL LOCK, COMPOSE, and KANA events is maintained by the
; host and NOT the keyboard", and "LED states are set by sending a
; 5-bit absolute report to the keyboard via a Set_Report(Output)
; request". A keyboard whose Caps Lock lamp never lights is not a
; cosmetic problem - it is the only feedback the person typing has about
; a state the host invented.
;
; The direction bit is checked rather than assumed. Calling this with a
; device-to-host request type would queue a Data Stage TRB with
; TRB_DIR_IN clear while the device tries to send, and the transfer
; would fail in a way that names nothing.
;
; A zero length is allowed and is not the same as calling XhciControlIn
; with zero: this one leaves the bounce buffer alone. That matters only
; to a caller that is about to read it, and none is, but "did this
; procedure scribble on the shared buffer" should have an answer.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i XhciControlOut(bmRequestType.i, bRequest.i, wValue.i, wIndex.i, *src, wLength.i)
  Define i.i

  If (bmRequestType & $80) <> 0
    xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_ARG)
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If wLength < 0 Or wLength > #XHCI_BOUNCE_BYTES
    xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_ARG)
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If wLength > 0 And *src = 0
    xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_ARG)
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf

  ; PokeA/PeekA, one byte at a time. NOT a wide copy: *src is a caller's
  ; buffer at an address this file did not choose, and with the MMU off
  ; every access is Device-nGnRnE, where an unaligned wide access is a
  ; fatal Alignment fault with no vector to catch it. A byte loop cannot
  ; be misaligned. See the CACHE section in this file's header.
  i = 0
  While i < wLength
    PokeA(xh_bounce + i, PeekA(*src + i))
    i = i + 1
  Wend

  ProcedureReturn xh_Control(bmRequestType, bRequest, wValue, wIndex, wLength, 1)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XhciCopyIn - copy `n` bytes out of the bounce buffer into a caller's
; destination. PeekA, not PeekB: a descriptor byte above 127 read
; through PeekB comes back negative.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XhciBounceAddr - where the control-transfer data buffer lives.
;
; DIAGNOSTIC ACCESSOR. A caller has no business writing here, and the
; normal way to read it is XhciCopyIn. It is exported because "the
; transfer reported 44 bytes and the copy came out zero" has two
; completely different causes - the DMA did not land, or the copy read
; the wrong place - and without the address there is no way to tell
; them apart from outside this file.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i XhciBounceAddr()
  ProcedureReturn xh_bounce
EndProcedure

Procedure.i XhciCopyIn(*dst, n.i)
  Define i.i
  If *dst = 0 Or n <= 0 Or n > #XHCI_BOUNCE_BYTES
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  i = 0
  While i < n
    PokeA(*dst + i, PeekA(xh_bounce + i))
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn n
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XhciGetDescriptor - GET_DESCRIPTOR on the default control endpoint.
; USB 2.0 specification 9.4.3, and the request itself is architectural:
;   bmRequestType $80 (device to host, standard, device)
;   bRequest       6  (GET_DESCRIPTOR)
;   wValue        (type << 8) | index
;   wIndex        0, or the language id for a string descriptor
;   wLength       how many bytes are wanted
; Returns the byte count in the bounce buffer, or -1.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i XhciGetDescriptor(dtype.i, dindex.i, langid.i, wLength.i)
  ProcedureReturn XhciControlIn($80, 6, ((dtype & $FF) << 8) | (dindex & $FF), langid, wLength)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XhciGetDeviceDescriptor - fetch the 18-byte device descriptor into
; the caller's buffer. Returns the byte count, or -1.
;
; TWO TRUTHS ABOUT THE FULL-SPEED CASE, and they are not hidden:
;   * the endpoint was configured with a GUESSED 64-byte max packet
;     (see xh_MaxPacketFor), which the USB core is entitled to do only
;     for the FIRST EIGHT BYTES of the descriptor;
;   * correcting it needs an Evaluate Context command. WAS WRONG until
;     2026-08-28: this said "that this file does not implement".
;     XhciSetEp0MaxPacket() is that command, and it is the CALLER who
;     has to issue it, because the caller is the one holding the byte.
; So on a full-speed device, ask for 8 first, hand byte 7 to
; XhciSetEp0MaxPacket(), and only then read anything longer. Without
; that call a longer read is unproven and on a device whose real max
; packet is 8 - which is what the HID receiver on this bench reports -
; it comes back short every time. On high-speed and SuperSpeed devices
; the max packet is fixed by the specification and the full 18 bytes
; are fine.
;
; The length check against bLength is not pedantry. A device that
; returns fewer bytes than its own descriptor claims has been
; misconfigured or mis-clocked, and a caller that trusts the buffer
; will read whatever was there before.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i XhciGetDeviceDescriptor(*dst, maxLen.i)
  Define n.i
  Define blen.i

  If *dst = 0 Or maxLen <= 0
    xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_ARG)
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  ; USB 2.0 spec table 9-5: descriptor type 1 is DEVICE. Its defined
  ; length is 18 bytes.
  n = XhciGetDescriptor(1, 0, 0, 18)
  If n < 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If n < 2
    xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_SHORT)
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  blen = PeekA(xh_bounce + 0)
  If blen > n
    xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_SHORT)
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If n > maxLen
    n = maxLen
  EndIf
  XhciCopyIn(*dst, n)
  ProcedureReturn n
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  SET CONFIGURATION, CONFIGURE ENDPOINT, AND BULK TRANSFERS
; ======================================================================
;  Everything above gets a device addressed and talking on endpoint
;  zero. Nothing above can move bulk data, which is what mass storage
;  is made of.
;
;  THE ORDER MATTERS AND IS NOT NEGOTIABLE:
;
;    1. SET_CONFIGURATION on endpoint zero, so the DEVICE enables its
;       interface and its endpoints exist at all
;    2. Configure Endpoint, so the CONTROLLER knows those endpoints
;       exist, what type they are, and where their rings live
;
;  Doing 2 before 1 configures the controller for endpoints the device
;  has not switched on, and the first transfer times out with nothing
;  to say why.
;
;  THE DEVICE CONTEXT INDEX IS NOT THE ENDPOINT NUMBER. xHCI numbers
;  every endpoint context in one flat array with the direction folded
;  in:
;
;      DCI = (endpoint number * 2) + (1 if IN else 0)
;
;  so endpoint $01 (OUT 1) is DCI 2 and endpoint $82 (IN 2) is DCI 5.
;  DCI 0 is the slot context and DCI 1 is endpoint zero. Getting this
;  wrong writes a bulk endpoint over endpoint zero's context - breaking
;  the very channel that would report the problem.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; The DCI for a USB endpoint address. Bit 7 is direction, the low four
; bits are the number.
Procedure.i xh_Dci(epAddr.i)
  Define n.i
  n = (epAddr & $0F) * 2
  If (epAddr & $80) <> 0
    n = n + 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn n
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XhciSetConfiguration - SET_CONFIGURATION on the current slot.
;
; A control transfer with no data stage: bmRequestType 0 (host to
; device, standard, device), bRequest 9, wValue the configuration value
; out of the descriptor. XhciControlIn already handles a zero length and
; the OUT direction, so this is a wrapper and says so rather than
; duplicating the TRB building.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i XhciSetConfiguration(value.i)
  If XhciControlIn($00, 9, value & $FF, 0, 0) < 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XhciConfigureBulk - tell the controller about one bulk IN and one bulk
; OUT endpoint on the CURRENT slot.
;
; epOut/epIn are addresses from the endpoint descriptors, mpsOut/mpsIn
; their wMaxPacketSize. Returns 1, or 0 with the error set.
;
; ONE DEVICE AT A TIME, AND IT REFUSES RATHER THAN SHARING. There is a
; single pair of bulk rings; a second slot asking for them gets
; #XHCI_ERR_EP_BUSY. That restriction is deliberate and is the direct
; lesson of the shared endpoint-zero ring this file used to have: two
; device contexts pointing into one ring do not fail loudly, they fail
; as invented byte counts an hour later.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i XhciConfigureBulk(epOut.i, mpsOut.i, epIn.i, mpsIn.i)
  Define ictl.i
  Define sctx.i
  Define eo.i
  Define ei.i
  Define deq.i
  Define maxDci.i

  If xh_ready = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_NOT_READY)
  EndIf
  If xh_slot < 1
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_BAD_SLOT)
  EndIf
  If xh_mscSlot <> 0 And xh_mscSlot <> xh_slot
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_EP_BUSY)
  EndIf
  ; An OUT address must have bit 7 clear and an IN address must have it
  ; set. Swapping the two is an easy mistake to make from a descriptor
  ; walk and configures the controller backwards.
  If (epOut & $80) <> 0 Or (epIn & $80) = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_EP_ARG)
  EndIf
  If mpsOut <= 0 Or mpsIn <= 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_EP_ARG)
  EndIf

  xh_dciOut = xh_Dci(epOut)
  xh_dciIn  = xh_Dci(epIn)
  If xh_dciOut < 2 Or xh_dciIn < 2 Or xh_dciOut > 31 Or xh_dciIn > 31
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_EP_ARG)
  EndIf

  xh_RingReset(#XHCI_RING_BULK_OUT)
  xh_RingReset(#XHCI_RING_BULK_IN)

  ictl = xh_inCtx
  sctx = xh_inCtx + xh_ctxSize
  eo   = xh_inCtx + (xh_ctxSize * (xh_dciOut + 1))
  ei   = xh_inCtx + (xh_ctxSize * (xh_dciIn + 1))

  ; Input Control Context: add the slot and the two new endpoints. The
  ; slot flag is required because Context Entries is about to change.
  PokeN(ictl + 0, 0)
  PokeN(ictl + 4, #XHCI_SLOT_FLAG | (1 << xh_dciOut) | (1 << xh_dciIn))

  ; Slot context. Context Entries must name the HIGHEST DCI in use, not
  ; the number of endpoints; an undercount leaves the controller
  ; ignoring whichever endpoint has the larger index. The other slot
  ; words are copied from the OUTPUT context so nothing the controller
  ; established at Address Device time is lost.
  maxDci = xh_dciOut
  If xh_dciIn > maxDci
    maxDci = xh_dciIn
  EndIf
  xh_Inval(xh_devCtx[xh_slot], #XHCI_DEV_CTX_BYTES)
  PokeN(sctx + 0, (PeekN(xh_devCtx[xh_slot] + 0) & $07FFFFFF) | (maxDci << 27))
  PokeN(sctx + 4, PeekN(xh_devCtx[xh_slot] + 4))
  PokeN(sctx + 8, PeekN(xh_devCtx[xh_slot] + 8))
  PokeN(sctx + 12, 0)

  ; The two endpoint contexts.
  PokeN(eo + 0, 0)
  PokeN(eo + 4, #XHCI_EP_TYPE_BULK_OUT | #XHCI_EP_ERRCNT_3 | ((mpsOut & $FFFF) << 16))
  deq = xh_ringBase[#XHCI_RING_BULK_OUT] | xh_ringCycle[#XHCI_RING_BULK_OUT]
  PokeN(eo + 8,  deq & $FFFFFFFF)
  PokeN(eo + 12, (deq >> 32) & $FFFFFFFF)
  PokeN(eo + 16, mpsOut & $FFFF)

  PokeN(ei + 0, 0)
  PokeN(ei + 4, #XHCI_EP_TYPE_BULK_IN | #XHCI_EP_ERRCNT_3 | ((mpsIn & $FFFF) << 16))
  deq = xh_ringBase[#XHCI_RING_BULK_IN] | xh_ringCycle[#XHCI_RING_BULK_IN]
  PokeN(ei + 8,  deq & $FFFFFFFF)
  PokeN(ei + 12, (deq >> 32) & $FFFFFFFF)
  PokeN(ei + 16, mpsIn & $FFFF)

  xh_Clean(xh_inCtx, #XHCI_IN_CTX_BYTES)

  If xh_Command(#XHCI_TRB_CONFIG_EP, xh_inCtx, xh_slot, #XHCI_TMO_EVENT_MS) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  xh_Inval(xh_devCtx[xh_slot], #XHCI_DEV_CTX_BYTES)

  xh_mscSlot = xh_slot
  xh_epOut = epOut
  xh_epIn  = epIn
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; xh_Bulk - one Normal TRB on a bulk ring, doorbell, wait.
;
; Returns the bytes actually moved, or -1. The residual arithmetic is
; the control path's: the event reports what was NOT moved.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i xh_Bulk(ring.i, dci.i, addr.i, len.i)
  Define f3.i
  Define moved.i
  Define myTrb.i

  If xh_mscSlot = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_NO_EPS)
  EndIf
  If len < 0 Or len > $10000
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_ARG)
  EndIf
  If PcieDmaOk(addr, len) = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_DMA_RANGE)
  EndIf

  xh_RingRoom(ring, 1)
  ; REMEMBER WHERE THIS TRB LANDS, before queueing it. The transfer
  ; event names the TRB that produced it, and that is the only way to
  ; tell this transfer's completion from one left over on a shared event
  ; ring. Bulk-Only Transport does three exchanges per command and a
  ; single stale event puts all of them one reply out of step.
  myTrb = xh_TrbAddr(ring, xh_ringEnq[ring])

  ; TRB_ISP so a device that sends less than asked still completes
  ; rather than leaving the transfer outstanding; TRB_IOC so an event is
  ; posted at all. Without IOC this waits forever on a transfer that
  ; worked perfectly.
  f3 = #XHCI_TRB_IOC | #XHCI_TRB_ISP | (#XHCI_TRB_NORMAL << 10)
  xh_QueueTrb(ring, addr & $FFFFFFFF, (addr >> 32) & $FFFFFFFF, len & $1FFFF, f3)
  xh_Doorbell(xh_mscSlot, dci)

  If xh_WaitEventFor(#XHCI_TRB_TRANSFER, myTrb, #XHCI_TMO_EVENT_MS) = 0
    If xh_EpStateAt(xh_mscSlot, dci) = #XHCI_EP_STATE_HALTED
      xh_ClearHalt(xh_mscSlot, ring, dci)
      xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_EP_HALTED)
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  ; THE HOST SIDE ONLY, and here that is genuinely half the job. A stall
  ; on a BULK endpoint is a FUNCTIONAL stall: USB 2.0 section 8.5.3.4
  ; makes a control pipe unhalt itself at the next Setup, but a bulk
  ; endpoint stays halted at the DEVICE until the host sends
  ; CLEAR_FEATURE(ENDPOINT_HALT) - which is what Linux means by "class
  ; drivers clear the device side halt from a functional stall later",
  ; rpi-6.12.y_xhci_ring.c:2353-2355. That request belongs to the mass
  ; storage driver, which is where Bulk-Only Transport's reset recovery
  ; lives, and not here. Clearing the CONTROLLER's halt is still worth
  ; doing on its own: without it the endpoint is unusable even after the
  ; device has been unstalled, and the failure is a timeout that names
  ; nothing.
  If xh_lastComp = #XHCI_COMP_STALL
    If xh_ClearHalt(xh_mscSlot, ring, dci) = 0
      ProcedureReturn -1
    EndIf
    xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_STALL)
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If xh_lastComp <> #XHCI_COMP_SUCCESS And xh_lastComp <> #XHCI_COMP_SHORT_TX
    If xh_EpStateAt(xh_mscSlot, dci) = #XHCI_EP_STATE_HALTED
      xh_ClearHalt(xh_mscSlot, ring, dci)
    EndIf
    xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_XFER)
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  moved = len - xh_lastResid
  If moved < 0
    moved = 0
  EndIf
  If moved > len
    moved = len
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn moved
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XhciBulkOut / XhciBulkIn - move bytes to or from a caller's buffer.
;
; THE BUFFER IS DMA'd DIRECTLY, not bounced, so it must be reachable by
; the controller - PcieDmaOk is checked. Cleaning before an OUT and
; invalidating after an IN happen here rather than being left to the
; caller, because forgetting either gives data that is merely stale
; instead of obviously wrong.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i XhciBulkOut(*buf, len.i)
  If *buf = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_ARG)
  EndIf
  xh_Clean(*buf, len)
  ProcedureReturn xh_Bulk(#XHCI_RING_BULK_OUT, xh_dciOut, *buf, len)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i XhciBulkIn(*buf, len.i)
  Define n.i
  If *buf = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_ARG)
  EndIf
  ; Clean first even though this is a read. The lines are about to be
  ; overwritten by the device, and a dirty line written back afterwards
  ; would land on top of whatever arrived.
  xh_Clean(*buf, len)
  n = xh_Bulk(#XHCI_RING_BULK_IN, xh_dciIn, *buf, len)
  If n > 0
    xh_Inval(*buf, n)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn n
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XhciEpState - the controller's own view of an endpoint.
;
; Word 0 of an endpoint context, low three bits. xhci.h EP_STATE_MASK:
;   0 disabled   1 running   2 halted   3 stopped   4 error
;
; This is the OUTPUT context, so it is what the controller believes
; rather than what was asked for. An endpoint that Configure Endpoint
; reported success for and that reads back DISABLED is the difference
; between a command that was accepted and a command that took effect.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i XhciEpState(dci.i)
  ProcedureReturn xh_EpStateAt(xh_slot, dci)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XhciClearHalt - put the CURRENT slot's endpoint 0 back to work by hand.
;
; DIAGNOSTIC AND RESCUE. Nothing in normal operation needs this: every
; path that can halt an endpoint now clears it before returning. It is
; exported for two things - a probe that wants to demonstrate that the
; recovery works by halting an endpoint deliberately, and a caller that
; has somehow found XhciEpState(1) = #XHCI_EP_STATE_HALTED and would
; otherwise have to throw the device away.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i XhciClearHalt()
  If xh_slot < 1
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_BAD_SLOT)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn xh_ClearHalt(xh_slot, xh_slot, 1)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i XhciDciOut() : ProcedureReturn xh_dciOut : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciDciIn()  : ProcedureReturn xh_dciIn  : EndProcedure

; The slot context's Context Entries field - the highest DCI the
; controller thinks is in use. If this is 1 after configuring bulk
; endpoints, the Configure Endpoint did not take.
Procedure.i XhciCtxEntries()
  If xh_slot < 1
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  xh_Inval(xh_devCtx[xh_slot], #XHCI_DEV_CTX_BYTES)
  ProcedureReturn (PeekN(xh_devCtx[xh_slot] + 0) >> 27) & $1F
EndProcedure

Procedure.i XhciBulkReady() : ProcedureReturn xh_mscSlot : EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  USB 2.0 HUBS - the tier this board cannot avoid
; ======================================================================
;  WHY A USB HOST DRIVER ON THIS PARTICULAR BOARD MUST SPEAK HUB.
;
;  On most hosts a hub is an accessory and a driver can ship without
;  one. Not here. Root port 1 of this controller is permanently
;  occupied by the VL805's own integrated USB 2.0 hub - observed on
;  silicon 2026-08-26 as $2109:$3431, class 09, high speed, with
;  nothing plugged into anything. See THE TOPOLOGY in this file's
;  header. Every low-speed and full-speed device on this machine -
;  which is every keyboard and every mouse - sits behind it.
;
;  So the choice was not "add hubs or keep the driver simple". It was
;  "add hubs or have no keyboard". This is the smallest hub support
;  that reaches a device one tier down, and it is deliberately not more
;  than that:
;
;    * ONE TIER. The route string is built and honoured, so the
;      arithmetic for deeper trees exists, but nothing here walks a hub
;      found behind a hub. #XHCI_ERR_ROUTE says so out loud rather than
;      truncating a route that would then alias two devices.
;    * ONE HUB AT A TIME. There is one hub's worth of state below, not
;      an array, because there is one hub soldered to this board and a
;      second would need its own slot, its own route nibble and its own
;      TT - none of which is hard and all of which is untestable here.
;      XhciHubAttach() on a second hub REPLACES the first rather than
;      pretending to track both.
;    * NO INTERRUPT ENDPOINT. A real hub driver watches the hub's status
;      change endpoint to learn about hot-plug. This one polls port
;      status when asked. Nothing here is interrupt-driven; there is no
;      GIC on this machine and everything else in this tree polls.
;    * NO USB 3 HUB. bDeviceProtocol 3 is a SuperSpeed hub, whose
;      descriptor type is $2A and whose port features differ (USB 3.0
;      spec table 10-8, listed in usb_defs.h:288-293). There is no such
;      hub on this board. XhciHubAttach() refuses one rather than
;      reading a $2A descriptor as though it were a $29.
;
;  THE SPECIFICATION for everything below is USB 2.0 chapter 11, as
;  staged in RaspberryPi4/Reference/v2025.01_usb_defs.h (the constants)
;  and v2025.01_usb_hub.c (the sequence and the timings). Both were
;  fetched into the repo on 2026-08-28; before that there was nothing on
;  this machine that described a hub.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; ---- request types. usb_defs.h:50-62 and :234-235:
;   USB_TYPE_CLASS   $20   (0x01 << 5)
;   USB_RECIP_DEVICE $00   USB_RECIP_OTHER $03
;   USB_DIR_IN       $80
;   USB_RT_HUB  = USB_TYPE_CLASS | USB_RECIP_DEVICE = $20
;   USB_RT_PORT = USB_TYPE_CLASS | USB_RECIP_OTHER  = $23
; A "port" request is addressed to recipient OTHER, not to an endpoint
; and not to an interface. Getting that wrong is a hub that STALLs every
; port request and looks broken.
#XHCI_RT_HUB_IN   = $A0   ; USB_DIR_IN | USB_RT_HUB
#XHCI_RT_HUB_OUT  = $20   ; USB_RT_HUB
#XHCI_RT_PORT_IN  = $A3   ; USB_DIR_IN | USB_RT_PORT
#XHCI_RT_PORT_OUT = $23   ; USB_RT_PORT

; ---- standard requests reused by the hub class. usb_defs.h:160-165.
#XHCI_REQ_GET_STATUS     = $00
#XHCI_REQ_CLEAR_FEATURE  = $01
#XHCI_REQ_SET_FEATURE    = $03
#XHCI_REQ_GET_DESCRIPTOR = $06

; ---- descriptor types. usb_defs.h:110-125.
#XHCI_DT_DEVICE = $01
#XHCI_DT_CONFIG = $02
#XHCI_DT_HUB    = $29     ; USB_TYPE_CLASS | 0x09
#XHCI_DT_SS_HUB = $2A     ; USB_TYPE_CLASS | 0x0a - refused, see above

; ---- port features. usb_defs.h:246-259.
#XHCI_PF_CONNECTION     = 0
#XHCI_PF_ENABLE         = 1
#XHCI_PF_RESET          = 4
#XHCI_PF_POWER          = 8
#XHCI_PF_C_CONNECTION   = 16
#XHCI_PF_C_ENABLE       = 17
#XHCI_PF_C_SUSPEND      = 18
#XHCI_PF_C_OVER_CURRENT = 19
#XHCI_PF_C_RESET        = 20

; ---- wPortStatus bits. usb_defs.h:273-283.
#XHCI_PS_CONNECTION  = $0001
#XHCI_PS_ENABLE      = $0002
#XHCI_PS_SUSPEND     = $0004
#XHCI_PS_OVERCURRENT = $0008
#XHCI_PS_RESET       = $0010
#XHCI_PS_POWER       = $0100
#XHCI_PS_LOW_SPEED   = $0200
#XHCI_PS_HIGH_SPEED  = $0400

; ---- hub descriptor layout, USB 2.0 table 11-13. The non-variable part
; is seven bytes - usb_defs.h:128, USB_DT_HUB_NONVAR_SIZE 7:
;   0  bLength
;   1  bDescriptorType     $29
;   2  bNbrPorts
;   3  wHubCharacteristics low
;   4  wHubCharacteristics high
;   5  bPwrOn2PwrGood      in TWO-MILLISECOND units
;   6  bHubContrCurrent
;   7+ DeviceRemovable bitmap, then PortPwrCtrlMask
#XHCI_HUB_DESC_MIN = 7

; wHubCharacteristics, usb_defs.h:314-323. Only the think-time field is
; used here; the power-switching and over-current fields are read and
; reported by the diagnostic rather than acted on, because this file
; powers every port unconditionally either way.
#XHCI_HUB_CHAR_TTTT = $0060   ; HUB_CHAR_TTTT, bits 6:5

; bDeviceProtocol of a hub, usb_hub.c:711-731 and its USB_HUB_PR_*
; cases: 0 full-speed hub, 1 hi-speed single TT, 2 hi-speed multi TT,
; 3 SuperSpeed.
#XHCI_HUB_PR_FS        = 0
#XHCI_HUB_PR_HS_SINGLE = 1
#XHCI_HUB_PR_HS_MULTI  = 2
#XHCI_HUB_PR_SS        = 3

; How many downstream ports this file will walk. The field is eight bits
; and the specification allows 127; four is what is on this board and
; sixteen is the point past which the route string's single nibble
; cannot name the port anyway (see #XHCI_ERR_ROUTE). Refusing at 15 is
; therefore not an arbitrary limit - it is the limit implied by the
; one-tier route string above, stated where it can be seen.
#XHCI_HUB_MAX_PORTS = 15

; ---- timings, all from usb_hub.c and all labelled with their source.
; HUB_SHORT_RESET_TIME 20, HUB_LONG_RESET_TIME 200, MAX_TRIES 5 -
; usb_hub.c:47-48 and :246. The reset loop starts short and lengthens,
; which is :278 and :331.
#XHCI_HUB_RESET_SHORT_MS = 20
#XHCI_HUB_RESET_LONG_MS  = 200
#XHCI_HUB_RESET_TRIES    = 5
; usb_hub.c:213-216: wait max(100, bPwrOn2PwrGood * 2) ms after powering
; the ports, "so that the power can stablize before the devices are
; queried". The 100 is the floor.
#XHCI_HUB_PGOOD_MIN_MS   = 100
; How long to keep asking for PORT_POWER after the power-good delay has
; already elapsed, and how often. The VL805 needed 300 ms to bring all
; four ports up and bounced one of them on the way; 2000 ms is generous
; enough that a slower hub is a measurement rather than a guess, and
; short enough to sit inside a 15-second deadman.
#XHCI_HUB_PPWR_MAX_MS    = 2000
#XHCI_HUB_PPWR_STEP_MS   = 50
; USB 2.0 section 7.1.7.3 connect debounce, the same 100 ms usb_hub.c
; uses in hub_port_debounce().
#XHCI_HUB_DEBOUNCE_MS    = 100
; USB 2.0 section 7.1.7.5, quoted at usb_hub.c:400-405: a device must be
; able to accept SetAddress "after the reset recovery time 10 ms".
; SKIPPING THIS IS A DEVICE THAT ANSWERS ITS FIRST TRANSFER AND NOTHING
; AFTER, which reads as a broken device.
#XHCI_HUB_RECOVERY_MS    = 50
; TEN is the spec minimum (USB 2.0 section 7.1.7.5, TRSTRCY) and it is
; NOT what working software uses. Raspberry Pi's own kernel waits
; 10 + 40 - rpi-6.12.y_usb_core_hub.c:3156-3163, comment "TRSTRCY =
; 10 ms; plus some extra" - and adds a further 100 for a hub carrying
; the USB_QUIRK_HUB_SLOW_RESET flag. Raised from 10 to 50 on 2026-08-28
; after a wireless HID receiver behind the VL805's internal hub failed
; its first descriptor read; the spec minimum is a floor, not a target.

; ---- the one hub's worth of state.
Global xh_hubSlot.i  = 0   ; 0 = no hub attached
Global xh_hubRoot.i  = 0   ; the ROOT port the hub itself is on
Global xh_hubPorts.i = 0   ; bNbrPorts
Global xh_hubChar.i  = 0   ; wHubCharacteristics, as read
Global xh_hubTtt.i   = 0   ; the two-bit TT Think Time field
Global xh_hubPgood.i = 0   ; bPwrOn2PwrGood * 2, in milliseconds
Global xh_hubProto.i = 0   ; bDeviceProtocol, as the caller read it
Global xh_hubMulti.i = 0   ; always 0 - see XhciAddressDeviceAt's header
Global xh_hubStatus.i = 0  ; the last wPortStatus | (wPortChange << 16)

Procedure.i XhciHubSlot()   : ProcedureReturn xh_hubSlot  : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciHubPorts()  : ProcedureReturn xh_hubPorts : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciHubChar()   : ProcedureReturn xh_hubChar  : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciHubTtt()    : ProcedureReturn xh_hubTtt   : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciHubPgood()  : ProcedureReturn xh_hubPgood : EndProcedure
Procedure.i XhciHubRootPort() : ProcedureReturn xh_hubRoot : EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; xh_HubSelect - make the hub the current slot for a control transfer.
;
; Every hub request below goes to the HUB's endpoint zero, not to the
; device behind it, and XhciControlIn/Out act on whatever slot was last
; selected. A caller that has just been talking to a keyboard and then
; asks for port status would otherwise send a hub class request to the
; keyboard, which stalls it - and a stalled control endpoint is not
; recovered by this file, so the keyboard would be gone for the rest of
; the session. Selecting explicitly, every time, costs two assignments.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i xh_HubSelect()
  If xh_hubSlot = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_NOT_HUB)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn XhciUseSlot(xh_hubSlot)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XhciHubSetPortFeature / XhciHubClearPortFeature - USB 2.0 table 11-15,
; via usb_defs.h:88-99, SetPortFeature and ClearPortFeature.
;
;   bmRequestType $23 (out, class, other) or $23 for clear as well
;   bRequest      SET_FEATURE (3) or CLEAR_FEATURE (1)
;   wValue        the feature selector
;   wIndex        the port number, ONE-BASED
;   wLength       0
;
; wIndex IS ONE-BASED and U-Boot's own code passes `port + 1` everywhere
; because its loops are zero-based (usb_hub.c:176-181, :287). This file
; is one-based throughout, matching XhciPortSc() and the rest of the
; port API here, so there is no + 1 anywhere below. That is a deliberate
; difference from the reference and it is why it is written down.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i XhciHubSetPortFeature(port.i, feature.i)
  If xh_HubSelect() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If port < 1 Or port > xh_hubPorts
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_HUB_PORT)
  EndIf
  If XhciControlIn(#XHCI_RT_PORT_OUT, #XHCI_REQ_SET_FEATURE, feature, port, 0) < 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i XhciHubClearPortFeature(port.i, feature.i)
  If xh_HubSelect() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If port < 1 Or port > xh_hubPorts
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_HUB_PORT)
  EndIf
  If XhciControlIn(#XHCI_RT_PORT_OUT, #XHCI_REQ_CLEAR_FEATURE, feature, port, 0) < 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XhciHubPortStatus - GetPortStatus, USB 2.0 section 11.24.2.7.
;
; Four bytes come back: wPortStatus then wPortChange, both little-endian
; sixteen-bit. They are returned PACKED into one value, status in the
; low half and change in the high half, because the two are read
; together and always interpreted together - "connected" and "the
; connection just changed" mean nothing apart.
;
; Returns -1 on failure, which is distinguishable from every legitimate
; packed value because both halves are sixteen bits and the result is
; therefore never negative.
;
; The four bytes are assembled with PeekA and shifts, NOT with a PeekW.
; The bounce buffer is 64-byte aligned so a PeekW at offset 0 and 2
; would in fact be aligned - but the byte-wise form cannot become wrong
; if the offsets ever move, and with the MMU off an unaligned wide read
; is a fatal Alignment fault with no vector. See the CACHE section in
; this file's header.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i XhciHubPortStatus(port.i)
  Define n.i
  Define st.i
  Define ch.i

  If xh_HubSelect() = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If port < 1 Or port > xh_hubPorts
    xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_HUB_PORT)
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  n = XhciControlIn(#XHCI_RT_PORT_IN, #XHCI_REQ_GET_STATUS, 0, port, 4)
  If n < 4
    ; Fewer than four bytes is not a short descriptor, it is a hub that
    ; did not answer the question. Reporting it as a status of zero
    ; would read as "nothing connected", which is a lie with the same
    ; shape as the truth.
    If n >= 0
      xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_SHORT)
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  st = PeekA(xh_bounce + 0) | (PeekA(xh_bounce + 1) << 8)
  ch = PeekA(xh_bounce + 2) | (PeekA(xh_bounce + 3) << 8)
  xh_hubStatus = st | (ch << 16)
  ProcedureReturn xh_hubStatus
EndProcedure

Procedure.i XhciHubLastStatus() : ProcedureReturn xh_hubStatus : EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XhciHubPortConnected - is anything on this hub port?
; Returns 1, 0, or -1 if the status could not be read at all. THREE
; answers, not two: "no" and "the hub did not say" send a bench in
; different directions.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i XhciHubPortConnected(port.i)
  Define s.i
  s = XhciHubPortStatus(port)
  If s < 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If (s & #XHCI_PS_CONNECTION) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XhciHubPortSpeed - decode a hub port's status into this file's speed
; numbering, so that a caller can hand it straight to
; XhciAddressDeviceAt().
;
; usb_hub.c:385-399: LOW_SPEED and HIGH_SPEED are two BITS, not a field,
; and FULL SPEED IS NEITHER OF THEM SET. That default case is the one to
; get wrong - a driver that tests for a "full speed" bit finds none and
; reports undefined for the commonest kind of device there is.
;
; USB_PORT_STAT_SUPER_SPEED ($0600) is in usb_defs.h:281 with the
; comment "faking support to XHCI", and it is not a real USB 2.0 hub
; status bit combination - a USB 2.0 hub cannot have a SuperSpeed device
; on it. It is not decoded here for that reason: pretending to is how a
; SuperSpeed max packet size gets handed to a full-speed device.
;
; Returns a #XHCI_SPEED_* value, or 0 (#XHCI_SPEED_UNDEF) if the status
; could not be read.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i XhciHubPortSpeed(port.i)
  Define s.i
  s = XhciHubPortStatus(port)
  If s < 0
    ProcedureReturn #XHCI_SPEED_UNDEF
  EndIf
  If (s & #XHCI_PS_CONNECTION) = 0
    ProcedureReturn #XHCI_SPEED_UNDEF
  EndIf
  If (s & #XHCI_PS_LOW_SPEED) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn #XHCI_SPEED_LOW
  EndIf
  If (s & #XHCI_PS_HIGH_SPEED) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn #XHCI_SPEED_HIGH
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn #XHCI_SPEED_FULL
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XhciHubPortReset - reset one downstream port and leave it enabled.
;
; usb_hub.c:272-345, usb_hub_port_reset(), and this follows its shape
; including the retry:
;
;   for tries in 0..4:
;       SetPortFeature(PORT_RESET)
;       wait  (20 ms the first time, 200 ms after that)
;       GetPortStatus
;       if PORT_ENABLE: break
;   ClearPortFeature(C_RESET)
;
; WHY THE RETRY IS COPIED RATHER THAN SIMPLIFIED. The obvious reading is
; that one reset and one wait should do, and U-Boot's own comment at
; :310-325 explains why it does not: a real device was observed to
; "briefly drop off the bus when this second bus reset is executed, yet
; if we retry this loop, it'll eventually come back after another reset
; or two". That is a measured behaviour of real hardware, recorded by
; somebody who had the hardware. It is not the kind of thing to
; second-guess from a desk.
;
; THE C_RESET CHANGE BIT MUST BE CLEARED. It is not decoration: an
; uncleared change bit leaves the hub reporting a change forever, and a
; caller polling for hot-plug then sees every port as constantly
; changing.
;
; Returns 1 with the port enabled, or 0.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i XhciHubPortReset(port.i)
  Define tries.i
  Define wait.i
  Define s.i
  Define ok.i

  If xh_HubSelect() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If port < 1 Or port > xh_hubPorts
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_HUB_PORT)
  EndIf

  wait = #XHCI_HUB_RESET_SHORT_MS
  tries = 0
  ok = 0
  While tries < #XHCI_HUB_RESET_TRIES
    If XhciHubSetPortFeature(port, #XHCI_PF_RESET) = 0
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    xh_DelayMs(wait)
    s = XhciHubPortStatus(port)
    If s < 0
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    If (s & #XHCI_PS_ENABLE) <> 0
      ok = 1
      Break
    EndIf
    wait = #XHCI_HUB_RESET_LONG_MS
    tries = tries + 1
  Wend

  ; The change bit is cleared whether or not the reset took. A failed
  ; reset that leaves C_RESET set is a port that lies about changing for
  ; the rest of the session, and the failure is already being reported
  ; by the return value.
  XhciHubClearPortFeature(port, #XHCI_PF_C_RESET)

  If ok = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_PORT_ENABLE)
  EndIf

  ; USB 2.0 section 7.1.7.5, usb_hub.c:400-405. Ten milliseconds of
  ; reset recovery before anybody may address this device.
  xh_DelayMs(#XHCI_HUB_RECOVERY_MS)
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XhciHubAttach - take a device that has been addressed and configured,
; establish that it is a hub, tell the CONTROLLER it is a hub, and power
; its downstream ports.
;
; THE ORDER HERE IS NOT NEGOTIABLE and each step fails invisibly if the
; one before it is skipped:
;
;   1. the caller has already done Enable Slot, Address Device and
;      SET_CONFIGURATION on this device. A hub that has not been
;      configured answers GET_DESCRIPTOR and stalls every port request,
;      because port operations belong to the configured state (USB 2.0
;      section 9.4). That failure looks like a hub that does not support
;      the request.
;   2. the HUB DESCRIPTOR, twice. Four bytes first to learn bLength,
;      then bLength bytes - usb_hub.c:640-655 does exactly this and it
;      is not paranoia: the descriptor's tail is a variable-length
;      bitmap whose size depends on the port count that is inside the
;      descriptor.
;   3. XhciMarkSlotAsHub. Without it the controller will not schedule
;      split transactions and every device behind this hub times out.
;   4. SetPortFeature(PORT_POWER) on every port, then wait. An unpowered
;      port reports nothing connected no matter what is plugged into it.
;
; `protocol` is bDeviceProtocol out of the device descriptor, which the
; CALLER has already fetched - this procedure does not re-fetch it,
; because the caller had to read bDeviceClass to decide this was a hub
; at all and re-reading is one more transfer that can fail differently.
;
; Returns 1, or 0 with the error set.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i XhciHubAttach(slot.i, rootPort.i, protocol.i)
  Define n.i
  Define blen.i
  Define i.i
  Define wait.i

  If xh_ready = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_NOT_READY)
  EndIf
  If slot < 1 Or slot > #XHCI_MAX_DEVS
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_BAD_SLOT)
  EndIf
  If xh_devMps[slot] = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_BAD_SLOT)
  EndIf
  ; A SuperSpeed hub's descriptor is type $2A with a different layout
  ; and different port features. There is none on this board and
  ; reading a $2A as a $29 would produce a plausible port count out of
  ; the wrong byte. Refuse, by name.
  If protocol = #XHCI_HUB_PR_SS
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_NOT_HUB)
  EndIf

  ; Everything below talks to the hub. Claim the slot BEFORE the first
  ; transfer, and record it, so xh_HubSelect() works from here on.
  xh_hubSlot = slot
  xh_hubRoot = rootPort
  xh_hubProto = protocol
  xh_hubPorts = 0
  If XhciUseSlot(slot) = 0
    xh_hubSlot = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; ---- 2a. four bytes, to learn bLength.
  n = XhciControlIn(#XHCI_RT_HUB_IN, #XHCI_REQ_GET_DESCRIPTOR, #XHCI_DT_HUB << 8, 0, 4)
  If n < 4
    xh_hubSlot = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_HUB_DESC)
  EndIf
  blen = PeekA(xh_bounce + 0)
  If blen < #XHCI_HUB_DESC_MIN Or blen > #XHCI_BOUNCE_BYTES
    xh_hubSlot = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_HUB_DESC)
  EndIf
  If PeekA(xh_bounce + 1) <> #XHCI_DT_HUB
    ; The device answered a hub-class GET_DESCRIPTOR with something that
    ; is not a hub descriptor. Believing it would build the port count
    ; out of whatever byte 2 happens to be.
    xh_hubSlot = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_NOT_HUB)
  EndIf

  ; ---- 2b. the whole thing.
  n = XhciControlIn(#XHCI_RT_HUB_IN, #XHCI_REQ_GET_DESCRIPTOR, #XHCI_DT_HUB << 8, 0, blen)
  If n < blen
    xh_hubSlot = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_HUB_DESC)
  EndIf

  xh_hubPorts = PeekA(xh_bounce + 2)
  xh_hubChar  = PeekA(xh_bounce + 3) | (PeekA(xh_bounce + 4) << 8)
  ; bPwrOn2PwrGood is in TWO-MILLISECOND units - usb_hub.c:169,
  ; "pgood_delay = hub->desc.bPwrOn2PwrGood * 2". Storing the
  ; already-doubled value here means the unit conversion happens once,
  ; at the place that knows the unit.
  xh_hubPgood = PeekA(xh_bounce + 5) * 2
  xh_hubTtt   = (xh_hubChar & #XHCI_HUB_CHAR_TTTT) >> 5
  ; Always single-TT until somebody issues SET_INTERFACE(0, 1). See the
  ; DEV_MTT discussion above XhciAddressDeviceAt().
  xh_hubMulti = 0

  If xh_hubPorts < 1 Or xh_hubPorts > #XHCI_HUB_MAX_PORTS
    xh_hubSlot = 0
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_HUB_DESC)
  EndIf

  ; ---- 3. tell the controller.
  If XhciMarkSlotAsHub(slot, xh_hubPorts, xh_hubTtt, xh_hubMulti) = 0
    xh_hubSlot = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ; XhciMarkSlotAsHub issued a command, not a transfer, so the current
  ; slot is unchanged - but it is re-selected here anyway rather than
  ; relying on that, because "which slot is current" is exactly the kind
  ; of invariant that a later edit breaks silently.
  If XhciUseSlot(slot) = 0
    xh_hubSlot = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; ---- 4. power every port, then wait for it to be worth asking.
  i = 1
  While i <= xh_hubPorts
    If XhciHubSetPortFeature(i, #XHCI_PF_POWER) = 0
      xh_hubSlot = 0
      ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_PORT_POWER)
    EndIf
    i = i + 1
  Wend

  ; ---- 5. the power-good delay the descriptor asked for.
  ;
  ; This is NOT sufficient on its own - see XhciHubSettle() below, which
  ; the caller must run before it believes a port status. Measured on
  ; silicon 2026-08-28: at this point every downstream port of the
  ; VL805 still reads $0000, PORT_POWER included.
  wait = xh_hubPgood
  If wait < #XHCI_HUB_PGOOD_MIN_MS
    wait = #XHCI_HUB_PGOOD_MIN_MS
  EndIf
  xh_DelayMs(wait)

  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XhciHubSettle - wait until the downstream ports mean what they say.
;
; MEASURED ON SILICON 2026-08-28 with pi4HubWatch.pi4, on the VL805's
; internal hub ($2109:$3431). Immediately after XhciHubAttach returns,
; ALL FOUR ports read $0000 - not "no device", but PORT_POWER ITSELF
; CLEAR, on ports we had just powered. Sampling every 100 ms after
; that:
;
;   t=100ms  port 2  $0000 -> $00010103   connection+enable+power
;   t=100ms  port 4  $0000 -> $00000100   power only
;   t=200ms  port 3  $0000 -> $00000100
;   t=200ms  port 4  $0100 -> $00000000   <- power BOUNCED back off
;   t=300ms  port 4  $0000 -> $00000100
;
; So bPwrOn2PwrGood is a lower bound this hub does not honour, and port
; 4 shows the line is not even monotonic while it settles. The visible
; symptom was a USB mouse sitting on port 2 reported as "no keyboard
; and no mouse found": a wrong answer, silently, with no error raised
; anywhere, because AN UNPOWERED PORT AND AN EMPTY PORT ARE BOTH $0000.
;
; WHY THIS IS NOT INSIDE XhciHubAttach, where it was first written:
; there, the GET_STATUS transfers do not succeed at all, so the loop
; either spins out its whole budget or - worse - mistakes the failure
; for an answer. Run from the caller, after attach has returned, the
; same reads work. The placement is the fix, not the loop.
;
; Two distinct waits, and they are not interchangeable:
;
;   POWER-GOOD - poll until every port reports PORT_POWER, rather than
;   assuming any fixed delay was enough. Polling is what makes this
;   right on a hub slower than this one instead of only on this one.
;
;   CONNECT DEBOUNCE - USB 2.0 section 7.1.7.3 wants 100 ms of STABLE
;   connection before the status is trusted, which is precisely what
;   port 4's bounce above demonstrates. usb_hub.c does the same in
;   hub_port_debounce().
;
; Returns 1 if every port reported power, 0 if the budget ran out.
; RUNNING OUT IS NOT AN ERROR and must not abort enumeration: a hub
; with one dead port should still bring up its live ones, and the
; debounce is applied either way.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i XhciHubSettle()
  Define waited.i
  Define allOn.i
  Define st.i
  Define i.i

  If xh_hubSlot = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  waited = 0
  allOn = 0
  While waited < #XHCI_HUB_PPWR_MAX_MS And allOn = 0
    allOn = 1
    i = 1
    While i <= xh_hubPorts
      ; XhciHubPortStatus RETURNS -1 ON FAILURE, and -1 has every bit
      ; set - so `status & #XHCI_PS_POWER` is TRUE for a read that
      ; never happened. The first version of this loop did exactly
      ; that, decided all four ports were powered after 0 ms, and
      ; reproduced the original bug precisely. The negative test comes
      ; FIRST, and a failed read counts as not-ready: a hub that will
      ; not answer has not told us its ports are up.
      st = XhciHubPortStatus(i)
      If st < 0 Or (st & #XHCI_PS_POWER) = 0
        allOn = 0
      EndIf
      i = i + 1
    Wend
    If allOn = 0
      xh_DelayMs(#XHCI_HUB_PPWR_STEP_MS)
      waited = waited + #XHCI_HUB_PPWR_STEP_MS
    EndIf
  Wend

  ; Unconditional. Port 2 above was already reporting a connection at
  ; the first sample and STILL wants the settle, because the bits read
  ; next are the speed bits, and those only mean anything once the line
  ; has stopped moving.
  xh_DelayMs(#XHCI_HUB_DEBOUNCE_MS)
  ProcedureReturn allOn
EndProcedure

; XhciAddressBehindHub - the whole "there is something on hub port N,
; give me a slot for it" sequence, in one call.
;
; It exists because the four steps have to happen in this order with
; these arguments, and getting the route string and the TT fields from
; two different places at the call site is how they end up disagreeing:
;
;   1. reset the hub port - which is also what makes its speed readable
;   2. read the speed FROM THE PORT, after the reset and not before
;   3. Enable Slot
;   4. Address Device with route = the hub port, TT = this hub's slot
;      and this hub's port
;
; STEP 2 IS THE ONE WITH A TRAP IN IT. A hub port's speed bits are not
; meaningful until the port has been reset - the same rule that
; XhciPortReset() documents for root ports, and the reason the earlier
; root-port probe printed "CONNECTED, undefined" and was right to.
;
; Returns the new slot id, or 0.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i XhciAddressBehindHub(hubPort.i)
  Define speed.i
  Define slot.i

  If xh_HubSelect() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If hubPort < 1 Or hubPort > xh_hubPorts
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_HUB_PORT)
  EndIf
  If XhciHubPortConnected(hubPort) <> 1
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_NO_CONNECT)
  EndIf
  If XhciHubPortReset(hubPort) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  speed = XhciHubPortSpeed(hubPort)
  If speed = #XHCI_SPEED_UNDEF
    ProcedureReturn xh_Fail(#XHCI_ERR_SPEED)
  EndIf

  slot = XhciEnableSlot()
  If slot = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ; The TT is named for LOW and FULL speed devices only. A high-speed
  ; device behind a high-speed hub talks high-speed end to end and there
  ; is no split transaction to translate; naming a TT for it is refused
  ; by XhciAddressDeviceAt, which is why the zeroes are explicit here
  ; rather than passed through a variable that "usually" holds the hub.
  If speed = #XHCI_SPEED_HIGH
    If XhciAddressDeviceAt(slot, xh_hubRoot, speed, hubPort, 0, 0, 0) = 0
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
  Else
    If XhciAddressDeviceAt(slot, xh_hubRoot, speed, hubPort, xh_hubSlot, hubPort, xh_hubMulti) = 0
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn slot
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  ERROR TEXT. One string per code. The text names the STAGE, not the
;  fix - the fix needs XhciLastSts() as well, and a driver that guessed
;  at causes in a string would be wrong out loud.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i XhciErrorText()
  Select xh_err
    Case #XHCI_ERR_NONE
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: ok"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_NO_CNTFRQ
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: CNTFRQ_EL0 is not a usable frequency - cannot bound any wait, nothing attempted"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_NO_DEVICE
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: nothing answering a config read at that bus/device/function - link down, wrong b/d/f, or VL805 firmware never loaded"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_BAR_IO
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: BAR0 is an I/O BAR - this is not an xHCI memory-mapped controller"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_BAR_HIGH
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: BAR0 is a 64-bit BAR above 4 GB - outside the root complex's outbound window"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_BAR_ZERO
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: BAR0 reads back zero - no address was ever assigned to the controller"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_BAR_RANGE
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: PcieCpuFromBus refused the BAR - it is outside the mapped outbound window"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_CAPLENGTH
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: CAPLENGTH is not a plausible capability-block length - wrong address, or the controller is not answering"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_HCIVERSION
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: HCIVERSION is not an xHCI version number - wrong device at this address"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_HCSPARAMS
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: HCSPARAMS1 reports zero ports or zero device slots"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_DMA_RANGE
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: the ring arena is above the 3 GB PCIe DMA limit - the controller could not reach it"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_DMA_ENABLE
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: PCI bus mastering enable did not read back; controller was not started"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_TAKEOVER
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: firmware register routing or halted DMA handover was not verified"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_ARENA
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: the ring arena ran out - XHCI_ARENA_BYTES is too small for this controller"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_HALT
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: HCHalted never set - the controller did not stop"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_RESET
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: HCRST never self-cleared - the controller did not reset"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_CNR
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: Controller Not Ready never cleared after reset - no operational register may be written yet"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_PAGESIZE
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: the controller's PAGESIZE is not 4 KB - this driver has no scratchpad pages of that size"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_SCRATCH
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: the controller wants more scratchpad buffers than this driver provides"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_RUN
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: HCHalted never cleared after setting Run/Stop - the controller did not start"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_NOT_READY
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: XhciInit has not succeeded - nothing else may be called"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_PORTNUM
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: port number outside 1..MaxPorts"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_NO_CONNECT
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: nothing is connected to that port"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_PORT_POWER
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: port power never came up"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_PORT_RESET
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: the port never reported its reset complete"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_PORT_ENABLE
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: the port reset finished but the port is not enabled - the device did not answer"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_EVT_TIMEOUT
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: no event of the expected type arrived - see XhciSkippedEvents for what did"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_CMD_FAILED
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: the command completed with a failing completion code - see XhciLastComp"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_NO_SLOT
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: Enable Slot succeeded but returned no usable slot id"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_BAD_SLOT
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: slot id outside 1..MaxSlots, or no device has been addressed"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_XFER
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: the transfer completed with a failing completion code - see XhciLastComp"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_STALL
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: the device stalled the control endpoint - this driver does not reset a stalled endpoint"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_ARG
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: a caller argument is refused - null pointer, negative length, or length above the bounce buffer"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_SPEED
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: the port reports a speed this driver has no default max packet size for"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_SHORT
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: the device returned fewer bytes than its own descriptor claims"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_TOO_MANY_DEVS
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: the controller handed out a slot id past XHCI_MAX_DEVS - refused rather than folded onto another slot's ring"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_EP_BUSY
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: the bulk ring pair already belongs to a different slot - a second mass-storage device needs its own pair"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_EP_ARG
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: the endpoint addresses are not one bulk OUT and one bulk IN, or a max packet size is zero"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_NO_EPS
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: a bulk transfer was attempted before Configure Endpoint"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_NOT_HUB
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: that device is not a hub - its device descriptor does not report class 09"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_HUB_PORT
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: hub port number outside 1..bNbrPorts"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_HUB_DESC
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: the hub descriptor is too short, or names more downstream ports than this driver will walk"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_ROUTE
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: that route string cannot be built - a hub port above 15, or a hub behind a hub, which this driver does not walk"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_TT
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: a low- or full-speed device behind something that is not a high-speed hub - there is no transaction translator to name"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_EP_HALTED
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: the endpoint is HALTED - the controller never ran the transfer, so nothing was sent and nothing timed out on the wire"
    Case #XHCI_ERR_UNHALT
      ProcedureReturn "xhci: Reset Endpoint and Set TR Dequeue both ran and the endpoint is still halted - the pipe is gone, re-address the device"
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn "xhci: unknown error code"
EndProcedure
